      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Mr. Swinton's Salary.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present—Messrs. L. Woodward (presiding), C. Roylance, J. Bolshavv, H. Eyes; P. Mounfleld, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. Stokes, J. Whit low, A. Broome, T. Baxter, H. M. Davies, C. E. Linaker, J. H. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, G. Pilling, W. Harrison, S Horton. J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, C. Horton, J. Fryer, J. Rutter, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, R. Billington, C. E. Linaker,-jnn., W. Turner, A. G. Guest, T. Antwis, G.F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), W. Diggle and J. Swinton (surveyors) and Dr. Adams (medical officer).</p>
        <p>INCREASE IN THE RATES. The Clerk presented his estimates for the coming half year. The probable balance in hand at the end of the current half year was £BB4, and the probable expendi- ture for the ensuing six months was :—Highways, £2,963; police and contingency, £4,355; and general public health £1,392, which made a sum of £5,575 to be provided from rates. It was recommended that a call be maJe for that sum which would necessitate an increase of id. in the £ on the present rates. The probable expenditure in the various parishes was Acton Grange £140; Alvauley, £63 ; Antrobus, £97 ; Appleton, £242; Aston, £173; Aston Grange, £18; Partington, £22; Budworth, £77 ; Clifton, £133; Crowley, £5O ; Dares- bury, £46; Dalton, £297; Frodsham, £495 ; Frodsham Lordship, £321 ; Grappenhall, £307 ; Halton £lBl ; Halton,£ 6; Helsby, £375; Keckwick, £120; Kingsley ,£208; Kingswood, £101; Latchford Without, £113; Manley, £72; Moore, £173; Newton-by-Daresbury, £46; Newton-by-Frodsham, £34; Norley, £125; Norton, £222, Preston-o'-th'-Hill, £134; Sevenoaks, £518; Stratton, £74 ; Sutton, £246; Thelwall, £185; Walton Inferior, ; Walton Superior, £4; Stockkam, £24; Stockton Heath, £518; Weston, £513; Whitley Inferior, £59; Whitley Superior, £6B. The estimates were approved. MR. SWINTON'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>.£205</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling moved that the salary of Mr. Swinton be incaeased for the next six months to £2.00. He explained that Mr. Swinton had acted as surveyor for some street making in Stockton Heath,and had carried out the work In a workmanlike way.—(Mr. Whitley : " Question.") The streets were the best paved streets in the district. —(Mr. Hallows "Hear, hear.)—Mr. Swinton was promised the usual percentage of the work, which had to be paid by the property owners, and which amounted to £3O. The Local Government. Board, however, objec- ted to Council Surveyors having a private practice, and the only way in which Mr. Swinton could get the money was by increasing his salary for six months. Mr. Hallows seconded.</p>
        <p>Opposition to the proposal was made on the ground that the work had been done in the District Council's time for which Mr. Swinton was paid a regular salary. It was, however, felt that the sum having been promised the Council were in honour bound to pay it. Mr. Pilling pointed out that most of the work was done in Mr. Swinton's own time. The motion was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN YOUNG MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.—The mem- bers of the above class were on Tuesday afternoon addressed by the Rev. J. Francis Matthews, of Latch- ford. The reverent gentleman took for his subject the following queries, viz :—" Whom am I? What am I doing here ? and where shall I go on leaving here ?" The treatment of the last question, with reference to the future life, was very deep and extremely interesting in the discussion which followed the address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>FASHION IN DAHLIAS.</head>
        <p>THE FASHION IN DAHLIAS.</p>
        <p>The dahlia show held at Earl's Court is one of the finest of recent years. A novelty is a new variety, the " Pilot." It is a perfect cactus type, of a beautiful saffron colour, and is exhibited by Messrs. Hobbies, o t Dereham, Norfolk. The fashion in dalias this year is for those known as " pompoms," a rather small bloom, but perfect in shape and colour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CHSTIPATION</head>
        <p>CHSTIPATION</p>
        <p>MRS. SARAH RAVEN. of</p>
        <p>. . • •• , ' ' , . 87, Bourne Road, Colchester, says : "For fifteen years I suffered from constipation and piles. Often have I paced the room the -whole night Through. Fora whole month at a time I had no peace night and day, and had • to crawl about or kneel down, doubled up, in order to ohtain relief. I had no appetite, and my food did me no good. ~. "I went twice to the Colchester Ilos- pital as an out-door patient. From ,7 one doctor I received oils,which proved , • useless. The • caused me such intense suffering that I have many a tune sunk down through exhaustion. My Jinsbniid purchased medicines of ail descriptions, but I s,ti 11 continued to suffer. " I heard of Bile Beans, and im- mediately bought a box. A short time "after commencing with. the Beans, I felt relief. My pains were not so acute or so enduring. I bought a further supply of Beans, and was comp!etely rid of the constipat,ion and the piles by the time I hail taken four t. boxes. I aril now quite well and -; strong, can eat with relish, am putting flesh on very quickly, and can do my .• work with energy and pleasure.' ft. it. • . • . 1:?g</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The German Crown Prince and Tennis.—The Crown Prince could not have a better tutor in the game of tennis than Miss Toupie Lowther, who is just as skilful with the racquet as she is with the rapier. Whilst staying at Homburg she has been giving lessons in this popular outdoor game, to the Crown Prince and his brother, Prince Eitel Fritz, and Prince Adalbert. The German Emperor used to be a great tennis player, and his preference for the outdoor amusements of Oxford. to the beer drinking and duellings of Bonn, was not at one time regarded with favour by the average Berliner. As a nation we are probably less liked now than we were then by the German people' a state of things rather emphasised by the fact that our King never once met the Emperor during his Majesty's recent visit to their country, but the Crown Prince is likely to suffer in popular favour, for any attachment to our national games. He is tall, slim, and very youthful in appear- ance, but is very cordially regarded by the German people, as also is his wife the Crown Princess, who since her marriage, has quite won the hearts of the populace. Ac Belvorr Castle.—Belvoir Castle, the seat of the Duke of Rutland, which was visited by the King last week, was a fortress in feudal times, but it was rebuilt in the early part of the last century, after the old Castle had been almost entirely destroyed by fire in 1816. Some of the old feudal customs still survive,and among others that of a night watchman who patrols the Castle battlements and calls the hour and the state of the weather. Lady Victoria Manners presides as hostess for her father, the Duke, who is in his eighty-eighth year, and has long been a widower. She is her father's right hand, managing most of his affairs, and is to the Duke of Rutland what Lady Gwendolen Cecil was to her widowed father, the late Lord Salisbury. Lord and Lady Burton.—The King's visit to Glen Quoich this week, is the second his Majesty has paid to Lord and Lady Burton at their beautiful home in Scotland, since the death of Queen Victoria, who was also familiar with Loch Qnoich, and its neighbourhood. Lord and Lady Burton do not belong to Scottish families, but they are very fond of the Highlands and they have also entertained the King quite recently at their seat in Staffordshire. Lady Burton was a Miss Thornewill of that county and married in 1869. She has always been a great favourite in Society from her kindly nature, her bright spirits, and vivacious manners, all of which qualities are fully shared by her daughter the Hon. Mrs. James Baillie of Dochfour. Lord Burton is now in his sixty-eight year, and both he and Lady Burton are devoted grand-parents to the children of Mrs. Baillie who is an only child heiress to the Burton peerage. . .</p>
        <p>The' attractions of the Looking Glass.—lt is said thata woman, if she lives to a goodly old age will , spend on the whole, about one year of her life in front of the looking glass. From the age of six to ten she will, according to a statistician, spend about seven ; minutes a.day looking at herself, for the next five years it will be quarter of an hour,and from fifteen to twenty half an hour, whilst after twenty it is calculated she will devote a good hoar a day to the mirror. How long this goes on before she begins to fear the looking glass, depends of course upon circumstances, and per- haps not a little on the mirror,for there are mirrors and mirrors, and none of them do us full justice. Like the camera they emphasise lines and wrinkles, and as no woman is quite natural when she looks at herself in a glass, she does not see the changing expressions or the chart.in,, light in the eyes, which are perhaps her greatesecharm. There is consequently this comfort for those who have lost their youthful interest in the mirror, which may be trusted, not at any time of our life, to over-flatter us. . . . .</p>
        <p>Misfortunes not difficult to put up with.—The misfor- tune of being both rich and beautiful, is one that most ; of us could put up with, though certainly those who are the most favoured in both respects are not as a rule the happiest people in the world. The beautiful girl is nearly always over-ambitious,andwhilst she is the:centre of envy and jealousness, and often of spiteful remarks among her own circle of friends, the may fail to attain to the ideals in life upon which she had set her mind. To be unambitious is a misfortune, but too much zeal in this direction almost necessarily involves a sacrifice of all contentment in life. To be really happy is no doubt more difficult for the beautiful girl than for her less favored sister, and we have it upon the authority of Lady St. Helier, that there is no class of people so utterly miserable, as the idle rich. All the philosophy upon such matters may however be reduced to the simple question of ability to make a wise use of gifts and opportunities, and though the world does not accept the philosophers view, except perhaps in theory, there is some comfort in the reflection that it is not the rich or the beautiful only, who have the best time in life. The Feminine Walking Stick.—lf anything could testify to the popularity of the Crown Princess of Germany it is her setting the fashion of carrying a cane, which hitherto has been regarded as shockingly unwomanly in that country. Among all the Western nations, the Germans have been one of the most back- ward in following the advanced ideas and aims of the modern woman, but, when once the lead was given, they apparently found no difficulty in emulating their American sisters in the cult of the feminine walking stick. With us it has long been the'habit to carry a stick in the country, where.it is very; useful, but so far the custom of carrying a cane for purely ornamental pur- poses, in towns and cities, has not obtained a very firm hold over our affection. In the days of long ago, the fashionable dame used to carry a gold-headed tasselled cane—almost as long as a shepherd's crook,when on her walks abroad, but it has had no counterpart in modern times, and it would be just as well perhaps to confine , the feminine walking. stick to the country, where it is of real use and service to us, out of doors. Cottage Lace Making.—Various reasons are given to account for the decadence of the Devonshire lace indus- try, but there is no doubt that the coming of the Board Schools had much to do with it. Though it can be followed in old age, the art of lace-making must be acquired in childhood, and there are fewer opportunities for this than was formerly the case when education made no demand upon the time of the children of the rural population. Annther contributing cause is the fact that less attention has been given in Devonshire to the work of organising the industry upon a more business-like basis, than has been done in Ireland and in Buckinghamshire. The revival of interest in Irish lace is7due in no small measure to the organised efforts that have been made, and brought it prominently before the public,it is the same with the industry in Buckinghamshi re Here, lace-making and its homely traditions still flourish in many of the villages, and it would be a pity if this— one of the most useful of these cottage industries7s ere allowed to die out. One great advantage it has, is, that it can he followed by the old women when they are past all other kinds of wo-k. Autumn Shirts and Blouses.—Authorities on the subject of coming modes predicted a return of the huge sleeve, and the disappearance of thepouch. As a matter I of fact all the new autumn blouses shew sleeves of very moderate dimensions, moreover, the generality of women appear to have discovered that the blouse or• bodice drawn down tightly into the waistbelt in front is by no means so becoming as the slightly pouched effect. So dainty and inexpensive are autumn shirts and blouses that the home worker will have little inducement to build her own. The great drawback so far has been the fit, which so frequently leaves much to be desired. Viyella shirts and blouses may, however,be trusted never to give trouble in this way. They are of irreproachable cut, well-made; they wear and clean splendidly, while the patterns and colourings this season are very beautiful and artistic in the extreme. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>RETIRED COLONEL'S LEAP.</head>
        <p>RETIRED COLONEL'S LEAP.</p>
        <p>At Aberystwyth on Tuesday night a man on the end of the pier rapidly divested himself of his clothes, and exclaiming, "Em going to drown myself," leaped into the sea. No lifebelt was handy, and so the stage ropes of the pavilion were flung to the drowning man, who was found to be a retired volunteer colonel named C.E. Bembridge, from London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 23, 1905. WOMAN'S REALM.. !SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS. IntESS—FASHION—HOME.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 23, 1905. WOMAN'S REALM.. !SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS. IntESS—FASHION—HOME.</p>
        <p>SHORT-SDNITEDNESS OF INSECTS.</p>
        <p>It is contended by many entomologists that insects generally are shortsighted. And .an ob- servation on the wasp seems to confirm this. A number of wasps were hawking at the flies 'settled on a notice-board fastened against the wall of a building. They not only made frequent dashes at the flies settled on the board, but also at spots of black paint, nail heads, etc. These latter were evidently mistaken for flies. • And Professor Plateau, of Ghent, believes that while the eyes of insects are ' short-sighted, and per- haps colour-blind, they are very keen to detect motion. A moving object impresses them vividly, while a stationary one is scarcely perceived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DRESS AT DREEY LANE.</head>
        <p>DRESS AT DREEY LANE.</p>
        <p>There are some beautiful dresses in "The 2rodigal Son." In the first act we see Thora wearing a simple black dress, made with a short, tight-fitting coat like an Eton jacket, decorated with, cuffs of black velvet, and worn over a white bodice covered with bands of gold. This plain dress is changed in the third scene, where Thora is a bride, for a dainty white silk embroidered in silver. The bodice and sleeves fit tightly, which gives a very quaint effect, and there is some handsome silver embroidery down the front. Thora also wears a curious peaked cap of white satin, from which a long spangled net veil falls to the ground. In Act 11. Thora wears a tea-gown of cream lace bordered with ermine, and opening over an under-dress of soft wbite satin covered with net. The elbow sleeves are very wide and pointed, and the bodice is cut in a "V" and edged with a deep frill of lace. In the same act, Thora's sister Helga wears a pale 'leliotrope silk quaintly trimmed with lace of a deeper shade of the same colour. In a subse- quent scene she throws over her shoulders a cape of purple velvet arranged with long stole ends, and she also wears a picture hat which is wreathed with roses. In the third act Helga wears a cloak made of white satin shaded with pink and so to a deep red. From foot to waist it is covered with a mass of large and small chiffon roses arranged in sprays and bunches in every possible variety of pink and red. The sleeves are .to the elbow, pointed in shape and covered with roses, and at the neck is a yoke of shaded scarlet silk, the whole being completed by a wide waistband of brilliant red. On her head Helga wears a fillet repeating this prevailing colour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHARMIWG Gows.</head>
        <p>A CHARMIWG Gows.</p>
        <p>Fictured in our, illustration is a charming goon of thick brown woollen voile. This voile, which slightly resembles a woollen crepe. will .be much used during the autumn for indoor and afternoon frocks. It drapes admirably, and has the additional advantage of being far from ex- pensive. The gown at present under our notice is extremely simple, though very smart and be- coming. The bodice is slightly full, and has a suspicion of pouch at the waist. Round the shoulders it is finished with a turn-down collar of brown chiffon velvet, edged with a strapping</p>
        <p>of white cloth and kilting al brown silk. Above this collar, and in the opening in front of the bodice, appears a dainty vest of Irish guipure. The belt and the turn-back cuffs on the short sleeves are of the brown velvet, while frills of lace soften the sleeves at the elbow. Round the hem of the plain, full skirt runs a band of the chiffon velvet, giving it a weight and firmness eat cause it to hang in very graceful folds.</p>
        <p>- • . .:k-../.....e. ' t // . . .. ~ /9- "•41,`:'''' )1 " %,/, ,'; \ 1 \ to II ,1, 4., • , • \ fl '4 ' ''-1;711?., It - 4.• ' ',:,;,./;,-';..- .-: .-.• , , ..(A,,,,, ;:,,,,, -‘ . • `,,re, t k• - 'A, 1;•. - ' ";0,- , \.' / \ \ //ill • ii, ( . • it i A., .. 1.,• ,„ ~., ~ '\.\ \''' ' / \\ \\\ • , 5,,...:::::i 1 tc‘\\ \a‘ \ 4rl • . 4.-s''' ' ' ‘ ''' .16`-...,...±—.7.4 .... I lid' ".. II .-...; ~..S.- ir</p>
