      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>UNIFORLI "STYLES OF -DRESS..</head>
        <p>UNIFORLI "STYLES OF -DRESS..</p>
        <p>The wearing of a uniform dress by women for certain occupations is, to our mind, a most sensible idea, writes "X and Z" in the Globe. Nothing has shewn this more thoroughly than the adoption of a uniform by nurses. Recently we were staying at a perfectly ideal small hotel in the North of Ireland, on the shores of Lough Foyle. It is a private house being successfully run as a hotel by two ladies. They wear simple dresses of pale grey-blue Irish linen, with folds of white silk muslin round the necks and down the fronts. Snow-white aprons and sleeves drawn up on their linen sleeves to above the elbows, and they look so neat and smart, and like their business, that one is at once inspired with confidence in their ability, an inspiration founded upon fact. It would be such a mercy if girls who work for their living adopted a uni- form style of dress suitable for their occupation, and wore it always on working days. We should be saved sad sights, if they did, of cheaply imitated smart toilets in varying degrees of shabbiness and draggle. If girl clerks wore grey tweed in winter and alpaca in summer, tele.- graphists brown, and all simple and neat, how nice they would look, and how much the dignity of their labour increased. There is no reason why nurses should be the only women workers who always look neat and nice. UP-TO-DATE COAT.</p>
        <p>Long coats and wraps of every description are extremely popular this autumn. Quite the most popular and the smartest, however, are those which have a suggestion of the Empire style in their fashioning. When chosen of a suitable material and colour, these coats are a very</p>
        <p>economical investment, for they can be worn right through to the summer, and are wearable over any kind of dress. The coat worn by the lady in our sketch is of fine seal-brown cloth. The upper part of the coat is laid in four wide tucks, which are held in place by a wide Empire belt, stitched and adorned with buttons. Below this belt the tucks are released, and the fulness falls in soft folds to the hem. Deep brown velvet edged with cloth forms the small roll-back collar and the large gauntlet cuffs. The sleeves are full, and are gathered two or three times before being put into place. The accompanying hat is of brown straw, trimmed with velvet and one long, shaded feather.</p>
