      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>SCALLOPS OF .VEAL A LA NAPOLITAINV.—BoiI 4oz. of macaroni in salted water for about two hours, strain it, cut it into inch-length pieces, and heat it in a gill of tomato sauce. Cut the scallops from an underdone fillet of veal and coat one side of them with the following sauce: Re- duce a gill of puree of .onions to a paste, mix it with teaspoonful of liquid glaze and , the yolks of thro earls. and rub it throueb a wire sieve. When the puree Is set on the sclllops, cup them in breadcrumbs, then in beaten egg and bread- crumbs, and fry them in a bath of hot lard. Arrange them round the macaroni, and pour little skluted tomato sauce round both,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>UNUSED SUN-HEAT.</head>
        <p>UNUSED SUN-HEAT.</p>
        <p>An American scientific journal draws atten- tion to the enormous amount of unused sun-heat sent to the earth from the sun. A little ides of the unused sun-heat is given on the estimate of Professor Langley, who states that there is enough heat to warm 2,900,000,000 worlds like ours. "Every minute," this authority calculates, "there is enough of the sun's heat falling to the earth to raise to boiling 37,000,000,000 tons of water. But the heat which thus falls on the earth is not a thousandth part of 1 per, cent, of what the sun sends elsewhere, and all the coal beds of Pennsylvania, for instance, though they can supply the country for hundreds of years, would not keep up this heat during the one- thousandth part of a second."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>MIGRATION OF THE SWALLOW</head>
        <p>THE MIGRATION OF THE SWALLOW</p>
        <p>Records of the dates of arrival and departure of migratory birds have of late yearr been ob- tained through the services of a large Pumber of observers widely scattered over the country. From these it has been found that it takes the swallow 105 days to complete its summe.: coloni- sation of Europe. Tbat is to say, not until 105 days _ after da _first arrival at Gibralta- in the south does the pioneer swallow reach Lulea in the north. By the time the old birds have reached the latter locality the first brood is fully fledged in the former. In Hungary there is a difference of seventy days between the first arrival of the swallow in the south and its appear- ance in the north. This may be due to the fact that those birds which go furthest south to winter go furthest north to breed, and so have longer distances to travel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PLANT THAT MIGRATES.</head>
        <p>A PLANT THAT MIGRATES.</p>
        <p>Viz interesting example of a plant which mi- grates to warmer quarters for the winter is afforded by the frog-bit. Its pretty white flowers and rounded leaves, like those of a water-lily in miniature, are familiar on the surfaces of ponds and ditches in summer. In winter, however, it disappears from view, having migrated to the bottom of the pond, where the water is warmer. Fob according to the well-known peculiarity of water in cooling, the bottom of the pond remains 70deg. above freezing. The migration of the frog-bit is effected by certain shoots detaching themselves from the plant, becoming heavier than the water, and so sinking to the bottom. There the plant is secure from the winter's cold. When the warm weather returns, the hiberna- ting shoots become lighter, and rise to the sur- face, over which they rapidly spread their leaves and flowers.</p>
        <p>MEN IN THE RECESSES OF THE MOON.</p>
        <p>Professor Max Haushofer, of the University of Berlin, is inclined to 'believe that the moon is still inhabited by creatures capable of feeling and thinking, creatures from whom the semblance of man has not vanished. He arrives at this conclu- sion notwithstanding all that can be said against it from a scientific standpoint. Pro- fessor Flaushofer states that it is siot un- thinkable that the dead torso of the moon retained in its inner recesses a certain quantity of moisture, or water, and of air, which clung to its caverns, pits, and canyons. These sources and retreats of life are hidden from the mortal eye of the astronomer, although they may have nourished a little world of their ovin from time immemorial. These conceptions, singular as they may appear, are somewhat supported, says Science Siftings, by the discoveries and conclu- sions of Professor Pickering, and, indeed, of other astronomers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>IMITATING LIFE PHENOMENA.</head>
        <p>IMITATING LIFE PHENOMENA.</p>
        <p>Experiments made by Biitschli, Rhumblns and others have shewn that drops of emulsion under certain conditions may' imitate the phe- nomena of life as exhibited by Amceba. They move, that is to say, towards certain agents, and away from others, while they .show " choice " in taking in certain substances, and rejecting others. In the _American Naturalist, Mr. H. S. Jennings reviews these experiments and con- tends that they do not ahem through what phy- sical factors the activity a the Amceba, or other organism, is brought about. Such experimental imitations, in fact, do not stand a critical com- parison with what actually takes place in the liv- ing animal. In them there are factors at work essentially different from those acting in the organism. Local changes in the surface tension in the drops of emulsion were supposed to ex- plain most of the activities of the Amceba. Mr. Jennings. however, contends that the surface tension theory completely breaks down.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WASPS AS FLOWER FERTILISERS.</head>
        <p>WASPS AS FLOWER FERTILISERS.</p>
        <p>The wasp is a more frequent visitor to flowers than is generally supposed. Darwin noted foul species as visited by it for the nectar, viz., helle. borine, figwort, snowberry, and tritoma. There are, however, several other flowers to which it not infrequently resorts. During July and Au- gust it may often be seen on the white flowers of the cow parsnip, working as carefully and sys- tematically as a bee. The hive bee may also frequently be seen on the same flower. In late autumn the wasp does not despise the honey of the ivy flower. Other flowers on which it may be seen are fuchsia, evening primrose, goose- berry, and fennel. Lord Avebury remarks that the figwort is one of the few flowers specially visited by wasps. "the honey being not pleasing to bees." Yet bees do at times visit it, and in America it has a great reputation as a honey plant. The helleborine, according to Darwin, depends exclusively on wasps for fertilisation. "If wasps were to become extinct in any dis- trict," he wrote, "so would the epipactus folio."</p>
        <p>ARTIFICIAL DIAMOND MAKING. Moissan's well-known method of forming arti- ficial diamonds involved the employment of a very high tethperature in the electrical furnace, as well as a very high pressure. Mr. C. V. Bur- ton argues that if carbon be made to crystallise at a comparatively low temperature, the pressure required will also be less. Thus it is estimated that with a temperature of from 550 deg. to 700deg.C., ordinary atmospheric pressure would suffice. On these data Mr. Burton has instituted some experiments in the artificial production of diamonds. It is found that a molten alloy of lead with 1 per cent. of calcium will hold in solu- tion a small quantity of carbon. If this calcium is afterwards eliminated from the alloy some of the carbon crystallises out. This may be done by subjecting it to the action of steam, by which a crust of lime is formed on the outside, the lead remaining unattacked. Now, if this elimination is effected at full red heat the carbon is found to crystallise as graphite. If, however, a very low red heat is employed, a number of microscopic crystals are formed which have many of the pro- perties of the diamond. Other methods by which minute diamond crystals appear to have been ob- tained are by boiling benzine with finely- powdered potassium dichromate, or heating it with chloroform to a temperature of 200 deg. or 300deg.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PROPERTIES OF IRON ALLOYS.</head>
        <p>PROPERTIES OF IRON ALLOYS.</p>
        <p>In a paper communicated to the Royal Society of Dublin. Messrs. Barrett, Brown, and Hadfield describe some of the physical effects produced on iron by admixture with the various elements generally found in its alloys. The thermal and i,than manganese, for the diminution being larger quantities of the electrical conductivities of iron are decreased by carbon d proportionately greater for small additions of these elements same ingredients. Tungsten has the least, and aluminium the greatest, effect in reducing the can thermal and eleetri4l conductivities of iron; nickel also decreases tbe conductivities, but to a much smaller extent than carbon or manganese employed in similar quantities. The minimum conductivity (both thermal and electrical) was obtained with an allaY containing 6936 per cent. of iron, 25 per cent, of nickel, and 504 per cent. of manganese. Steel alloys containing copper and small quantities of carbon and matt. ganese have lower conductivity co-efficients than pure iron. The thermal and electrical conduc- tivities are increased by annealing. As com- pared with copper, the electrical conductivities of iron and its alloys are generally lower than -their thermal conductivities. A small percentage of silicon or aluminium alloyed with iron increases the magnetic permeability beyond that of pure commercial iron, whilst a sufficient amount of manganese (itself ma l'iletic) renders iron prac- tically non-magnetic. The microstructure of the alloys favours the suggestion that the sincbn or aluminium by comoining wirn oxygen may free the iron from oxide giving it a closer texture, and thus raising the magnetic perme- ability.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Special Services.</p>
        <p>At the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday, special sermons were preached by the Rev. T. Gallaod Hartley, of Liverpool, chairman of the district. The rev, gentleman briefly alluded to the loss the church had sustained in the death of Mr. Henshall, and asked the congregation for their prayers and sympathies for the widow and family. The pulpit and the front of the organ were draped in black. The anthem in the morning was "Sweet is Thy mercy," and in the evening "How lovely are the messengers." Mrs. Booth was soloist in the evening. The organist was Mr. G. W. Weaver. The offertories were in aid of the society funds.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street P.S.A. Society. Sunday afternoon's meeting was the occasion of the half-yearly distribution of prizes to the members of the above society. Mr. E. Osborne presided, and gave an encouraging address on the P.S.A. movement and the distribution of good literature by these societies. At the conclusion of his address, Mr. Osborne presented eight prizes to the successful competitors of an essay competition (the donors of the prizes being Mr. A. H. Crosfleld and Mr. E. Osborne). The subject was :—" Are P.S.A. Societies a benefit to the people." The Chair- man said that several good papers had been sent in and he awarded the prizes to the following :—(Gentlemen), 1, Mr. H. Atty ; 2. Mr. J. Moss; 3, Mr. J. Lee; 4, Mr. B. Dinaelow ; 0, Mr. W. Shaw: (Ladies), 1, Mrs. A. Simpson; 2, Mrs. H. Atty ; 3, Miss L. Fairhurst. Mr. John Taylor, L.D.S., in the course of a few remarks, said it was the first time he had been amongst the members of Buckley-street, and he was pleased to see such a large gathering. He hoped to be with them again. He tlien presented about 180 books to the mem- bers. During the evening Miss W. Cox sang " The Gift," and Master A. Cox gave 'The Holy City " with great taste. Miss M. Plinston. A.L.C.M., presided at the organ. Mr. W. Shaw and Mr. H. Woolley also took part in the service. Ten new members were welcomed at the close of the meeting making a total membership of 238.</p>
        <p>---4-- THEFT OF BEEF.</p>
        <p>At Warrington on-ga-turday Margaret Griffiths, 89 Paclgate-lane, was charged with stealing from a trap near the King and Queen Hotel, Padgate-lane, on Friday night, 251 h of beef, of the value 155., the pro- perty of Mr. James Newton, 15, Orford-lane.—The Chief Constable said Mr. Newton was a well-known farmer and contractor, and on Friday night ho entered the Kingand Queen Hotel, leaving the trap, which con- tained a quantity of beef in a basket, outside, On coming out a few minutes later, on hearing the pony moving, he found that the basket and beef were missing but the pony was still there. He It once gave informa- tion to the police. In company with Detective Clough complainant went to the prisoners house and found the beef there. When charged with the theft the prisoner said she did not steal the beef, but found it on the footpath.—The Chairman said it was a most cheeky theft, and the prisoner would be fined 40s.</p>
        <p>ELASTIC STOCKINGS. KNEE CAPS, &amp;C.—For Varicos Veins and all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the Leg- Sprains, They are porous,. light in texture, and in- expensive, and are drawn on like an ordinary stocking. —Price 4s. Gd., 7s. Gd. and 10s. Gd. each. Postage free. WHITE &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical. Instrument and Truss Makers, 96, Renshaw Street, Liverpool.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
