      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>THE...</head>
        <p>THE...</p>
        <p>WHITE ROSE) By GERALD BISS. , _ CHAPTER Xll.—Continuedi I Atherly rose from his seat in his lazy way. " I think," he said, "that the Earl of Bea Nevis would be an excellent person to invite. He is a good soldier of no small experience and might be valuable in many ways. He comes of Jacobite stock. His ancestors were out both in the 5 and the '45, and the family estates were sequestered.</p>
        <p>He sat down stroking his long moustache. "An admirable selection," said the Duchess -of Hampstead from her seat near the duke. "I agree with you thoroughly," assented her husband enthusiastically. "No one could be better. Has anyone any objection to an invitation being issued to Lord Ben Nevis ? " Approval was signified all round the table. "Enter it in the minutes," said the duke to Gordon, "that the Earl of Ben Nevis be invited to join the White Rose Committee. I will instruct Mr. Frazer to approach him on the subject. I think he should prove a very wily recruit." Ellis Gordon made the required note, and then the duke rose from his chair. " Ladies and gentlemen," he began, "it seems to me that the time is now ripe for us to sound Prince Rudolph of Bavarienburg, as the rightful heir to the throne, upon, his wishes in the matter. Things have now gone so far that we require him in our counsels before we can draw up a definite plan of campaign or decide upon the best course of action. I would, therefore, suggest that a letter be sent to his Majesty, explaining the present state of affairs and inviting him to come over to take part in our deliberations as a preliminary step. Without him we cannot proceed further at present." "Hear, hear," ran round the table, as the duke resumed his seat. Lord Struan immediately rose. "I am quite at one with our chairman in the matter," he skid.-" In fact, I feel that, perhaps, we have already gone too far without consulting his Majesty's wiShes in the matter. I would, therefore, propose • that the Duke af Belsize, assisted by Mr. lan Frazer, draft a letter to his Majesty immediately, laying our plans and our hopes before him and inviting him to visit us. Without him we can •lo nothing further, and we have already a substantial nucleus to show him. Indeed, we have an organisation which, aided by some coup (Petal, would not take long to develop into an enormous political force. But we must seek his counsel and hear his views. The sooner that a letter is dispatched to him the better it seems to me." The Marquess of Swanage spoke next, formally seconding the proposed invitation: and member after member of the committee followed, all agreeing as to the advisability and necessity of approaching Prince Rudolph of Bavarienburg. At length the duke rose again. "This suggestion, which seems to meet with general approval, I must admit, emanates from our active and energetic secretary, who first put it before me. Mr. Frazer, of whom, I am sure, we all hold the highest possible opinion, even went ;further and offered to follow the letter at once in person, so as to arrive in time to interview his Majesty and explain everything fully to him, before he could reply formally to it. Mr. Frazer, for whom I speak in his unavoidable absence, suggests that the matter is so momentous that it can hardly be committed fully to paper for many reasons, and that there are many points and details which admit only of a personal explanation. Any questions which his Majesty might ask could then be answered promptly and fully by word of mouth. This idea of a personal visit from the secretary oi the Head Centre appeals to me very strongly." Again signs of complete assent ran ,round the table. "I take it, then, ladies and gentlemen," went On the enthusiastic Chairman, "that you are all with me in the matter. I will, therefore, put it formally to the vote that the committee authorises Mr. Frazer under my supervision to draft a letter of invitation to his Majesty, commonly miscalled Prince Rudolph of Bavarienburg, and to follow it in person for the fuller enlightenment of the King in matters of detail." The proposal was unanimously agreed to. "Then," began the duke, as Ellis Gordon made a note of it in the minutes, "then-" He was interrupted by three loud knocks on •the door, followed by a low penetrating whistle. The air was the opening bars of the old Jacobite song. • "Frazer ! " exclaimed two or three of the conspirators. "And Oddingly I " And a sudden silence fell on the wholf room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CHAPTER XIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII.</p>
        <p>THE duke handed Gordon the key of the door.</p>
        <p>"Who goes there ? " asked the barrister, drawing back the portiere. " Bridge " came back the answer in Frazer's cold, metallic voice.</p>
        <p>Gordon unlocked the door and threw it open. "This way, Lord Oddingly," said Frazer, bowing on the threshold: and as they entered the door was again locked behind them and the curtain drawn.</p>
        <p>Lord Oddingly seemed dazed by the sud- den bright light and surprised by the scene. "I —l—," he began, looking round an- xiously and suspiciously. "I don't under- stand. What does this mean ? "</p>
        <p>"Welcome, Lord Oddingly," said the Duke of Belsize, rising and coming forward.</p>
        <p>The whole committee rose from their seats.</p>
        <p>"I fail to understand this play-acting," said Lord Oddingly stiffly, as he recovered from his first surprise. "It seems to me that I have been lured up here under false per- tences."</p>
        <p>"My dear sir," interposed the duke; deprecatingly. "Pray allow me to explain." ' The sooner the better," replied the Secretary for War shortly " Mr. Frazer," began the chairman; " doubtless explained to you that the object in inviting you here to-night was to ask you to. join a_committe for a. particular •purpose..l</p>
        <p>':Mr. Frazer was anything but `exilicit„l, interjected the new arrival. "We, who are gathered here," went on the+ duke, heedless- of the interruption, "except the Earl of Portcullis, who is in a similar position to yourself, form the head centre of the White Rose League, the• chief Jacobite organisation in this country." " I must say that I still fail to understand,' said Lord Oddingly with increasing coldness. "From this head centre we administer the outer circles of a widespread plot to reinstate th eHouse of Stuart on the throne from which it was so wrongly ousted," explained the duke with a touch of pomposity. "You mean to say that you are engaged in a plot against the King and the present dynasty? " asked the other in' a puzzled voice, conscious of five and twenty pairs of eager, glittering eyes focussed upon him. "We are here to right a great wrong. We are here to restore their lawful heritage to tlie Stuarts. The question has, with the exception of two abortive attempts, been allowed to lie dormant for considerably over two centuries, but it is now felt that the time has come for action." "Are all these ladies and gentlemen in har- mony on the subject ? " asked the Secretary for War, with a colloctive wave of his hand.</p>
        <p>, The company round the oak table variously 'signified their assent. _ - -</p>
        <p>"But it may be the cause of bloodshed," he objd. - -</p>
        <p>"As little as possible, I assure you, answered the chairman. "Our wish is, as far as we are able, to bring about a peaceful 'change of dynasty." "That would impossible, even were it advisable or necessary," said Lord Oddingly curtly : " awl the very idea seems to me as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>opened at its approach. Both the occupants were silent and pre- occupied. At length the Cabinet Minister Spoke. " I should not wonder," he said, "il we were in great danger—l mean in danger of assassination. I cannot help putting two and two together, and it seems to me that to-night's gathering it the solution of tht White Rose murders. Poole—Duval— Morning,ton—and next it will be ourselves i: we are not careful. They dare not "risk at exposure, and they may be sure that Wf should speak." " I suppose so," muttered Portcullis. " I will go straight back to Graivenoi Square," went on Lord Oddingly, laying hi_ plans, "and to-morrow morning, firs thing, I will communicate with the Home Secretary and Verschoyle by telephone. We car hold a consultation at my house, and I need not leave it till my safety is assured. May I offer you a bed? It will lessen your danger, and I shall want your corroboration." "Thank you,' , said the other between his set teeth. They were opposite a belt of trees, which made the road temporarily darker. Port- cullis drew up the car with a sudden jerk and a grating noise. "Something gone wrong," he said, "I . must hurry up and see to it. I hope it's nothing serious." " How unfortunate I " exclaimed his companion, excitedly. "Delay may prove serious to us." They got out and Portcullis raised the bonnet and examined the engine. - " It's nothing much," he said reassuringly, " It's only something that wants tightening. up. Do you mind holding the bonnet up, while I get a spanner out of the tool box ? ' Lord Oddingly did as he was requested while Portcullis made his way round to the back of the car and opened the door of the tonneau. Then he took something out of his breast pocket which glinted in the star- light. " It's the back cylinder," he called out, " Do you see where it is ? " Lord Oddingly went over and peered into the engine. With a quick movement the other made his way quietly round the car, as his victim leant forward. The knife gashed through the air and buried itself in his back near the left shoulder blade. . A dull plud and a groan : and all was over. The Secretary for War fell forward, and the bonnet crashed down upon him like a gull- : lotine, in the middle of the back. - Cool in the hour of need and danger from force of habit and long experience after bit, game. Portcullis quickly raised the bonnet and drew the dead man out by the collar of his coat With a twist he withdrew the knife, and, making a pad out of the dead peer's own scarf, he arranged it to staunch . the flow of blood. Then he lifted the still warm body and carried it round the car, r . placing it carefully on the floor of the tonneau, ii where it was well out of sight. - u Next, he drew a white rose from his button- hole and placed it in that of his victim, and then he closed the door. ll Shutting down the bonnet he jumped into Ithe driving seat and started the car. The whole tragedy had not occupied more than three minutes. " Now to dispose of the body in an out-of- the-way, corner," he murrnur,cci, " as far from Hampstead as possible." Driving on his top speed he left the Heath, cutting sharply across Harbpsteatt in the direction of Maida Vale. From that point he made for the Bayswater Road, through the purlieus of Paddington. - Once in the rn ai,u —west _thorough fare he drove straight ahead towards Shepherd's Lush. Once there, he turned off , sharply down a side street, where all was quiet, and drew up at the gate of a lonely garden. Lifting his burden from the tonneau, he carried it swiftly across the pavement and laid it down just inside the gate, on which was painted "Acacia Villa." In less than a minute he was back in the car, driving eastwards by side streets, till he thought it safe to strike the main road, a mile away from the spot. To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>AsSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>AsSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
