      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON RINGERS "A " v. WARRINGTON "A.'</p>
        <p>These teams met at Broughton before a large crowd on Saturday. The visitors were the first to attack, and clever combination caused hot work on the Rangers' goal line. By the aid of short rushes the Rangers cleared, but the respite was not of long duration, for Marsham, Short, and Jordan excelled in some back play, and after Bibby had been held up near the line. Jordan scored from a sharp pass, and Knight placed his side further ahead when he landed a goal. The game took an even turn after this, and for a lengthened time play waged between the quarter flags until the Rangers rushed several packs, and from a penalty Horton landed a goal. At half-time Warring- ton led by five points to two points. Changing ends the superiority of the visitors was again manifest, and their backs establishing a strong attacking position, Knight eventually landed a penalty goal. The Rangers con- tinued to defend well, and after Horton effected relief by finding centre touch, a little of the defiit was rubbed off when C. James placed a penalty goal. Warrington were always the better team and eventually ran out winners by two goals one try (seven points) to Broughton Rangers, two goals (four.points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>CARE OF THE SICK POOR.</head>
        <p>CARE OF THE SICK POOR.</p>
        <p>Proposed Resident Medical Officer at Warrington Workhouse.</p>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD TO BE APPROACHED.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Board of Guardian; on Wednesday decided to apply to the Local Government Board for an order enabling them to appoint a resident medical officer at the Workhonie if the Guardians, after further con- sideration, decide that the change is necessary. The meeting was presided over by Mr. J. Billinge and there were also present :—Hrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Rev. Dr. Mercer, Dr. Murray, Messrs. W. Hickman (vice-chairman), A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw, G. Thompson, P. Longton, T. H. Ashcroft, S. Davies, A. Brooks, W. Houghton,J.Ball,J.Backhouse, R.Flannagan, M. W. Seton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools.) THE PROPOSED RESIDENT MEDICAL OFFICER. , RESIGNATION OF THE MASTER'S CLERK. At a meeting of the House Committee held on September Bth, the question referred back of the desir- abslity of appointing a resident medical officer was again considered and after a full and lengthy discussion it was unanimously resolved to recommend the Board Warrington v. Broughton Rangers. to approve of the minutes of August 18th as orignally submitted. Cup-Holders Trounced at Home I The recommendations were as follows :—After pro- longed discussion it was agree@l unanimously " that There was an excellent attendance at Warrington on Saturday to witness the encounter with Broughton owing to the growth of inmates, it is no longer p ractic- Rangers. Though such men as Fish, Shugars, and able for a doctor in general practice to give sufficient Naylor were absentees from the home side it was ex- time for the proper treatment of the sick indoor poor, panted that the cup-holders would give the Rangers a and that the Board be recommended to proceed to the hard game. No one anticipated the actual result, for appointment of a resident medical officer for the Work- Warrington were completely out-classed and had to house*" To give effect to this the following three suffer defeat by 19 points to nil. Their form was too resolutions were adopted : bad to be true, while the visitors were super-excellent. I (1). That the Guardians appoint a resident medical , officer to be responsible for the medical and surgical Splendid weather favoured the contest, and the ground was in the best condition fot a fast game. Teams i treatment of the indoor poor, of the indoor staff, : Warrington.—J. Hallam, back ; Harris, Isherwood, I (2). That the Local Government Board be requested and for the training of the nurses. Dickinson, and Kenyon, three-quarter backs ; Hocken- hall an Brooks, half backs ; Preston, Boardman Ito issue an order, empowering the Guardians to appoint d Thomas, J)lley, Swift, Harmer, Ashton and Cook, such an officer. forwards. I (3). That the Clerk be instructed to communicate I . .</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Broughton 11 2 5 19 *Warrington ... 0 0 0 *York . _ _</p>
        <p>*Dewsbury *Rochdale H. 0 1 3 Millom 0 1 • 3 *Salford Leigh 1 1 5 *Swinton 0 0 0 *Kefghley Wakefield *Oldham ...... ...... 2 2 10 Wigan *Leeds Hull *Halifax *Widnes Runcorn (Played on the ground of the Club marked *).</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers.—Poole, back; Hogg, Wilson, Harry, and Harris, three-quarter backs ; S James and NV. James, half backs ; Trotter, Darlison' Smith, Preston, kVinskill, Oram, Bentham and Ruddock, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Bruckshaw (Stockport) ; touch judges, Messrs. E. Gordon (Widnes), and W. H. Cawley (Runcorn). The Ringers won the toes and opened in strong fashion with a good breeze behind them. Capital pass- ing by the brothers James caused the home defence some anxious moments, but eventually Hallam cltared. The forwards worked hard and rushed the game to the Broughton end where Preston nearly scored. Only a brief stay was made here for the visitors showed great determination and punted well. Kenyon fielded the ball well in his own half but was heavily thrown and lost the ball. The Rangers dribbled up speedily, and Dickinson failing to stop the rush Wilson got a some- what easy try near the posts. NV. Harris easily added a goal. Warrington rallied pluckily but could make little headway against the strong work of Poole and Wilson. The Rangers were soon attacking wmin,and S.Janies made a nice opening for Wilson. The fatter ran strongly and though challenged he swerved. cleverly over the line with a fine try, which was not improved upon. Hardly had the game restarted when Warrington were penalised and W. Harris kicked a fine goal from the half-way line. The home team showed to little advantage until the interval when the Rangers led by ten points to nil.</p>
        <p>these resolutions to the Local Government Board, together with the statement of the terms and conditions upon which Dr. Braids holds the position of Workhouse medical officer, and ask the Board whether they will approve of those suggestions ; on the termination of Dr. Braide's appointment as Workhouse medical officer, and the amount of compensation if any to which he may be entitled. At the same committee meeting the resignation of Mr. Turpin, master's clerk, was accepted with regret consequent upon his appointment as assistant master of Southampton Workhouse. The Master suggested and the Committee approved the suggestion that the desig- nation of the office should in future be officially known as the assistant master,and instructions were given that such an officer be advertised for at a salary of £35 rising to a maxmimum of £4O after one years's approved service, together with the usual allowances. The following contracts were accepted :—leather, Messrs. Birchall (Liverpool); drapery, I'. J. Lee ; meat, H. Singleton ' • fish and ice, J. Porter, Limited ; . brushes, S. N. Green ; boots, A. Taylor ; uniform, .1. Critchley ; tailors goods, T. J. Lee ; coffins, E. Connor; clogs, W. Jones ; groceries, H. Milling and Co. ; flour &amp;c., J. Hallvvood •, wines and spirits, Rowson and Son ; milk, Messrs. Whitlow (Derbyshire) ; coal, Messrs. Evans and Co. At a meeting on September 15th, probationer Nurse Hackney wrote resigning her post, and asking to be liberated at the end of a fortnight. The resignation was accepted, and it was resolved to insist upon one • _ .</p>
        <p>On resuming with the wind behind them Warrington made a promising show and were quickly in the Rangers quarter. Hockenhall, Isherwood and Kenyon got away with fine passing, but the wing man failed to get oier the line. Broughton at once transferred play to the other end and some quick transfers to the right, left Hogg in possession. The Scotchmen easily handed Kenyon off and scored at the corner. Again Warrington took the upper hand for a moment and Kenyon was only pulled down on the line. From the next scrum, S. James kicked away and Hallam being slow, Hogg took the ball from him and carried it the length of the ground, Ruddick finally completing a remarkable effort. The homesters were still capable of a spasmodic effort and the forwards once bungled a grand chanc3 on the line,but the Rangers were greatly the superior team. They established themselves in the home half and clever combination between halves and centres resulted in Wilson scoring the fifth try far out. Final: Broughton Rangers, 5 tries (19 points) ; War- rington, nil. MORECAMBE v. RUNCORN. In beautifully fine weather, and before a moderate gate, this game was contested at Morecambe. The visitors kicked off, and after several exchanges Runcorn exhibited good passing, and a movement along the three-quarter line resulted in Flannigan getting over, Butterworth failing at the goal. There was no further score in the first half, when Runcorn led by three points to_Morecambe nil.</p>
        <p>month's notice to be given as per agreement upon appointment, or the forfeiture of a month's salary. It was resolved that Wm. Turner be permanently appointed tailor at a weekly wage of 28s. The three lady guardians were appointed to purchase the necessary furniture for the assistant master's quartes. Mr.PORSHAW, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said that the change of name from the master's clerk to assistant master was a distinction without a difference. It was only the same officer under another name, and by giving him that name they would probably get a better man. Mr. Titowsos seconded. The committee, he said went very carefully into the question of a resident medical officer. The second part of the recommendation troubled him a little until that morning—that was that the Local Government Board be requested to issue an order empowering the Guardians to appoint such an officer. He had it from Mr. Sutton that the Board did not commit themselves to anything whatever by that resolutiun. The CLERK : It would simply be an enabling order Mr. TuomPsos said the Board had the option when they got the order as to whether they accepted it or not. So long as that was the case they were on safe ground. Personally he failed to see when a medical officer had done the work for many years, as was stated at the last meeting, in one hour per day, where a resident officer would find work enough to keep him employed. The Board would have to go into the question of whether some • .</p>
        <p>On resuming Morecambe worked hard, Lamb, Beach. and Shaw shining, and liongmire made a couple of good runs. At length this player got over the line and equalised, he failing to convert his own try. Runcorn though they lost more than one opportunity, played trongly, and Watton scored a try from some scrambling splay, and Flannigan also got over the line. Butter- worth failed to improve either of these tries. Result:— Runcorn 3 tries (9 points), Morecambe 1 try (3 points). WIDNES v. ST. lIELENS. Played at Widnes, before 3,000 spectators. The home team had the advantage of the wind, and early on both Davies and Alf. Taylor made unsuccessful attempts to place a goal. Subsequently tha Widnes backs executed a fine round of passing which ended in Peacock scoring a try. Alf. Taylor failed at the goal kick. After this Widnes kept up the attack, and the St. Helens defence was sorely taxed. Half-time score : Widnes 1 try (3 points), St. Helens nil. Early on in the second half Widnes were penalised, and this enabled Traynor to kick a goal from an easy position. After this the game was very fast and ex- citing, and some feeling was shown by both teams, several players being cautioned by the referee. Nothing more was scored, and the result was Widnes 1 try (3 points), St. Helens 1 goal (2 points). Teams : Widnes—Davies, back; Gill, J. Taylor, Whelan and Peacock, three-quarter backs ; Ditolifield and A. Taylor, half-backs ' • G. Aspey, S. Aspey, Cockram, M`Cabe, M`Donald, Rigby, Webster, and Whitfield, forwards. St. Helens—Foulkes, back; Hunter, Traynor, Creevev and Barton, three-quarter backs ; Toole and Banks,half- backs ; Briers, Prescett, Mooney, Carney, Lee,Wharton, Johnson and Parr. forwards. Referee, Mr. R. H. Moore, Oldham; touch judges, Messrs, Shaw, Wigan, and Davies, Flixton. RUNCORN v. OLDHAM. At Runcorn, before 1,000 people. Both teams were well represented. Runcorn had slightly the best of the I first half, but could not score. They also held the advantage in the second half and executed some floe kicking and passing, but were unable to break down the Oldham defence. The game ended in a pointless draw. Runcorn.—Houghton, back ; Harrison, Butterworth, Hesketh, and Flanagan, three-quarter backs ; Jolley and Roberts, half backs ; Walker, Richardson, Dixon, Leyland, Padbury, Sraith,Kennedy,and Dobcon,forwards Oldham.—Thomas, back ' • Cash, McLean, Fletcher,and Carpenter, three-quarter backs ; Lees and White, half- backs ; Ferguson Owens, Wright, Ham, 'Popham, Ellis, Smith and Vowles forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Kennedy, Bronehton. Touch judges, Mssrs, Turner, Warrington, and Critchley, Widnes.</p>
        <p>additional work could be found to keep a medical officer fully employed. It was a regret to all of them that Mr. Turpin was leaving the service of the Board. (Hear, hear.) He had been a very good assistant and Mr. Awbery would be very much at a loss without him. They could only hope that the assistant master they were advertising for would be a suitable person. Mr. ASHCROFT moved an amendment that the whole question of the appointment of a resident medical officer be referred back. fie contended that the Unions visited by the sub-committee before resolving upon the appointment were not suitable com- parisons with Warrington as their populations were larger. It was stated at the last Board meeting that Dr. Braide was very attentive to his duties at the House but that they only occupied about an hour per 1 day. He (Mr. Ashcroft) tailed to see why the Board should appoint a resident medical officer when they could not find sufficient work for him to do. It had been contended that if the Board did not appoint a resident medical officer it would be necessary to employ trained nurses at a greater cost. He, however, under- stood that at present they employed twelve probationers, whereas if trained nurses were substituted only eight would be required. The amendment was not seconded, and fell to the , ground. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said that to be quite frank in the matter the Board, by confirming the minutes, would be binding itself to the definite statement that owing to ! the growth of inmates it was no longer practicable for la doctor in general practice to give sufficient time to the proper treatment of the sick indoor poor. There was an absolute agreement on the committee as to the unsatisfactory nature of the existing conditions. They had practically agreed that the time was come for a resident medical officer to be appointed. Mr. Ashcroft's statement that fewer trained nurses than the number of probationers would be required, was in absolute opposi- tion to the opinion of the medical officer, the superin- tendent nurse, and all the members of the House Com- mittee. ' The Rev. Dr. MERCER consideied Mr. Ashcroft's re- marks were premature. Certainly the Board committed themselves to the desirability of having a resident medical officer but they did not commit themselves to the absolute necessity of appointing one. It would be a matte: of what further arrangements could be made. He agreed with the new appointment provided they could find the resident medical officer enough work to do. It was only fair to remember that whilst the Board had great sympathy with the sick poor inside the House they must have some regard also to the honest work- ingman who strove his utmost and kept himself. It was not fair that that man should be taxed for men of his own class who bad not striven but had made them- selves chargeable to the rates. Mr. HICKMAN supported the minutes, pointing out that when the Board obtained the order they would have the option of making the appointment or other- wise. He thought the appointment would be a step in the right direction. The minutes were approved. THE VAGRANCY PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>The Chairman was elected to represent the Board on a committee selected from the Unions of the North Western district to deal with the vagrancy problem. Mr. THOMP,ON said it was time the authorities put their heads together and did something to put a stop to the vagrancy evil which was a disgrace to the com- munity. Mr. DAVIES did not think the Workhouses ought to be troubled with tramps. There should be a central place in the town, under the supervision of the police, where deserving men could obtaiu tickets entitling them to food and-lodgings.</p>
