      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SEPTEMBER 23rd 1905.</p>
        <p>General BOOTFI'S THE POOR MAN AND emigration scheme, THE LAND, which is still some-</p>
        <p>what lacking in de- tail and definiteness, has given a new meaning to the word Imperialism. It has proved, for one thing, that the Australian colonies, contrary to expectation, are prepared to open their doors wide to immigrants from the Mother Country. It has also given a stimulus to the movement for divid- ing up the lands of the earlier settlers into smaller lots. Australia, with a, most wonderful opportunity of establishing a land system on a new basis, which might have been an example to the world,absolutely got into a worse tangle than that of the old countries. The early settlers took what land they pleased, and took it in vast quan- tities. They led hard, lonely lives. They had no neighbours for scores of hundreds of miles. But where some of them lived solitary on their farms, towns and cities have since sprung up. Land that was waste no great number of years has row the value that is given to it by a numerous population. The old settler families are wealthy aristocrats nowadays, with as firm a hold of their acres as any English Duke. The question now is whether they shall not be com- pelled to sell, at reasonable rates, all but as much land as would still leave a large estate. The people of the older parts of Australia are getting impatient at being 'kept away from the land, that is to 'say, and this impatience has been greatly stimulated by General BOOTH'S inquiry as to what amount of space can be placed at the disposal Of 5,000 families of desirable British immigrants. This plan for finding accommodation in the new world for those who have had a rather chilling experience of the old is General BOOTH'S way of thinking Imperially. He has succeeded with his Canadian emigration scheme, and there is, there- fore, fair ground for supposing that, the means being placed at his command, he would succeed in Australia on h much larger scale. M.mit of the Colonies are ready, even eager, to welcome and second his efforts. The explanations that have I been made as to the immigration laws not being r intended to keep out such immigrants as those which General BOOTH has in his mind, are them. selves important results of the question so directly and unhesitatingly put by the head of the Salva- tion? Army. It certainly has appeared as if the Australian Colonies desired, from fear of competition and a consequent lowering of wages, to keep out emigrants from this country. It is not even now certain that the immigration laws will not stand in the path of General BOOTH. If, however, they should do so it now seems probable that they will be altered to meet the newly-arising circumstances. Australia is waking up to the fact that it needs men, and that population means prosperity. The plan of sending "the landless men to the manless land" has taken hold of the imagination alike of Australia and of England. There are 2,000,000</p>
        <p>acres available, close to railways in the Artesian Belt, says the Premier of New South Wales. Similar messages have come from other parts of Australia. The main difficulty now will be for General BOOTH to raise the funds fox carrying out his scheme. He will naed £1,000,000, and there are multi-millionaires who complain of the burden of their wealth, and who, according to their own account, are determined not to die rich. All that is necessary therefore, is to bring about a rapprochement between the chief of the Salvation Army and some great owner of millions who is staggering under the too vast orb of his fate.; That the settlement in Australia of a large number of such families as General Boma con- templates in his scheme would be for the advantage alike of the emigrants and of the Colonies there can be no reasonable doubt. It is not so clear that any equivalent advantage would accrue to this country by the removal from it of so many of those who should be the backbone of the nation. The landless man has surely some right to obtain access to the land on that side of the world on which he was born. But he is being constantly and in larger numbers driven away from the soil. In places where he might get a little land to work upon he cannot get a cottage to live in. Landlords will not build cottages nowadays. In times before so much !farming was done by machinery they were compelled to build cottages, for they must have their farm labourers about them. But all that is changed,and the cottage is too generally regarded as a nuisance which should be left to ruin and decay. If General BOOTH'S scheme is to be carried out successfully in Australia, where it is said that even the sons of Australian farmer cannot obtain access to the land, there will have to be a reforma- tion of the Colonial land laws. A similar reforma- tion here would make the scheme unnecessary, and energy, perseverance, and enterprise would meet with the same reward in England as beyond the seas.</p>
        <p>At a special Town MORE SECRECY. Council meeting held on Monday evening the resolutions concerning the appointment of a coin- nsittee of investigation into Mr. Lawson's contract, the engaging of an expert civil engineer and a shorthand writer were formally confirmed. The proceedings lasted a minute and a half, which con- stitutes a record—and a not very enviable one— for a Warrington Town Council meeting. Natur- ally the business was not very illuminating. Prkr to this meeting, however, the members sitting as a Genoral Purposes Committee, had had a most interesting and heated confab lasting a full hour. The business was really conducted here with closed doors—the so-called Council meeting being nothing more or less than a formal farce. If the latest developments relating to the huge expenditure under the contract really required discussion as well as confirmation, then the procsedings should not have been conducted in camera. The whole trouble of to-day can be traced to a policy of secretiveness in the past, and we are very sorry to see that the lesson is only half learned. •</p>
        <p>Every argumen THE INIQTJITY 01' CHINESE even the m let LABOUR. specious —that we</p>
        <p>lave heard in de- fence of Chinese labour was pulverieed by Mr. R. L. Outhwaite in the course of his two addresses this week. We rejoice that he has had an audi- ence of at least 2,500 Warrington people, for the weight of his utterance must have a lasting influence on the publin mind. The subject has been a vexed one ever since the Chinese Labour Ordinance was passed by Parliament, and unfor- tunately only a comparatively small number of people have had any comprehension of what it involved and the iniquitous conspiracy which led up to the introduction of the Chinese coolie to the Rand mines, People have said the cheap labour was necessary, even while they deplored the neces- sity, and without expert knowledge it was difficult to refute the statement. Mr. Outhwaite is:clearing the air of misapprehensions wherever he goes, and he has let a lot of light into the subject in Warrington. Chinese labour is not even necessary to work the poorest paying mine. The skilled white Iminer could get out the ore at as low a working cost, and endure the arduous work just as readily, but—and here is the crux of the matter—he would in time want a vote. The Rand-lords— those unscrupulous financiers who lisp their sham patriotism in broken English—do not want the white man with his independent spirit, with his power of industrial combination, and with his capacity for having a voice in tha management of affairs. And they have succeeded so far in barring him out. With what a thrill of shame and dis- gust did the representative audience on Wednes- day listen to Mr.Onthwaite's story of the rise of the continental capitalist in;SouthAfrica.lle engineered the wit., he overthrew the Boer Government, and " ftee, enlightened England" helped him. Now, this worst species of gold-bug owns the colony we spent £250,000,000 and 20,000 lives to bring beneath the flag, and can practically snap his Lingers at the Mother Country, which, like a deluded old hen, has been hatching viper's eggs. But the voice of public opinion has righted greater wrongs and, in course of time, it will release the Chinese serf from the compound and open the way for white labour in the country which was won with white man's blood. Mr. Outhwaite is doing a vast public service and we hope and believe he will succeed in waking the people of this country to a full and conscientious realisation of their bounden duty towards the South African colony which is speeding on to social ruin and inevitable anarchy.</p>
        <p>The cause of Free To DEFEND FREE TRADE, Trade in this dis-</p>
        <p>trict should be strengthened considerably by the formation of a branch of the Free Trade League for the Warring- ton Parliamentary division. Free Traders of whatever political colour, are now enabled to meet on one platform in defence of the policy which they believe should be maintained in the interests of our commercial prosperity and the well-being of the people. The League is in no sense a party organization and, as was pointed out at the inaugural meecing last (Thursday) evening, that critical body the Labour Representation Committee is quite satisfied as to its bona fides in this respect. Men who have felt it their duty to dissociate them- selves from the official Conservative policy on this most vital of all questions to the nation, are among the most active workers of the League. To their mind Mr. Chamberlain's proposals—and the great Prophet of Protection openly claims to ' have the support of Mr. Balfour—would spell dis- aster and must be resisted. Mr. Lindsoll, who has acted as election agent to the Conservative party I in the Altrincharn division, was particularly I' earnest in his advocacy of the League at the War- rington- meeting, and he complained with justice that Conservatives have not been treated with frankness on • this great issue by their leaders. There has already been abundant evidence that the people of England do not intend to abandon the system which lies at the root of our national progress, but when Free Trade is so repeatedly assailed in certain quarters, too much educational work cannot be dons. We wish the Warrington branch of the Free Trade League the fullest measure of success.</p>
        <p>Local Artistes at Newton. A highly successful vocal recital was given in the Congregational Schoolroom, Newton, on Thursday evening by Madam Hettie Bradley Kelly, of New York concerts, whose songs were much appreciated. The programme was also contributed to by Mrs. Harry Haviour (solo violin) and Mr. J. llabbitt (tenor) of Warrington. Mr. W. H. Payton,' Mus. Bac., • accompanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>"Now tell us all about the war, And what they killed each other for."—SournEr. Mr. T. W. 11. Crosland*, o*f `*' The Unspeakable Scot" fame, will be pleased to learn from the Royal review that there are still at least 38,383 Scots on the further side of the Border.</p>
        <p>* * * "It is not so much the fact that the masses are missing the Church as that the Church is missing the masses." Thus Mr. E. Osborne at the Co-operative Hall on Wednesday. There is some wisdom in small compass here. What have the clergy and ministers to say? * * *</p>
        <p>Germany is one of those blessed lands where Pro- tection flourishes. The British worker will therefore be interested in a "Daily Chronicle " Berlin telegram which states that since the recent scarcity in meat began to be felt the consumption of horseflesh has multiplied fourfold in most of the populous centres. * * * Is coughing a natural or an acquired habit? The latter, undoubtedly, in the majority of cases. Thera is an old gentleman in the reading-room of the British Museum who, whenever he is disturbed in his studies by a cough, invariably imitates it. He is a perfect mimic, and can reproduce every variety of cough instantaneously. At first indignant,the cougher aftet- words sinks into silence, for coughing can be con- trolled by will-power.---" Daily Chronicle." * * * Wartington's rates are high enough in all con- science, but we are not by any means the most heavily burdened community in the kingdom. In the island lof Barra, in the Outer Hebrides, where the adminis- tration of the poor-law has deve!oped into a deadlock, the local rates have reached 20s. 8(1, in the pound- The rates due to the paupers some weeks ago have not yet been paid, and it is said that over sixty frail and indigent persons have been obliged to beg from door to door for the bare necessaries of life. * * * Figures for Protectionists : St. Louis. Liverpool. House Rent (per annum) £2lO to £350 £5O to £7O Coal (per ton - to 35/- 13f- to 19/- Business Suit Frockcoat Suit Lsdies' Hat Gloves (per pair) - to 9/- 3/- to 4/- Bread (per 41b.) Hair Cutting l/6 to 2/- 3d. to 6d. Shaving 7ld. to 10d. 3d. to 4d. The figures are taken from " Milling," which received them from a visitor from the States last month. * * * The importance of teaching children to swim is becoming so generally acknowledged by those at the heed of educational affairs in the London area that it has been decided to make some most elaborate arrangements for reserving the ornamental waters in the public parks for the use of school children of both sexes at certain fixed hours. At these times the chil- dren attending the elementary schools will be taken once a week in batches and taught swimming under competent instructors. The arrangements are to be completed during the winter, so that when spring returns a regular course of tuition may be undertaken- This is a splendid object lesson to the provinces. Our large towns do very little in the way of helping the children to learn to swim. * * * An interesting"character sketch of D. C. Cummings appears in this month's issue of "The Labour Record." He was elected by the committee to be its chairman for the year, and he becomes president of the next Trade Union Congress at Liverpool. Born at Greenwich in December, 1361, he was educated at Roon's School and at one of the London Board schools heading the list of scholarships in 1874. On leaving i school, n 1875, he was apprenticed at Rennie's ship- building yard, and in the year 1880, at the age of 19, joined the Boilermakers and Iron and Steel ship- builders Society. * * * " Having declared that the war was unjust, we (the Congress of Trade Unions) "can wipe our hands of our responsibility absolutely and entirely of any- thing which has followed. We must spend 250 millons of money, and to a great extent that is pFn- ducing the slackness of work from which our industries ' are suffering. We lost something like 20,000 of our boys, and we have 60,000 or 70,000 maimed -workers at home, and all the results that have accrued from r this enormous expenditure of treasure and life and our integrity is this miserable chattel slavery which is re- introduced within the Empire. We want the ordinance I entirely revoked and the slaves returned to China at the expense of the mine owners "—John Ward, on Chinese slavery. * * * The superintendent of Mr. Rockefeller's estate at Forest Hill says that the richest man in the world is unable to throw off the gloom occasioned by the accusations that his money is tainted and by other attacks. He describes his employer as taking no interest in anything except cuttings from the news- papers which contain a flood of denunciations, which seem to fascinate him. Mr. Rockefeller eagerly grasps at and preserves anything showing a friendly disposition towards him. He sits hours in his garden* listless, idle, and not speaking a word except where it is absolutely necessary. He reads again and again the statement by Miss Ida Tarbell (the historian of the Standard Oil Company) that his wealth has besmirched charity and religion where it has touched them.</p>
        <p>* * * The story of a Tory's idle boast and what came of it affords amusing reading. During the South African war Mr. F. Hastings Medhurst, the Conservativo candidate for West Islington, made a challenge that he would give ten guineas on each occasion on which the services of Mr. Lloyd-George, or certain other so-called pro-Boers, were secured for a meeting in that. constituency. He was in consequence obliged to pal, twenty guineas to the West Islington Liberal ana- Radical Association, and they have divided that sure '- equally between the Surgical Aid Society and the Friendly Societies' Convalescent Home at Dover, Ifr* Medhurst, however, is not yet out of the wood, sale the " Star," for Mr. Lloyd-George will certainlY address further meetings in the division between now 4 and the General Election, come when it may. * * * Our readers will, we have no doubt, be keenly ' terest,ed in the man who made so remarkable a speech at. Parr Hall on Tuesday evening—Mr. Robert Leonard Outhwaite. He is a typical " Cornstalk,' standing well over six feet high, but residence irt Eegland and the strain of addressing so many night meetings have removed the bronze usually seen on the faces of our Antipodean cousins. Mr. Outhwaite 'a father was one of the early wool-growers in the colony of Victoria, and the subject of our note spent the early year of his life in pastoral pursuits. Latterly he has been settled as a farmer in Victoria, always taking, however, a keen interest in local- affairs. By the way, there is little to suggest the farmer in Mr. Outhwaite's exterior. Of gentlemanly address he wears a grave and thoughtful expression and it is evident that to-day deeper questions agitate his mind than those relating to sheep-grazing and the cultivation of crops. He was one of the founders• of the Free Trade Liberal Association of Australia,which has branches in every State, and is a member of the executive. After the conclusion of the South African War, through which a younger brother fought, Mr'• ' Outhwaite went to South Africa to investigate the general conditions there, particularly those relating te land settlement questions. After a trip across the veldt Mr. Outhwaite returned to Johannesburg, and very soon learned from a Colenial friend that there had been a meeting between Mr. Chamber- lain and the mine owners on the Rand at which the Colonial Secretary had consented to the introduction of Chinese labour on their promising t° assist in the flotation of a ,-..:30,000,000 war loan. leaflet was immediately got out and circulated itt Johannesburg, but Mr. Chamberlain denied the state- ment. From that time, however, the Chinese labour agitation grew, and finally the ordinance was passed by the House of Commons. Then Mr. Outhwaite cane to England for the purpose of putting the facts of the case before the British electorate, with the mandate of ' his Association and ten million Colonials behind him. !Since arriving in England he has taken part in. crl.3 by-elections and addressed about 400 ineetin,e- -' During his peregrinations Mr. Outhwaite landed at .• Birmingham and began a series of enthusiastic mil , ings right in the heart of "Joseph's " stronghol !.,, • under the auspices of the West Birmingham Liberal Association. The sequel to this is that Mr. Outhwaite , has been requested to fight the General Eleotion that constituency against Mr. Chamberlain. Thatt invitation has, so far, not been deanitely acoopted bu, when the campaign opens it may be taken for grailti.e.cit ,e we think, that Mr. R. L. Outhwaite will try a . with his red-mbtable opponent and probably `Allusc' • him considerably, ' " '• ' : - 7 •</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Notice. The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on Priday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the Proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any • address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- ' lioon without extra charge. ' Address all communica- tions to "Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington WASHING AND WRINGING MACHINES try J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55 Bridge-street, Warrington,— ADVT.</p>
        <p>index to Inside Pages. PemE. 2. Women's Chat and Fashions. The Waterworks Contract. Local Churches. 3. "The White Rose," by Gerald Bias. Football. Board of Guardians. 6- Education Committee. Police News. Children's Corner. 7. "Mr. Balfour and Golf" by Dr. McPherson, Runcorn Board of Guardians. Bowling. H. M. AsuToN's LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Coming Events A public meeting will be held in the Co-operative 'all, Sankey-street, on Monday week when a presenta- tion of a purse of 300 guineas and an enlarged photo- g, Mph will be made to the manager, Mr. A. H. Rath- bone, in recognition of the completion of 40 years' service with the Co-operative Society. A musical Programme will be submitted. A- bazaar will be held in the Parr Hall on October fitb, 6th, and 7th, in connection with St. Mary's (R.C.) Church, Buttermarket-street. The bazaar will be 9„Pened on Thursday by Lady Acton, on Friday by Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., and on Saturday by Mr. A. H. Crosfield. Particulars of the entertainments, &amp;c., 'which will be held, will be found in our advertising eolumns. The Education Committee has arranged for a course "sf University Extension lectures on " A trio of esSayists : Lamb, Hazlitt, and De Quincey," to be delivered in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, com- mencing on Friday, October 6th, by Yr. W. Lawrence ehroeder, M. A. (teacher of reading and elocution in the Victoria University of M-Inchester.) A bazaar will be held in the Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom on Wednesday and Thursday next, by 'which it is hoped to raise £3OO on behalf of the build- ing fund in connection with the new Wesleyan Church and school at Stockton Heath. The bazaar will be opened on Wednesday by Mrs. J. Dodds and on Thurs- ,949 by Mrs. J. G. Withinshaw. During the proceed- ?Rs musical selections will be given by Miss Lloyd Williams' junior orchestra. The harvest thanksgiving services in connection 'with St. John's Presbyterian Church will be held on The preacher will be the Rev. J. L. ▪ B.D. , concert will be given by the Warrington Apollo gale Voice Choir in the Parr Hall on Monday evening. e artistes will include Miss Annie Goodwin, Mr. H. 'err", Mr. A. Weber, Mr. John Paley champion cornet eoloist of the world and Mr. Wilson Blacklodge. T▪ he harvest festival in connection with Bold-street wesleyan Church will be held on Sunday when the Preachers will be the Revs. C. Parker and S. Pitt. ' The harvest thanksgiving services in connection ' ith the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church will be, held on Sunday, when sermons will be delivered by Rev, W. Scott, of Wishaw. In the afternoon the sacred cantata "Esther the Beautiful Quen" will be given, A recital will he given by Mr. John Duxhury, the known Manchester elocutionist, in the Wesleyan tilurch, Bold street, on Monday evening. Vocal items *will be contributed by Mrs. A. Booth. .The harvest thanksgiving services in connection lth S. Barnabas' Church commence this (Friday) 'Zening, when the preacher will be the Rev. Canon ;-"-x°ll, of Waterloo.--On Sunday the services will be „antinued, and the preachers will be :—Morning, Rev. "i• Pollock-Hill; afternoon, Rev. T. W. Barber; e°suing, Rev. W. Brown-Pollock, of Liverpool. Personal. The Bishop of Chester on Tuesday instituted the E. P. Wetherhead, M.A., to the Rectory and alish Church of Ortghtington, near Warrington.</p>
        <p>„ ASH for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL ATRRS.—Advt. I 1 Late Mr. S. B. Stringer. Ihexvill of the late Mr. Samuel Bennet Stringer, of "”. Wilderspool-road, Warrington, has been proved 3157. tuning Classes at Stockton Heath. -T The Stockton Heath evening classessvill open in St. 1 2 025' school during the week commencing October 01",41- Classes will be held in arithmetic, English and j'°rtliand. Intending students are requested to send their names to the teachers, whose addresses will found in our advertising coining, on or before the 'Jove date. ~Ntkrt, CARTS Mid Bsssinettes. the latest designs. . "• S. GARLAND. 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. Ida -Arrington Tradesmen's Association. general meeting Of the Warrington Tradesmen's eociation was held in Toft's Assembly Rooms, ,;(Ige-street, on Tuesday evening 4 Mr. W. Roberts, Presiding. There was a good attendance The dection Of officers took place. The position of Presi- c?tit Was conferred upon Mr. Joseph Shaw as the a'ziest tradesman in the town. The Vice-Presidents W. Roberts, J P., Mr. H. N. Houghton, Mr. Pe ndlelDury, Mr. W. Isaac and Mr. J. E. Richardson. ir following were elected as the committee, viz:— \\;.• Councilor James Evans, Mr. Councillor Lord, Mr. t, • T. Williams, Mr. G. Bowcock, Mr. J. Banks, Mr. J. .21st, Mr. J. 11. Houghton, Mr. J. W. Hall, Mr. J. eY, Mr. W. Isherwood, Mr. C. Toft, and Mr. "n. Mr. J. Banks was appointed treasurer, and h. w oolhouse, secretary. Several subjects of Portance were discussed.</p>
        <p>Prizes for Children. Miss J. Bond, Wilderspool road, and E. Bonen members of our Children's Corner League, have been war led prizes for competitions they have entered. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGHCLASb MINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Athletic Successes. At Matlock sports on Saturday, J. Moran, of War- rington, from the six yards mark, won the first prize in the 220 yards handicap:in 23 seconds. C. Berry, of Warrington, was third in the half-mile bicycle, and W. Price, of Warrington, third in the two-mile walk. Frodsham Pear Tree's Record. Mr. George Jones, a Frodsham fruit grower, has this season gathered and marketed 14cwt, of pears from one tree. In addition to these about icwt. were spoiled, whilst quite levrt. was left on the tree, it being impossible to gather them. 'Considering the adverse season 151-cwt. of pears from one tree must surely be considered quite an unique record for Cheshire. The pear goes under the name of Victoria, and is a cross between a Hazel and a Goldnap.</p>
        <p>PITBLIC BENEFIT.-UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS 114 LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>--- - Success of Warrington Swimmers. At the Regent-road baths, Salford, on Monday even- ing, four Warrington swimmers, T. S. Pemberton on seven seconds, R. H. Allen on seven, J. Peers on five, and C. Turner on five, met with success in the 50 yards open handicap. In the semi- finals Pemberton won his heat comfortably, but he was eventually disqualified for starting before his name was called. Allen, unfortunately, had to swim in the same heht against his clubmate, Turner, who just beat him by a touch. Peers was also successful. The final, which was an exciting race resulting in a dead heat between Turner and Redfern, Peers third and Morris fourth. Turner and Redfern again swain twice resulting in a dead heat each time, and after a fourth struggle Turner won by three inches.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Ad Vt.</p>
        <p>Death of a Warrington Bowler. The death took place early on Saturday morning of IMr. James Shaw, aged 45 years,who resided in Farrell- street. Deceased, who was better known as " Shay " Shaw, was at one time one of the most prominent bowlers in Warrington and district, but he had taken no part in the game for about eighteen months, having been in failing health. A fortnight ago pneumonia set in, from vddch he never recovered. Before he had reached the age of 20 he took a great interest in bowling, and had been a member of the Bull's Head Bowling Club for several years. He assisted that club to win the Lancashire and Cheshire County Championship Cup in 1899. He had taken part in many important engagenaents' including the Black- pool main in 1003, and had carried off numerous prizes. He had met some of the best bowlers of the day, including Green, Ball, Taylor, Holker, Leach,and Hart.</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay. The chapel anniversary services in connection with the above were held on Sunday last. Mr. Peter Webster, of Widnes, preached two eloquent sermons, taking for his subject in the morning "An Ideal Man" (Isiaah 32-2), and in the evening The Larger Life." (John 10-10). The evening service was particularly impressive, this being the last occasion on which Mr. Webster will officiate before leaving for Canada to take up ministcAal work there. At the close of the service the hymn " God be with you" was sung with fervour, and Mr. T. Dale, on behalf of the members of the congregation, in a few well chosen words, wished the preacher God-speed in his new sphere of labour. In the afternoon a musical service was held, the following choruses being admirably rendered by the Warrington Male Voice Choir : " The Word went forth,' "The Peace of God," and "The Son of God." Solos were contributed in an excellent manner by Miss Carrie Hastings and Mr. S. Hassell. Mr. Webster gave a short address on "Is there a God ? " Mr. F. Atkinson presided at the rgan during the day. The services were very well ttended, and the collections realised £0 10s. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Adtit.</p>
        <p>People's Services. The first of a series of services for the people was held on Sunday evening when the Parr Hall was filled. Mr. A. E. Trow presided at the organ and Miss Fair- hurst was the soloist, giving a vary fine rendering of "He was despised." The Rev. H. Minnie°, who con- ducted the service, preached upon "The Lordship of Christ." The person of Cbrist, he said, was beyond doubt a subject of viital interest. The ripest scholar- ship and the most patient research had been brought to bear upon His life on earth, the social conditions of His day, and His relation to men of all ages. The greatest problems in the field of history all centred in the person and life of Jesus. Christ came to establish the Kingdom of God, and by that was meant the Kingship of God, the authority of God, and the government of God in and over, and above every sphere of life. God's will was not done as yet and the present conditions of life were not ideal, but by and by all that would be altered and Jesus would reign as universal King. It was sometimes urged that the teachings of Christ were impracticable and even a bishop had s impossible to that it was to govern a country on the principles laid down in the Sermon on the Mount. To that he would willingly sub- scribe if it were not for one thing—the power of Christ. He made an appeal to all who desired to see the Kingdom of God set up to crbwn Christ, as King of their own individual life. _</p>
        <p>KITCHEN RANGES, Fire Grates, Tiles &amp;c., large stock.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street,</p>
        <p>Warrington.—ADvT.</p>
        <p>Spiritualist Harvest Festival. The Spiritualist Society celebrated their harvest thanksgiving festival on Sunday and Monday. Generous contribution of flowers, fruit, and vegetables had been received which a willing band of helpers had arranged in a very tasteful manner, the interior of the Druids Hall presenting a pleasing appearance. The services on Sunday were conducted by Mr. Wilfred Rooke, of Manchester, who in the evening spoke to a good congregation on "/The harvest of the ages; the reapers being the angels." The anthems 'The Lord is my shepherd" and Daughter of Zion" were well rendered by the choir, Mr. J. Foster presid- ing at the organ. On Monday evening a public tea was held in the hall, followed by an entertainment, opened by Miss Ethel Chapman, of Stretford, with a pianoforte solo. Miss Chapman later gave another solo, "Recollections of England," and also accom- panied the lady vocalists, Mrs. Hannan and Miss Brayshaw.A descriptive pianoforte solo was played by Master Sixsmith. Mr. S. Dark gave a capable rendition of Mozart's "Favourite" waltz on the violin, and Mr. H. Foster sang "The toilers," Mr. Fred Foster accompanying in each case. Miss Lewis gave a mandoline select ion,accompanied by Mr .Clinton Lewis. The President read one of Mrs. Caudle's famous " Curtain lectures," and Mr. Maidmerit amused the , company by a ventriloquial sketch. After a vote of thanks to all who had assisted in the festival celebra- tion, the fruit and vegetables were sold. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE' CLOCK. BRID GE-STR,EET.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Forestry at Warrington. A. meeting of the Warrington District of the Ancient Order of Foresters was held on Monday evening at the Mason's Arms, Mill-street, when a large number of members attended to hear the report of the delegate at the High Court meeting. The chair was occupied by Bro. R. Thornley, P.D.C.R., and among those present were Bro. Hipkiss, D.C.R., Bro. L. Davies, district secretary, and the following Past District Chief Rangers :.-.-Bros. H. James, G. Maybury, G. Rotherham, H. Hill, R. Thornley, J. Walsh, district trustee ,• the following Past Chief Rangers :—Bros. Hayes, W. Bancroft, S. Healey, S. Baskerville, W. Carter, T. Smith, J. Jervis, and others. The Chair- man said the excellent meeting augured well for the prosperity of the district. (Applause.) Bro. W. Hipkiss, D.C.11., the delegate, gave his report of the Newcastle High Court meeting. Friendly societies were formed and developed for the working men. The delegate gave information relating to the general laws, which tended to promote solvency. He though all societies, trade or any other, should be put on the same footing as the friendly societies, and they ought I to be registered, and publish their accounts in the ; sate way as friendly societies. (Applause.) Bro. L. I Davies, district secretary, moved a vote of thanks to • Bro. Hipkiss for his excellent report of the High Court meeting. He hoped the members would take a greater interest in the work of, the Courts, and so extend the Order in general. (Hear, hear.) Bro. .E. Clayton seconded, and Bros. S. Smith and H. James supported the vote of thanks, which was carried with applanse- i Bro. Hipkiss suitably replied, and thanked the brethren for their appreciation of his services. 'Bro. G. May- bury moved a vote of thanks to Bro. Thornley for his services in the chair, and to Bro. Rotherham for his kindness in entertaining the party with selections on the gramophone, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Music In the Park. Messrs. Crosfield's Prize Band will play in the Park on Sunday afternoon at 3.15. The Mayoralty. A meeting of the General Purposes Committee for the election of Mayor will be held on Monday evening. Accident to Mr. F. W. Purse. Mr. F. W. Purse, assistant electrical engineer, is at present in the Infirmary suffering from injuries to the eye, sustained on Tuesday at the electrical depot. He is progressing satisfactorily and his sight will not be permanently injured.</p>
        <p>Children's League of Pity. An official acknowledgment had been received of a donation of twenty guineas banded over to the funds of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to children as the result of a sale of work organized by the Warrington branch of the Children's League of Pity. Fatal Accident at Lymm. A serious accident, attended with fatal conse- quences, happened on Saturday. Two children named Elsie Miller (5), Brookfield Cottage, Lymm, were play- ing near their homes when a heavy door frame fell on them, inflicting severe injuries. They were at once removed to the Warrington Infirmary, where it was found that Elsie Miller had received a scalp wound, and had also broken her rig'lt arm. She is, however, improving. Nora Johnson suffered from a fractured skull and fractured thigh, and she succumbed to her injuries on Tuesday afternoon. ' '</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feeling.—(Advt.) Oak Carving at St. Mary's Church. Admirers of English oak carving should take the opportunity of a visit to St. Mary's Church, Latchford, Warrington, where they will find as beautiful a speci- men of its kind as is to be found in the country. The work, consisting of a Sedilia surmounted by the Coats of Arms of His Holiness, Pope Pius x. and the Bishop of the Diocese (Right Rev. Dr. Allen), beautifully gilt, and choir stalls is the gift of a generous parishioner i who has always taken a keen interest n the well- being of the Mission of St. Mary's, and well befits the magnificent sanctuary of the Church in which it has been erected. The designs are by Messrs. Douglas and Minshull, Chester, and the execution by Bridgeman, Lichfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 23, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 23, 1905.</p>
        <p>FREE TRADE LEAGUE. FORMATION OF A WARRINGTON BRANCH. A Non-Party Organization. Warrington has now a local branch of the Free Trade League—a non-party organization consisting of those who believe that the Fiscal proposals now before the country would, if adopted, seriously injure all the in- dustries of the nation,and bring poverty to the working classes. The branch was formed at a well attended meeting held in Toft's Assembly Room, Bridge-street, on Thursday evening and con- vened by a circular signed by the following gentle- men :—Joseph Ainsworth, Rigby Armitage, Ziba Armitage, James Carter, Thomas B. Carter, George R. Crosfield, J. J. Crosfield, Stephen Dale, Rev. Herbert Daimler:), Joseph Elwell, Philip C. Garnett, Robert Edward Garnett, Linnteus Greening, Nathaniel Greening, Wilkinson Haigh, William Hutchings, Arthur H. Lee, William Long, Councillor John Magian, Dr. K. E. Markel, Edwin Plinston, F. It. Roberts and Samuel Wright. Mr. S. RIGBY ARMITAGE presidedand the attendance included Mr. Tom Garnett of Clithero (chairman of the League), Mr. F. R. B. Lindsell, of Altrincharn (one of the hon. secretaries), Rev. Herbert Diumico, Captain G. ft. Crosfield, IMessrs.liA. Rathhone, C. E. England, J. Morton, S. Wright, J. Elwell, R. Wiley, E. Plinston, G. Holford and E. Wynne-Jones. The CHAIRMAN said their main object was to induce both Conservatives and Liberals to cast their votes for the man who stood for Free Trade. Personally, as a LFeleral, if the Conservative member were to go for Free Trade and the Liberal candidate for Protec- tion, hi vote would go solidly for the Free Trade man, (Hear, hear.) It was incumbent on each one of them to try and impress that as the most solid argument they could put before the public—to vote straight, for Free I Trade, irrespective of political opinion. (Applause) Mr. Tom flartNErr, in an interesting address, said that if they were Free Traders before, they were infinitely stronger Free Traders now that they had been forced by the action of Mr. Chamberlain to look into the question for themselves. He thought there was no doubt that they stood at the parting of the ways on this question. He did not thinkanyone who had studied the history be it social or political of this country since the great work of one of the greatest of Conserve- tivestatesmen, Sir Robert Peel, was completed, could rise from that study without feeling that though there was still much to be done in the way of social amelioration and though there were many blots in England one would like to see removed, the progress of the country under thelsystem bequeathed to it by Sir Robert Peel had been prodigious. Had it not been for the sudden plunge into the question of an adventurous gentleman who hailed from a town who name had become a by-word for every sham, fraud, and imposture in the world, probably those who were assembled in that room would have gone on comfortably without studying the question for themselves. It became quite evident that it was the intention of that ambitious, eager, and energetic gentleman not only to drag his own following but to drag behind him the representatives of a great political party that up to now had been proud of the work accomplished by Sir Robert Peel. Some of them who were Tories long before Mr. Chamberlain ever pre- tended to be one felt they could not permit, if it were possible to prevent it, the Tory party as a whole to be taken as synonymous with this belief in economic heresy. The Free Trade League was the only body of its kind which had received the imprimatur of so critical a body as the Labour Repre- sentation Committee as to the absolute bona-fides of its claims to bo a non-party organitation. It included men of diverse opinions who were sinking all mere party questions in face of the great crisis with which the country was faced. The very life blood of ths country was bound up with the Free Trade system and a very few years trial of the system of Protection would be sufficient to put us behind our eager and energetio rivals who were struggling to obtain a greater share of the wealth of the world. To his mind the surest way to split up the empire was to introduce a question of fiscal strife. (Applause). Mr. T. R. B. TAINDSELL remarked that he had fought as election agent for the Conservative party in Altrin- cham division for something like twenty-five years. To those who were Free Trade Conservatives it seemed fortunate that they were able to meet on a platform absolutely independent of either party whilst remaining in their party and adhering to the policy supported by that party until two and a half years ago. They were able to say that before all other considerations they supported Free Trade,howover hard and bitter it might be to separate themselves on that question from the official Conservative party. Mr. Lindsell proceeded to show that the consumer would pay the greater proportion of any tax on imported corn as was evidenced by the high prices of wheat in Pro- footed nations. Protection was only possible to be discussed to-day because the generation that knew all about it had passed away and the people of this country, having no experience of the ill-effects of Pro- tection, looked upon it as something that might possibly do good and which, if it did harm, could be reversed. A change in the fiscal policy could not be reversed beontwe - whila we- were lee-ying clutiee ea eux irn ports our trade would go to other people. Mr. Lindsell complained that the Conservative leaders were not straight on the question and criticised the tactics of Mr. Balfour and Mr. Chamberlain. He hoped the Warrington branch of the League would do as honest and good work as other branches were doing for the nation. (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN pointed out that Sir Allah:el Hicks- Beach, ex-Chancellor of the Exchequer, had told a meeting that he had been responsible fore duty of is. a quarter on wheat. Previous to imposing the duty he went carefully into the question as to whether it was likely to affect the consumer of bread and had come to the conclusion that it would not. Now, however, he had to tell them candidly that it did. Mr. Armitage concluded by moving that a branch of the League be formed for the Warrington Parliamentary Division. Captain CROSFIRIA) seconded. Quite apart from the question of Free Trade about which he was thoroughly convinced, he thought that the League would do a great deal of good because it tended to weaken the slavish adherence to the two party system which had become such a baneful influence in the country. He did not care whether a man was a good Liberal or a good Tory, but he did care if he was ft good Englishman, and he admired the speakers who bad given up their natural prejudices towards their own party and had come forward on this great national question. It was not, however, sufficeint to oppose as something should be done to improve the physique of the nation which it had been shown had degenerated and emnloyers should adopt more scientific methods. The resolution was carried unanimously. The Rev. H. DUNNICO moved that the gentlemen whose names appeared on the circular, together with those present, should constitute a provisional com- mittee. He hailed the formation of the branch with great pleasure. It had always beety tb him a matter of deep personal regret thaftheso great econotnie!and moral questions should be dragged at tha tail of any party. After all economic questions were at heart moral ques- tions and they could not touch the economic basis of any country without in some way affecting its morals. (Hear, hoer.) • Mr. S. Warowr seconded. In his opinion Protection would mean the sacrifice of everything the Labour Party had obtained for the welfare of the working classes. It behoved workin,r'men to see that their interests were not sacrificed by any man or party. (Applause). This resolution was also agreed to. On the motion of the chairman saeoncied by Mr. Plinston a vote of thaaks was accorded to the speakers. In responding Mr. GARNETT said Lh.. members of the League did not in the slighteit delree sacrifice their liberty and independence of action on other questions when once the foe of Protection had been slain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>KILLED BY AN EXYRESS. " TWO ST.HELEN'S PLATE-LAYERS CUT TO PIECES In the broad daylight on Wednesday afternoon two platelayers in the employ of the London and North- Western Railway Company were literally cut to pieces hy an express passenger train about midway between Carr Mill and Garswood Stations, near St. Helens. The unfortunate men were Thomas Hibbert, about 30 years of age of School-lane, Garswood, and Wiliam Warren Wakefield (21), of Haresfinch-view, St. Helens. The terrible occurrence gave rise to a most pathetic incident. The wife of one of the men was on Garswood Station, and had booked for a journey to Wigan, when the news of the accident reached there. Knowing her husband was working on the line, she was naturally anxious, but was assured that her husband had escaped, and was not one of those killed. She accordingly pro- ceeded to Wigan, and, on her arrival back at Garswood the officials had to impart to her the troth—which was ascertained after her departure—and the poor woman's grief can be better imagined than described.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CRAWFORD'S</head>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>CREAM CRACKERS</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. [Ely " Ovalist."' For the first time since the last Saturday in April the cup-holders are feeling humble. They have had nearly five months of glory, and had quite forgotten what the taste of defeat was like. Would they ever lose again ? Perhaps, perhaps not! Anyhow, they felt like that uo to Saturday. Then the heavens fell, and the proud cup-holders were overwhelmed by disaster and humiliation. Broughton Rangers had been looked upon as prospective victims to be decently interred behind the church alongside the fresh corpses of Leigh and Oldham. But they rebelled. Yes, they actually failed to see eye to eye with Warrington on the matter, and if it was not or the price of land in Broughton the remains of poor Warrington would have been taken there for in- terment by the bold bad Rangers. They did play havoc in the Wilderspool dove-cot, to be still more metaphorical, and people are wondering whether it ever happened before that Warrington were beaten on their own ground by 19 points to nil. Yes, nineteen poihte to NIL!</p>
        <p>How did it happen? People always ask this sort of question when there has been some great calamity. They seem to have a morbid interest in the harrowing details. Though it grieves me greatly I will tell the sorry tale. The Rangers had evidently a vivid re- collection of a certain gloomy evening at the back-end of last season when they were taken in and done for to the tune of 19-3 at Wilderspool. Something about' the incident must have rankled for they came here on vengeance bent. That, as far as I can learn, is the cams belli. Warrington, with the forgetfulness of conquerors, had probably thought bye-gones were bye-genes. Not so the Rangers. They cried for blood, as it were, and only when the poor mangled corpse of Warrington had not a kick left in it did they withdraw. Now there is a vendetta in the air !</p>
        <p>Coming to football, it must be admitted that the cup-holders gave about the weakest and most inept exhibition of the game that ever disgraced the prim- rose and blue jersey. They might have had backbones of chewed string for all the resistance they offered to the marauding Rangers. The latter literally toyed with them, and before it could never have been admit- ted that there existed a team capable of doing such a thing at Wilderspool. Warrington were nearly at full strength, but play football they could not. In no single department of the game was their inferiority not markedly manifest. And yet the Rangers side was practically :the same that fell so heavily in April. Fortunately there is not much reason to fear that Warrington will give such a display again. It was altogether incomprehensible, and can only be put down to one of those remarkable fluctuations of form which are occasionally seen in all games.</p>
        <p>The--measure of Warrington's weakness was like- wise the measure of Broughton's strength. Probably the Rangers never played a better game or were more favoured by good fortune. They obtained the inestim- able advantage of a five points lead in decidedly lucky fashion and after that there was no holding them. Their combination was magnificent, and the complete understanding between halves .and centres could not but be marked by the most unintalligent observer. The two James's were given plenty of scope and they played tele Welsh game to perfection while in Wilson they had a man to back them up without an equal in the Northern Union. fie was positively great on Saturday and made the home backs look very small fry. No less than five tries were I scored and of these Wilson obtained three and had a hand in a fourth. It is impossible to withhhold any praise from the Rangers. On the day's play, they were superb from back to front.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men seemed to work hard but aimlessly. There was no scheme of attack and no plan of defence. Only a week ago I referred in flattering terms to the tackling of the home halves. On Saturday they were helpless against the two James's. And why ? Not because they were afraid to tackle, but because they failed to play on to the men with the ball. The only way to stop the clever Broughton halves, unless the scrummage is controlled is to be on them immediately the ball comes out. Once they have got the passing machinery going and the damage is done. Now. Warrington should not need telling this. They have known it for years and pre- viously they hase_che4tunted the Rangers without much difficulty. On Sattriadylhey never appeared to try until too late and the consequence was that the home backs were most of the time employed in a hopeless stern chase. There was a notable want of aggressiveness in Warrington's defensive methods,and this cannot often be urgent against them. 1 hope they learnt their lesson well.</p>
        <p>There was one good result of the free way in which the Rangers handled the ball—it afforded an object lesson in combination. I am not alone in the view that the Warrington halves rely too much on the"beat your man " theory. This is excellent in its place. But it is speedily proved ineffective when the halves try every time to rush away from the scrimmage and get through the first line of defence before passing. The variety of tactics employed by the two James's was very noticeable. At one time Willie would work the scrum while Sam stood behind to receive and get the threequarters going. Again they would both be close up and eithar go off individually to right or left, or pass from one to the other. Frequently, too, the pass would come right from the base of the scrum to the centres, who always seemed able to get away owing to the very quickness of the handling. When it came too beating a man S. James could always do it, but he didn't try every time ! Now Warrington hive an excellent couple of half-backs, both young enough to learn a simple lesson. One theory won't win matches. Variety's the very spice of the game. Let us see more throwing out to the centres, and an individual run only at the right moment. Keep the other side guess- ing. That's the point!</p>
        <p>It is hardly worth while singling any of the War- rington men out for either praise or blame, for scarcely one did well. Even Jack Hallam lost his head at times and was far from reliable. Like captain like men! The forwards were beaten at heeling-out and only Preston and Thomas seemed to play with the resolution which was called for. Even when there was a chance of scoring it went begging, and three fairly easy tries were missed. The absence of Fish was much deplored and the Warrington men will be glad when he has got Green of Wigan well beaten and is able to fill the left wing position with his usual brilliance.</p>
        <p>The" A " team showed a far more resolute front at Broughton than their seniors did at home, and the victory by 7 points to 2 was an excellent performance. Jordan scored the try and Knight placed a couple of goals. To-day they will have the battle of the season at Wilderspool, Wigan being the visitors. The War- rington lads will take the 4eld as follows : —Knight, full back ; Short, Marshman, Dickinson, and Hewitt, three-quarters ; J. Davies and. Garside, half hacks ; Elijah Lunt, Ernest Lunt, Forster, Ashton and Fair- hurst, forwards.</p>
        <p>The first team will visit Wigan with some mis- givings, for the Central Park contingent is one of the strongest in the League this season. Still, War- rington have only to display some of their cup form to stand a fair chance 'of honours. Fish will, of course, be an absentee, and another important change will be Jordan for Dickinson. The " A "team centre has been showing fine form this season, and being a player of the robust order he is having a chance in better company. The full team will be : Hallam, full back ; Harris, Jordan, Isher- wood and Kenyon, threequarters ; Hockenhall and Brooks, half-backs ; Boardman, Preston, Thomas, Jolley, Swift, Cook, Harmer and Belton, forwards.</p>
        <p>Particulars of the teams chosen for the Comity Trial match at Broughton on Wednesday next are given on page 6. Warrington have four men in the two teams.</p>
        <p>The most interesting match which has been played just lately was the one at Runcorn on Wednesday eveninz Most people would have anticipated that the Oldham men would have secured a somewhat easy triumph at the expense of the "Linnets" but not so the Runcornians. They could always give Oldham a : warm reception at Irwell-lane—one year they took the Championship from them—and on Wednesday they proved that falling fortunes have not 'robbed them of all their old-time prowess. Oldham could only manage to draw, and they were lucky to escapa defeat. Runcorn played their own peculiar game to perfection. The punting of Jolley and Co. was perfection, and the visiting hacks had a very parroty time. The</p>
        <p>ctics of the homesters were an object lesson 'to one or two Warrington players who witnessed the contest. ! Warrington's weakest point is punting and somehow they never seem inclined to remedy the evil.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows For. Agst. Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. P. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage Leeds Salford 3 3 0 0 6 411 41 3 1 9 100.00 Runcorn Bradford 3 2 0 1 5 5 5 25 2 1 7 83.33 Halifax 3 2 0 1 5 3 8 30 3 2 12 8313 Oldham 5 3 1 1 7 512 46 G 2 18 70.00 Warrington 3 2 1 0 4 3 1 9 2 5 19 66.66 Wigan 3 2 1 0 4 6 8 36 3 4 18 66•66 Hull K. R 3 2 1 0 4 2 7 25 2 4 16 6666 St. Helens 3 2 1 0 410 14 62 0 4 12 66.66 Dewsbury 3 2 1 0 4 1 2 8 1 3 11 6666 Bro'ton R 3 2 1 0 4 610 42 4 5 23 66•68 Hall 3 2 1 0 4 4 8 32 2 6 22 66•66 Keighley 3 2 1 0 4 710 44 3 2 12 66•66 York 3 2 1 0 4 5 4 22 2 3 13 66•66 Normanton 2 I. 1 0 2 1 0 2 4 7 29 50.00 Widnes 2 1 1 0 2 0 1 3 2 0 4 50.00 Bramley Swinton 3 1 2 0 2 1 0 2 2 3 13 3313 Hunslet Leigh Batley 3 0 2 1 1 3 1 9 4 G 28 16.66 Rochdale 3 0 2 1 1 0 1 3 2 5 19 16•66 Millom 3 0 2 1 1 1 2 8 512 46 16.66 Waklield T 3 0 2 1 1 2 0 4 3 4 18 16-66 Morecambe 3 0 3 0 0 2 4 17 7 16 62-100•00 Hudlield 2 0 2 0 0 1 0 2 2 4 16-100•00, Brighouse 2 0 2 0 0 0 0 0 1 1 5-10000 Castlefleld 2 0 2 0 0 1 0 2 5 4 22-100.00 Barrow 2 0 2 0 0 1 1 5 2 5 19-10000 Pontefract 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 0 1 1 5-100.00</p>
        <p>The points are two for a win and one for a draw. The percentage is calculated upon the basis of the total points possible.</p>
        <p>Bowling, Angling, Cycling. Owing to the great pressure on our space we hay e been compelled to omit notes on the above this week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Warrington P.hetographic Society.</head>
        <p>The Warrington P.hetographic Society.</p>
        <p>- . There was a good attendance of mem-bers and friends on Tuesday evening to witness a practical demonstration of postcard printing by Mr. K. F. Bishop, who succeeded in maintaining the attentive interest of all present by the easy, fascinating way in which he showed how to produce these popular every- day articles in an artistic style by various methods. Mr. Bishop was accorded a very hearty vote of thanks. Sympathetic reference was made to the serious accident that had befallen Mr. F. W. Purse (one of the Com- mittee), and on the proposition of the President, seconded by Mr. Haggett, a resolution of sympathy was passed to him.—Mr. C. J. Harrison, of Man- chester, was announced as the next lecturer, the subject being : " Enlarged negative making."</p>
        <p>Local Photographer's Success at "The Royal." The fiftieth annual exhibition of the Royal Photo- graphic Society was opened at the New Gallery, Regent-street,London,on Thursday.ln many respects a great advance is made in the generally high FlLAnclArd i of the photographic show. This year n the pictoral section no medals are awarded, mere acceptance thus becoming much more important, and indeed it is difficult to see how the judges would have been able to decide to whom medals should have been given this year had any been awarded, so high is the general standard. Lancashire exhibitors include Mr. Henry Bond, of Warrington (two) ; Mr. A. W. Cooper, Preston (two) ; Dr .k T. G. Crump, Burnley (two) ; Mr. Alfred Greenwood, Burnley ; Rev. W. Sidegreaves, Blackburn (two) ; and Mr. Percy P. Wilding, Preston. " Besses " Band at Warrington. This famous band paid a visit to Warrington on Monday and gave two performances in Parr Hall. Notwithstanding that they are fresh from their i successful tour n France and the cementing of the entetre corciale they received only a poor recognition in Warrington. The audiences were poor both after- noon and evening, which is a matter of surprise to us seeing that there is a good deal of local brass band rivalry. Apparently the Besses-o'-th'-Barn," though the greatest of brass bands, are not the draw one would have expected in Warrington. This is as much Warrington's loss as the " Besses," for they played splendidly under the conductorship of Mr. F. Berry., giving selections from light opera,musical comedy and the more classieal --meebsisi iu. finishAcl-styla._ AVe, thought they were quite at their best in " William Tell," " San Toy," " Jennesse Dore," and " Taunhauser," to take four items which differ very considerably and afford plenty of scope for the blar- ing of the brass and the softer cadences also. Messrs. R. H. Pearson's, Ltd, as usual, arranged for the visit of the world-famed band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>PROMOTING TEMPERANCE,</head>
        <p>PROMOTING TEMPERANCE,</p>
        <p>Sale of Work at Cairo-straet Schools. The recently inaugurated " Y " or Juvenile branch of the British Women's Temperance Association is in a flourishing condition and gives great promise of ably supporting the work of the parent body. For an active propagation of the principles funds are of course neces- sary, and with the object of placing the branch in a sounder financial position a sale of work was held in the Cairo-street schools on Thursday afternoon. The sale was opened by Mrs. James Fairclough in the presence of a largo gathering. Miss BESSIE Durrox presided and referred to the occasion as being a very auspicious one as they found so many young people working together for the good of the cause. She was sure there had been grett love in the hearts of those who had looked forward to advanc- ing the society which they had recently formed, and was glad to think they could in some small way assist that very laudible effort to help the poor folk— not poor in the sense of being short of money but who needed a helping hand. It must be a strong and perse- vering band if the help is to be of any avail. (Applause). Mrs. FArncLoueit in declaring the sale open spoke of the pleasure it afforded her to help forward so good a cause as they had as heart. She hoped the " Y " Branch would meet with that success which it deserved. (Applause.) Mrs. GARNETT proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Fairclough for her services. The new branch which had been started promised to become stronger than the British Women's Association. If they could get the young people to work they would probably do !nose i work n Warrington than the older body had been able to accomplish. They had done a little, but not nearly so much as they would like to do. They heard with joy that temperance was advancing throughout the length and breadth of the land and their efforts, though small, had no doubt contributed something towards that end. (Applause.) She hoped the "Y "Branch would flourish exceedingly and would continue to work to make Warrington beautiful. Planting trees was a good thing but moral reformation was necessary to back up the efforts in that direction. They must all work for the beautiful and work for temperance was in that direc- tion. They must remember however,that prevention was better than reclamation andlmust strive after prevention while they sought to reclaim. (Applause.) Mrs. ARMITAGE seconded and the motion was heartily carried. In responding MTS. FAIRCLOCTGH moved a vote of thanks to Miss Dutton for her services in the chair. Mrs. JAMES DODDS seconded and the vote was heartily accorded. Miss Durro:s having briefly returned thanks the sales commenced. The stalls which contained a variety of useful and ornamental articles artistically arranged and were in charge of the following ladies :—Fancy stall, Miss Fieldhouse,Miss Walker, Miss M. Dodds, Miss Atkinson and Miss Gill; advertisement stall, Miss F. Beswick, Miss Short, Pemberton, Miss Walsh and Miss Norbury ; refreshment stall, Miss Mellor, Miss Rudd,lifrs. Tanner, Mrs. Shaw,Miss Jackson, Miss Spencer, Miss Beswick, Miss F.ll3eswiek and Miss A. Beswick. At intervals during the afternoon and evening songs, instrumental selections. &amp;c., were rendered by the fol- lowing ladies and gentlemen : Mrs. Holmes, Mrs, A. Willett, Miss Mabel Fairclough. Miss D. Fairclough, Miss Cox, Miss A. Beswick, Miss E. Dunn, Mr. A. Griffiths. Mr H. M. Ashton and Master T. Tanner. The sale waslin every way a success,the sum of £22 10s. clear of expenses being realized.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BAPTIST CHAPEL AUTUMNAL ANNIVERSARY.—The annual services were held in the above place on Sunday last. In the morning there was a young people's service which was well attended. Suitable hymns were sung and the preacher for the day, the Rev. A. J. W. Back of Lymm, delivered the address from the text "For God : so loved the world." Mr. James Strange officiated at the organ. In the afternoon the chapel was well filled. and Mr. Back delivered a sermon on the deliverance of Jeremiah. The choir sang an anthem. Tea was pro- vided in the adjoining room for visitors. In the evening the chapel was filled, Mr. Back again occupied the pulpit and took for his subject" God is a spirit." The choir rendered two anthems very ably, Nir. W. Hallos officiating at the organ. The collections for the day were good, being in advance of last year.</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</p>
        <p>MRS. BALLTN, THE GREAT AUTHORITY ON CHILDREN'S DISEASES, STRONGLY RECOMMENDS. VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. Mrs. ADA S. BALLIN, 5, Agar street, London,Editor of "Womanhood," writes :—" VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is an exceedingly successful remedy. It is very pleasant to take, and the relief it gives is very rapid. The preparation is perfectly safe for children. Mrs. HAGUE, Radcliffe Crescent, Lockwood, near Huddersfield, writes :—" My little boy suffered from hoarseness and bronchitis. We all thought he never would be cured or speak plain again. After taking VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGE/ CURE he was able to speak plainly. I have never seen anything to act so quickly." Ask for VEZ/O'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE at Chemists and Drug Stores. Price 91d., Is. 13d., and 2s. 9. Sold everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHILD'S DEATH. The Mother in Gaol.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHILD'S DEATH. The Mother in Gaol.</p>
        <p>The circumstances attending the death of Charles Henry Williams, the seven-weeks-old child of Samuel and Charlotte Williams, 18, Duttox-street, formed the subject of an inquiry which was opened at the Warring- ton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon by Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner. Mr. Luke Talbot (chief constable) was present on behalf of the police and Inspector Cox represented the N.S.P.C.C. The Coroner explained that the child was admitted into the Workhouse hospital on the previous Thursday suffering from parental neglect, was attended by Drs. Braide and Peacock, and died on Saturday afternoon at half-past four o'clock. The rolice reported that the case was brought to their notice ten days ago by the sanitary authorities stating that it required immediate attention. Dr. Smith made an examination of the child on behalf of the police and the case was then placed in the hands of the National Society or the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. A warrant was obtained for the arrest of the mother, and she was charged before the borough magistrates on Thursday with neglect, and sentenced to six months' imprison- ment. The child the same day was brought to the work- house, where it died on Saturday. A post-mortem was made by Drs. Peacock and Smith, and it now became necessary to inquire into all the circumstances to see if there had been any neglect on the part of the mother. They (the jury) had to decide that point, and they must not be prejudiced by the fact that the woman received a sentence of imprisonment. They had to begin de novo and decide if there had been negligence which was in their opinion of such a degree as to justify them in send- ing the mother for trial for manslaughter. The mother was at present in prison, and he (the Coroner) had decided to adjourn the inquiry so as to enable her to be present if she desired. Evidence of identification was given, and the inquest was adjourned until the ficth inst.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON FREE CHURCH COUNCIL. Mr. G. E. Parker and Chinese Labour. Under the auspices of the Warrington Free Church Council an enjoyable conversa.zione was held in the King's 0114 on Thursday evening. Mr. C. E. Parker presided over a largo gathering. In a brief introductory speech the CHAIRMAN said he desired to be allowed to speak on a subject which had weighed heavily upon his heart and conscience. Some 15 months ago at a meeting couveaed by the Free Churches of Warrington the Executive Committee put in his hand a res..lation which they asked him to move. At that time the conscience of the country had been shocked by the intelligence that the British Govern- ruent had acceded to the request of the mine owners and had decided to allow tad encourage the introduction of Indentured Chinese labour into South Africa. The ordinance in detail contained all the essence of slavery. The Free Church Council presented a resolution, expressing their indignation that such a thing could be introduced into British territory. This was unanimously endorsed. There might be some who objected to his introducing that subject on the ground that the question was a political one. It was more than a politi- cal one. (Hear, hear.) It was a question which deeply affected the honour of England, it was a question more of righteousness than politics. It mattered little to them whether the experiment was a commercial success or not they believed the proposal was wrong. Fifteen months had passed since that time and the wrong was already bearing its bitter fruit. They were already beginning to reap the harvest of what was then planted. The Chinese were refusing to do the work required of them and flogging and torture were being introduced into the mines to force the men to work. The consequence was that the coolies were breaking their bounds and the people of Transvaal had to carry arms to use if necessary upon the coolies. A state of terrorism was being created in Transvaal which would result in bloodshed and outrage. He mentioned that platter because as E.:I4IIShMeD, RS men believing in righteousness they must resolve to do all that they could to see that on th 3 first possible opportunity the evil stein upon the flag of Eng'aid was erased. (Appleuse). They must never rest until the wrong which had been perpetrated had been righted. (Applause.) The Rev. ADAM SCOTT, president of the Sonth-West Lancashire Federation of Free Caurch. Councils said it appeared to him that the Warrington Coilncil was in a vigorous and healthy condition. In one district near Warrington they had a very handsome church and in addition almost every phase of Nonconformity was represented with the result that there was a great deal of overlapping. In that district they had villedom with two or three exceptions going to the Parish Church and no sooner did they get a Nonconformist in the church but they had him in the front amongst the most active workers. It appeared to him that they would accomplish their object very much better if they had against that Church one large Church of their own. Pe- rhaps for many years to come they would not be able to do that but as Free Churchmen they must stop that sort of overlapping and remove churches from districts where there was overlapping to districts whore they wore wanted. As F. no Churchmen they must concern them- selves about the educational condition of the country. The education problem had its difficulties. He was a Scotsman and it was a mistake for English people to say that in Scotland they had no sectarian education. In the national schools the shorter catechism was taught. The people, however, had popular control and in the second place so far as the teaching staff was con- cerned they had no sectarian tests. (Applause.) In those two thing they had the irreducable minimum of what they must demand at the next election. They must have popular control and all sectarian tests must be swept away. (Applause.) He was delighted to listen to the Chairman's remarks regarding Chinese labour. They must stand shoulder to shoulder as one man to lift the moral tone of the country. (Applause.) The ltev, Torn: YONGE remarked that they had heard from time to time what was taking place in other parts of the country with deep thankfulness, and he hoped that they in Warrington during the coming winter were going to see greater things than they had seen in recent years. There was abundant need for it. He hoped the Churches would be filled with new enthusiasm, and that they would see great and glorious things in Warrington during the next six months. (Applause.) He proposed a vote of thanks to those responsible for the arrange- ments for the gathering. (Applause.) The Rev. J. F. NfaerirEws seconded. He suggested the drawing up of a Free Church calendar, upon which the dates of the non-moveable festivals should be placed, so that they could avoid a clashing in dates. He con- cluded by an appeal on behalf of the Council for greater financial assistance. The vote was heartily accorded. During the evening an enjoyable musical programme was submitted, to which contributions were made by Miss Fairhurst, Miss Woodland, Mr. W. Ford, and Master Moores.—Mr. F. Podmore acted as accompanist. land was aleo responsible for the arranging of the musical programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
