      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Mr. Balfour and the Volunteers.</head>
        <p>Mr. Balfour and the Volunteers.</p>
        <p>- - -------- - Sir,—l have just read your excellent leader on Mr. Balfour's speech and hasten to write you a line of con- gratulation in time to catch you before going to press. Our Prime Minister, as the " Afoming Post " pat it, successfully narked time at North Berwick, which operation consists in going through the motions of a movement without advancing or retiring. It is, how- ever, a poor policy to criticise without offering a remedy and as Lord Roberts has told us what he con- sidered the only right aryl safe course to pursue I should be glad to know how far you and your readers are pre- pared to accept his views as given in his speech at the Mansion House.—l aru, GEORGE CROSFIELD. September 21st.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>What Will the Voter Do?</head>
        <p>What Will the Voter Do?</p>
        <p>Sir,—The late Mr. John Cr-isfield, father of the future Member of Parliament for Warrington (if the electorate of Warrington are sane enough to vote for justice, equality and freedom) used to say it passed his com- prehension how any ‘vorkingman could be found to vote Tory. Truly a Conservative working man is some- thing of a curiosity! I always put it down that if a workingman votes Tory he either is ignorant of the history of his country and polities, or he has been caught and deluded by the clap-trap of his professedly Conservative friends. But surely now after hearing the splendid orations of Mr. Outhwaite and the proofs he produced of his statements at P,rr Hall and Co-opera- tive Hall on the; Chinese Question in South Africa no such cu ri osity as aConservative workingman will be found any longer in Warrington. Can not workingmen see that if it is right to import Chinese and plaCe them in compounds in South Africa, it would be quite right to import Chinese and place them in compounds in Eng- land—what is right in one country must of course be right in another? And are not the working men aware that if they remain quiet and take it "lying down" that there is a danger of the same system being introduced in England ? In fact there are plenty of men to-day who for the sake of dividends and profits if they dared and had the chance would import Chinese to-morrow. Now the question is, what are the workingmen in Warrington going to do at the next General Election. Are they going to be foolish enough to again vote for a Government that has by its policy increased the cost of their living by 3s. in every pound : and that went to war against the Boers for the sake of justice and freedom and in order to open up the country for the white man but instead has betrayed the country by introducing a system of slavery and the yellow man? Surely not—for if the men of Warring- ton are like'the men of other towns where elections have recently taken place instead of returning a "member for Cyprus "they will return a member for Warrington —Arthur Crosfleld, a gallant gentleman and an able politican to boot.—Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>FREE CRITIC.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>GREAT IRISH DEMONSTRATION. Mr. Joseph Devlin. M.P., to Visit Warrington.</head>
        <p>GREAT IRISH DEMONSTRATION. Mr. Joseph Devlin. M.P., to Visit Warrington.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warringtou Branch of the United Irish League have arranged to hold a demon- stration in the Parr Hall next Thursday, at which a prominent member of the Irish Parliament Party will make an important pronouncement re the present political situation. The meeting will be specially interesting as the promoters Fr.ve succeeded in arrang- ing with Mr. Joseph Devlin, 3/.P., to deliver an address. Mr. Devlin has recently been touting Australia and the United States and in the States has addressed some of the largest meetings ever held. A fluent and powerful speaker, Warring- ton is fortunate in securing his services as, since his return to this country, despite pressing requests he has repeatedly declined to appear on the public plat- form, Other speakers will include Mr. Thomas O'Dannel M.P., member for West Kerr!, Councillor Austin Harford, Liverpml, Mr. P. J. Farley, (8.C.) Manchester, and Mr. W. J. Lotwhley, organizer for Lancashire, Cheshire and North Wales. Cauncillor Magical will preside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING.</p>
        <p>--- The quarterly meeting was held at Padgrtte on Thurs- day evening. Tea was served by the Padgate friends in the school after which a meeting was held in the Church. The Rev.S. Pitt presided and there were present: The Revs. IV. Mellor, J. Manning, C. Parker, .T. Gunnell and J. Howard ; the Circuit stewards, Messrs. Within- shaw and Upson, the Secretary Mr. W. Siloock, and 70 representatives. A very cordial greeting was given to the three newly-appointed ministers. The numerical statement showed si slight decrease and the finances a deficit owing to the recent heavy expenditure. A scheme to remove the debt by voluntary contributions was proposed by the Circuit Stewards and adopted. A committee was appointed to carry it out. A vote of condolence and sympathy with the widow and family of the late Mr. R. G. Henshall was proposed by the Circuit Stewards. Many of the representatives present spoke of the worth, ability and genialit, of Mr. Henshall and of the immense services he had rendered to the circuit. The vote was carried in silence, all the representatives standing. Mr. W. Silcock was appointed Treasurer of the Foreign Mission Funds, and Mr. W. Hickman treasurer to the Circuit Travelling Fund. Both offices had been held by Mr. Henshall. The Rev. W. Mellor was ap- pointed secretary and Mr. G. Weaver jun., assistant secretary to the Missionary Society and arrangements were made and announced for the autumn and winter meetings. Mr. .LE. Richardson wasappointed treastwer for the Supernumerary Ministers Fund. The unanimity and earnestness, the determination of both ministers and laymen to do aggressive work augur well for the Methodist year which tuts just commenced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WHAT TELLS IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WHAT TELLS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>We may read column after column of testimony from some town a hundred miles away, but it can never have the weight with us that one little paragraph from is Warrington man or woman will It is local testimony that tells in Warrington. and that is the kind of proof we always give. Read this Warrington woman's hearty, grateful words. Mrs. Cecilia J. Rime, of 16, King-street, Warrington says : " I never used to have any faith in pills at all because nearly all of them professed to cure every malady, and T was not going to believe such a thing was possible. I had been a victim to kidney trouble myself, having suffered agonies from lumbago and rheumatism. The pains in my back used to be simply excruciating, and at nights I used to toss from side to side in my bed unable to sleep, because I couldn't rest Imy back. I never seemed free from terrible headaches, and was always drowsy and depressed. The slightest. cold seemed to fly to my tidneys and aggravate the trouble. There were other unmistakable signs of kidney sickness. "When my husband told me that there was some special medicine for backaches and kidney disorder, called Doan's Backache Rid aey Pills, my prejudice against these pills ava.a at once overcome, because the very name of this medicine showed that it didn't claim to cure anything but kidney troubles. I got some of these pills and I am very glad I did so, for they did ma so much good. Indeed, I may say I arc in better health since using them than I was for years before. I always take a pleasure in recommending Doan's Pills, and always shall, because I have proved them to bo so genuine.—(Signed) Cecilia J. Rnaue." Doan's backache kidney pills are two shillings and nine-pence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence.) Of all chemists and stores, or post free, on receipt of price, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Be sure you get the same kind of pills as Mrs. Ruane had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WEEK ENDING SEPTEMBER 16th, 1005. Daring the week, the following births and deaths were registered:—Warrington Registration District. births, 48, deaths, 27. The excess of births over deaths as 21.</p>
        <p>The birth-rate was equal to 36•0 The death-rate from all causes was equal to 23.3 per 1,000 againstll7•6 theavera,ge of the previous 4 weeksand against 251 the corresponding period of last year. The deatliTate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 5•2 per 1,000, against 5•7 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks and against 91 the corresponding period of 1901. The ages of mortality were:- 0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65—up. Total S 5 1 2 8 3 27. The deaths included-1 faom scarlet fever, 6 from diarrhoea, 3 from phthisis, 2from cancer, 2 from bron- chitis, 2 from pneumonia, 11 from all Other causes. The following cases of infectious diseases have been reported during the week : Total number of patients in hospital, week ending 16th September, 1905, 32, ewton 22,.1tural District 2, Borough 8 J. GUEST GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
