      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>31o8Day.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Wilkinson and Messrs. S. Wright, William Roberts, and H. Thornton. BEG nlNG.—William Ashton, of no settled abode, NM sent to goal for one month with hard labour, for begging on Saturday night.—Detective Clough said he saw the prisoner who was under the influence of drink accost about a dozen men and ask them for money. When arrested prisoner became violent and witness had to obtain the assistance of P.C. Woodland.—The Chief Constable described the prisoner as an habitual vagrant. DRUNK IN CllARGE.—Richard and Henry Jones, 14, Nicholson-street, were charged with being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and trap on Saturday morning.— P.C. Cooper stated that at about 2 o'clock on Saturday morning he was in Horsemarket-street when be saw a horse and cab come along without a driver. He stopped the vehicle and questioning the occupant found that the prisoner had been drunk and had fallen off the cab. Witness drove the cab back to Longford Bridge where he found the prisoner and a professional gentleman on the ground injured. He put them in the cab and drove to the Infirmary where their injuries were dressed. It transpired that when the prisoner fell off the cab the professional gentleman jumped out of the vehicle and in doing so severely injured himself.—Prisoner was fined Is. and costs. SCENE IN DIAL COCRT.—Michael Fitzsimmons, John Burke,Eliza Burke and Bridget O'brien all of Dial Court were summoned for disorderly conduct in Dial Court. It appears that the four were creating a great disturb- ance the two women by fighting.—Bridget O'Brien, who was the only one present in court was tined ss. The other three were fined ss. and costs. STONE THROWING NUISANCE.—Matthew Rogerson (12), 34. Gee-street, Edwin Martin, (12), 2, Erwood street, John Wright (14), 26, Erwood-street, and Francis (15) and John Ford, (14), 18, Portland-street, were each fined 2s. 6d. for throwing stones in the street. BEGGING THE MAGISTRATE'S PARDON.—James Hopkins, a Warrington character, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Winwie:-street. Prisoner was in company with two other men accosting people for money, and created a disturbance at the Central Station.—The Mayor: One month's imprison- ment.—Prisoner : Beg pardon, sir, but the other men only got seven days. (Laughter).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>inspector Rowbotham's Retirement.</head>
        <p>inspector Rowbotham's Retirement.</p>
        <p>Inspector Rowbotham, of the Warrington Police Force, has signified his intention of retiring from his duties, the retirement to take effect on the 12th inst. Inspector Rowbotham has been connected with the Warrington force for 26 years and has earned the high esteem of his brother officers who will join in wishing him well in the rest which he is to take. Having served two years and eight months in the Cheshire Constabulary, he joined the borough force on the 31st July. 1870; was made sergeant on September 7th, 1882; and inspector, September Ist, 1898, being raised to the rank of first class in 1903. He is entitled to a pension of :£Bl per annum. He will be succeeded by Sergeant Smith who, having served four years and four months in the Cheshire Constabulary, joined the Warrington force on the 23rd November, 1882 ; was made sergeant January 7th, 1892 ; and first-class sergeant January 9th, 1896. Sergeant Smith will be succeeded by P.C. Cooper, who joined the force on July 7th, 1887 ; was promoted to first-class constable September 12th, 1889 ; and obtained the second-class merit badge on March 21st, 1901. Both promotions, which will be popular with the men generally, will take effect from the 13th October.</p>
        <p>At the Revision Court for the Scarborough district of the Whitby Division on Wednesday in the list of voters for the Throxenbury district, the name of a man who had been dead ten years came up. Sir William Stephens. Mayor of Salford, was pre- sented on Wednesday evening by the members of the Hope Chapel congregation with a silver reading lamp. Sir William stated that it was the first present he had accepted since he commenced public service.</p>
        <p>For careworn, hard-pressed mothers of children what</p>
        <p>a boon! What a real boon . not to need to drudge on in the old way of washing clothes and cleaning house. Through the long, long list of matters of household and</p>
        <p>cleaning renovating, Fels-Naptha soap stands far away above all other helps for ease, quickness and thoroughness.</p>
        <p>Consider what a difference to the tired mother where able to save about half the mere manual force • required by the old way of washing clothes, as well as about half the time</p>
        <p>in doing it. Clothes come out whiter, sweeter and nicer.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha ap Wilson street London EC</p>
      </div>
