      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>"Do you manage your dog by love or fear, my dear?" a little one was asked. "Neither," was the answer, "I give him bones." AMBIGITOUS.—Late Arrival at Dinner: "I am afraid I am too late, dear Mrs. Smith." Mrs. Smith (effusively) : "Oh, dear, you could never come too late." "You want a flogging—that's what you want," said a parent to an unruly son. "I know it, dad, but ni try to get along without," said the independent hopeful. AN awkward sentence to spell :—"lt is agree- able to witness the unparalleled ecstasy of two harassed peddlers embarrassed in endeavouring to gauge the symmetry of two peeled pears." 1. HE MAIN POINT.--Irilia : "Father, Gaston says he loves me madly." Wise Father (ponder- ously) : "Oh, all young men say that." Irma : "Not to me, father." . _ „..</p>
        <p>_ "PROSPERITY has ruined many a man." "No doubt; but if I am given any choice in the matter, I'd rather be ruined by prosperity than by adversity. The process is more enjoyable." REASSURED.—TearfuI Wife: "Oh, doctor, Benjamin seems to be wandering in his Mind." Doctor (who knows Benjamin): 'Don't trouble about that ; he can't go far." SUFFICIENT.—"You say you never gossip?" said her friend with a slight air of incredulity. "Never," answered Miss Sharpe. "When I feel disposed to hear my neighbours discussed I merely mention a name and proceed to listen." GUY CHI:HUMOR : "I feel as if I were throw- ing myself away on that girl. Our house is one of the greatest in England." Dearborn Street : "Well, her father's pricking house is one of th 2 biggest in Bridgeport." HARRY : "Blanche says she has insuperable reasons for remaining single." Horace : "Yes, I know what they are." Harry: "Then she has told you?" Horace : "No; but I have seeil her." . _</p>
        <p>-7:YES, it's twins," growled Nupop. "What is that old saying? Misfortunes never come singly.' isn't it?" "Yes," replied Batcheller, gleefully, "or, better still, Misfortunes never come single.'" As COMPARED.—A: "Well, what's the differ- ence between a poet and a rhymester?" B: "A rhymester is one who talks proudly of the poems he has written; a poet is one who speaks modestly of his rhymes." SHE: "There isn't one man in a million who would be so mean to his wife and children as you are." He: "Now, that is what I admire in you, my dear; you have such a head for figures." A LAD took up to his teacher a piece of com- position containing the word "fizzic." "Here," exclaimed the teacher, "f-i-z-z-i-e doesn't spell physic." "Doesn't it?" answered the youth ; "what does it spell, sir?" The teacher gave it up. LADY: "Beware, little boy, of picking a toad- stool instead of a mushroom. They are easy to confuse." Boy: "That's all roight, miss. Us a-gob' to eat 'ern oorselves ; th'ey're a-goin' to market to be sold." _</p>
        <p>HOST: "Don't you think my little boy has a fine voice, Mr. A., and that I ought to send him to Italy to have it cultivated?" Mr. A.: "Yes : if I were you I would certainly send him at least as far as that." _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ A SCHOOLMASTER who had been telling of the doings of Ctesar ended up with, "And all this happened over 1,500 years ago." A little cherub, his blue eyes wide open wit:, wonder, said, after a moment's thought, 'Oh, what a memory you've got !" "TELL me the different things found on the coast," said a teacher, giving a lesson on a map. "Capes," said one little girl. "Quite right." replied the teacher. "Now give me something else." Then another child quickly raised her hand and cried, "Jackets !" VISITOR: "I don't believe that Miss Clacker is a true friend to you. She wanted me to say something against you last night." Lady : "And what was that?" Visitor : "She tried to press me for my real' opinion about you—but not I!"</p>
        <p>MRS. SHORT (to new servant): "I ant pleased to say I have always been fortunate enough to get honest girls, and have never had any cloth- mg stolen.' Bridget : "Shure, an' it is not very big you are—is it, mum?" ON THE CONTRARY.—Gentleman (to house agent): "The great disadvantage is that the house is so damp." House Agent: "Disadvan- tage, sir? Advantage, I call it. In case of fire it wouldn't be so likely to burn." SCHOOL TEACHER: 'What little boy can tell me where is the home of the swallow?" Bobby: "I kin. please." School Teacher: "Well, Bobby?" Bobby: "The home of the swallow is the stummick."</p>
        <p>HAWKINS : "Someone told me that your Uncle John had struck natural gas. Is it true !" Simson : "That's what. FTe married a widow last week who had talked three husbands into the cemetery. and she's still on the sunny side of forty." "I SHOULD like two weeks' absence, if you please, sir, to attend the wedding of a very dear friend of mine." "It must be a very dear friend indeed to make you want so much time. Who is it?" "Well, sir, after the ceremony she will be my wife." STERN FATHKR : "So you want to marry my daughter, eh?" Young Man: "I do." Stern Father: "What's your salary?" Young Man: "Oh, I'm not particular. Just give me a trial for three months, and if I fail to give satisfaction as a son-in-law you needn't pay me any salary." HE: "What a daring bicyclist that Miss Dash- ley must be ! I hear that when out riding the other day she went right over a cow lying in the road." "Yes. Miss Dashley went over, but the bicycle remained on the other side."</p>
        <p>AT a public school examination an eccentric examiner asked, "What views would King Allied take of universal suffrage, the conscription, and printed books if he were living now?" An in- genious pupil wrote in reply: "If King Alfred were still alive, he would be too old to take any interest in anything." "WHAT makes you so late? " asked his mother. "The teacher kept me in because I couldn't find Moscow on the map of Europe," replied Johnnie. Mother: "And no wonder you couldn't find Moscow. It was burned down in 1812. It's an outrage to treat a child that way." A TEACHER in a certain town (we considerately decline to be more specific) had a great deal of trouble to make a boy in his class understand a point in his lesson. Finally, however, he suc- ceeded, and, drawing a long breath, remarked: "If it wasn't for me. you would be the greatest donkey in this town." MOTHER: "Now, Georgie, I shall tell your papa to punish you severely for telling an un- truth. You said you didn't touch one of those six peaches, and there is only one left, and I found the five stones in your nursery." Georgie : "I told no story, mamma. The peach I didn't touch is the one that's left !" _</p>
        <p>HIS PRESENCE NECESSARY.--" SUpOOSHIg I decide to let you have the money, bow do I know that I shall get it back at the time you mention?" asked Brown. "I promise it, my boy, on the word of a gentleman," replied Moore. "Ah in that ease I may think better of it. Come round this evening and bring him with you." A YOUTH, in return for help in making a kite, had promised to buy his sister some ribbon for a bow. They went to the shop. Priti the ribl.wo was chosen from the window. Entering, the price was 'asked. "Is. 61. a yard," was the answer. "Oh, never mind, cut her a penn'ortb :" exclaimed the generous youth. Tim teacher had been .explaining the meaning of the well-known proverb. "A burnt child dreads the fire," and at the end of his discourse asked the class to mention a proverb formed on the same principle. After a lengthy pause little Jacob held up a grimy little paw. Teacher: "Well, Jacob?" Jacob (joyfully) : "Please, sir, 'A washed child dreads the water. ' _</p>
        <p>.... A CARETAKER ha a school threw up his job the other day. When asked what was the trouble, he Answered: "Why, a little while ago, I saw written on the board. • Find the common multiple.' Well, I looked from cellar to garret, and I wouldn't know the thing if I met it in the street. Last night. in big writin' on the black- board it said, 'Find the greatest ,common divisor.' Well, I says to myself, both there things are lost now, and I'll get blamed for 'inching 'em, so I'll leave,"</p>
        <p>"A MOVING TALE." An attractive and novel showcard is being exhibited. for the first time, in connection with the Bovril Stand at the Grocers' Exhibition, Agricultural Hall, London. It is called the Viograph," and is an ingenious arrangement whereby the idea of Bovril concentration is cleverly worked out. It is simply a cup of Bovril with endless bulls in ghostly form appearing among the steam. Needless to say, this novelty, which is the invention of Messrs. H. Whitworth Hird, Ltd.. Norwich and London, has drawn large crowds to the Bovril Exhibit during the past few days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday September 23, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday September 23, 1905.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>MR. MAY PROTESTS AGAINST SECRECY.</p>
        <p>The Employment of Married Women</p>
        <p>as Teachers.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday even- ing, Alderman Bennett presidin,.7. There were also present :—Alderman Pierpoint (Vice-chairman), the Mayor (Alderman Bolton), the Revs. Canon Morley Stevenson and Father Wilson, Aldermen Smethw•st and Tinnion, Councillors Wilkinson, Sankey, Craik, Fulton Smith, Brough, Evans, Hulse, Lord, Barton, Wright, Maginn, Messrs. J. D. Buckton,A. J. Jolley, C. E. Parker C. H. Unsworth, T. May, J. Upson, E. Plinston, R. T. Fairclongh, and J. Moore Murray (director of educa- tion).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MR. MAY AND SECRECY.</head>
        <p>MR. MAY AND SECRECY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance Committee notifications from the Board of Education of the payment of instal- ments of annual and fee grants in respect of elementary schools, amounting to £1,252 (fee grant) and £626 (annual grant) were submitted.—The appointments of William Thomas Eccles as office boy in the stores department and Miss F. Perdue and Mrs. A. Barber as cleaners at the Technical Institute were recommended. Mr. JOLLEY moved and Councillor Wilkinson seconded the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY pointed out that no comparative statement of the grants received last year was submitted. It would be very useful and instructive to have a compare of that kind. He had always asked for it, and he under- stood that at a meeting from which he was absent, they had a quarterly compare which was not, however, printed in the minutes. It seemed to him to be part of a general policy to keep things dark when such import- ant compares were suppressed and not printed on the minutes of the committee.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: I don't think you ought to make that charge. There is no such policy at ell and no such desire. Mr. MAY : I would like to have some reason for suppressing such important information. Through my absence from the meeting I am entirely ignorant as to whether there was an increase or decrease on the ques- tion specified,and that equally applies to all the mem- bers who were not present on that occasion. It also applies to the general public and the Press, and it is most important information.j The CHAIRMAN: I understand the grants are purely statements on account and it would not be possible to prepare comparative statements that would be of any service. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY : It is distinctly stated that the grants are instalments. .</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON : They are, therefore, exactly the same as they were this time last year and if you gave a com- parative statement you would have to print the same figures in parallel columns. The difference is rectified at the end of the year. The minutes were confirmed. SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMIrrEE.</p>
        <p>THE GENERAL COMMITTEE"SIMPLY CYPHERS." The School Management Committee's minutes' which were formally moved by the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson and seconded by Councillor Fulton Smith, contained the intimation that a report from the Board of Education upon the work done at the Warrington British Evening Continuation school during the last session, and the following statement of grants received in respect thereof, was submitted and noted, viz : Literary and commercial, £6l Is. 6d. ; occupations and industries, £2?. 18s. 6d.; cookery, £lO ; total, £94. Councillor LORD asked whether the report was good, bad or indifferent. The committee, he thought, ought to have some information on that point. The CH AIRMAN : It is satisfactory I understand. Alderman TINNION said he thought Councillor Lord's objection' if such it could be called, was a very proper one. He had come to the conclusion that there was really only one committee on that Education Authority, and that was the School Management Committee. The rest were simply cyphers that confirmed or otherwise the committee's minutes. He was not saying •that in any carping spirit but it was a fact that for the carrying out of almost the whole of the business of the machinery of the Education Authority in Warrington the powers were delegated to the School Management Committee. Those present were not all members of that sub-com- mitee, in fact there were not as many members of the Town Council on the committee as there would be this time next year. Therefore he thought the general committee should have a little information as the School Management Committee was by far the most important committee they had. He could quite under- stand that the various members of that committee were in touch with the work but on the minutes the nature of the reports received from the Board of Education was not stated and the Education Committee appeared to be merely registering the work done by the sub-committee. ..... . .</p>
        <p>THII GRAMMAR SCHOOL. At a meeting of the School Management Committee, a letter was submitted from the Board of Education for- warding a draft of the proposed order and scheme amending the existing scheme for the government of the Warrington Grammar School, and it was resolved that a sub-committee, consisting of Mr. J. D. Buckton, Mr. A. J. Jolley, Mr. Councillor J. Fulton Smith, and the Chairman of the Committee (the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson) be appointed to consider the same. Mr. MAY said the scheme was a most important one and ought, he contended, to have been submitted to the General Committee, instead of being left in the hands of the three gentlemen named. He did not wish to reflect on any of those gentlemen—in whom he had full confideace—bnt as a matter of information to himself, he would very much like to know what the scheme was, and he submitted that other members were in the same position. He moved that the resolution be referred back for the addition of a printed copy of the report. The CHAIRMAN : I think that is premature. The recommendation is purely preliminary. The sub-com- mittee will have to report to the School Management Committee, and that committee in turn to the full com- mittee. In due course the exact scheme will come before the Education Committee as a whole, and it would be premature to discuss it now. THE EMPLOYMENT OF MARRIED WOMEN. THE WORE OF THE SECONDARY SCHOOL. . .</p>
        <p>The following taachers were appointed by the School Management Committee : School. Name. Position. Salary per annum.</p>
        <p>2. s. d. St. Mary's Miss K. Burke, Uncert 42 10 0 Latchford assistant, St. Peter's, Mrs. I. Price, Cert. 85 .0 0 assistant, (supply) Trinity Mrs. Foster Supprtry 35 0 0 National assistant, (supply), Warrington Mr. W. A. Head 250 0 0 Parocial, Brown, master, do. Mrs. J. Cert. 90 0 0 Daintith, assistant, (supply), do. Mrs. Singleton, Head 115 0 0 mistress, Wycliffe Mrs. E. Cert. 85 0 0 British, Humphries, assistant, (supply). At a meeting of the same committee the Head Master of the Secondary school and Technical Institute presented his report upon the work of the school during the past term. Mr. PLINSTON said be noticed a large number of married woman bad been selected as teachers in the schools. He had no objection to ; the appointment of widows, so long as they were qualified. No doubt the chairman would he able to give a satisfactory reason for the appointment of other married women but he (Mr. Plinston) had noticed that numerous appointments of thatl kind bad been made for some time, and he was not in agreement with them.He would like to know some- thing about the reportionthe Secondary school. There had been complaints by one or two members of the committee with reference to the School NlanagementCommittee.He , thought the fault lay somewhat in the mover of the [ minutes sometimes overlooking the fact that there I were little matters which the General Committee might I think were important and on which information should be given. If explanations were offered at the outset there would not be so many resolutions to refer I back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MR. UPSON'S PROTEST.</head>
        <p>MR. UPSON'S PROTEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON said that as a member of the School Management Committee, and he was sure he was speak- ing in the name of the other members, he thought a protest ought to be made against the constant attacks on that committee. It seemed to him that if Alder- man Tinnion would compare the minutes of that com- mittee with those of any committee of the Town Council he would find that all their proceedings were most elaborately set out and that they gave an immense amount of information which the other committees did not give. There were large spending committees of the Council whose minutes were composed sometimes within the compass of a few sentences. The School Management Committee's ran of ten to half a dozen pages or more. He did not think any business came before the Committee that did not find its way on to the minutes. Seeing the amount of time the members of the Committee put into their duties, and the vast subsidiary work they had to do as visitors of the Evening Continua- tion Schools, Secondary School, and School of Art and on the various sub-committees, the work they did ought not to be so constantly , attacked by members who seemed to come to the meet- 1 ing merely to air their eloquence when the representa- tives of the Press were present. As a member of the School Management Committee 119 wanted to protest as 1 strongly as he could against the insinuation that the committee attempted to conceal anything they did or that they were not giving the fullest possible informa- tion in the minutes to all the members of the committee With reference to what Mr. May!had:said the committee</p>
        <p>were charged with having a very large printing bill, as it was. If the Grammar School scheme had been printed the committee would have had a small volume for their consideration, and if he knew anything of the members of the committee, the majority of them would have come to that meeting without having read either of the reports mentioned, seized upon some sentence or resolution in them,and got up and, to put it vulgarly, " slanged " the School Management Com- mittee up hill and down dale, lie protested strongly against the constant attacks made on the committee. Alderman TINNION said he made no attack on the com-mittee. He simply said he had come to the conclusion that the most important work of the authority was delegated to the School Management Committee. No doubt the members of that committee knew all the details, but unless the general committee received more information they would not be able to understand the bulk of the work. He repudiated altogether the suggestion that he attacked the com- mittee. Those who shared his views did not wish to 'condemn, but they did not wish to pass the business without having the opportunity of considering it. The CHAIRMAN said he had been a member of the School Management Committee from the outset and he was a member of the Council also. His opinion was that the Committee rather erred on the side of undue detail. They seemed to publish matters almost of infinitesimal importance often in extenso, and if they were to embody the whole of the reports they received from time to time in the printed minutes, a small volume would be requited. He did not think it desirable to do that. On the other hand he was sure the Chairman of the committee would be happy to give any possible information on any subject which came within cognizance of the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>CANON 'MORLEY STEVENSONS EXPLANATIONS.</head>
        <p>CANON 'MORLEY STEVENSONS EXPLANATIONS.</p>
        <p>Canon MOR.LEY STEVENSON explained that with regard to the question of the appointment of married women as school teachers, the rule of the committee was not to appoint a married lady except as a supply teacher—that was to say, to a temporary position which she held only at a week's notice and which was merely occupied until a permanent teacher could be found. The reason why it was inadvisable to have married women as permanent teachers would be obvious. That reason, however, did not apply to a person who could be dismissed, if necessary, at a week's notice. The only lady in the list now submitted who was not a supply teacher was Mrs. Singletonand she was a widow. Some little time ago the School Management Com- mittee considered the matter of the publicity of the Government reports on the various schools. The members of the committee would understand that there were very often subjects in the Government reports which were considered private and confidential with , the management of the schools and with the Education Committee which it would be extremely unwise and inexpedient to publish in the Press. There sometimes had to be, though very rarely, somewhat stringent criticisms of certain teachers, and it would be a very hard thing indeed that those criticisms, which were intenckd for the eyes of those who managed the schools, should be made public to the whole torn. The Committee, not with the 11 slightest wish to conceal anything at all, felt it better that such reports should not be made public. The re- ports were very carefully considered ; any matter which called for special consideration was referred to the managers of the school in order that their opinion might be expressed upon it, and then the School Management Committee dealt with it to the best of their power. He imagined it would always be within the power of any member of the Education Committee by calling at the office to see any particular report he wished to see. The report with reference to the particular school mentioned (Warrington British)was perfectly satisfactory and called for no comment at all. Mr. May might rest quite content that he would have every opportunity of hilly considering the new scheme with respect to the Grammar School. The scheme was very full, rather complicated, and very detailed, and the School Manage- ment Committee felt that it would not be either wise or respectful to submit it to the Education Committee until a sub-committee first and the School Management Committee afterwards had gone very carefully through it and given it their best consideration. If it had been presented to the committee in its rough form that evening there would have been no chance of the Town Council meeting taking place unless the Education Com- mittee had been forcibly ejected. (Laughter.) They would certainly have been there till ten o'clock. With egard to the report of the headmaster of the Secondary school and Technical Institute, that again it would be very unwise to make public. The headmaster reported confidentially to the School Management Committee who were the managers of the school and responsible for it and he had to speak very plainly concerning individual teachers and many matters in the school which it . would be extremely inexpedient to have published. The report was at the office and any mem- ber of the committee could inspect it at any time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>UNDISCUSSED mArrEas,</head>
        <p>UNDISCUSSED mArrEas,</p>
        <p>The committee recommended that Mr. Frank Lord, B.Sc., be appointed mechanical master at the Secondary school and Technical Institute at a commencing salary in accordance with the scale, viz :—at the rate of £l4O per annum. A report upon the woek done at the cookery centre during the past year—showing that, out of 983 girls from public elementary schools who received instruc- tions in cookery during the year, 896 qualified by regular attendance for grant, of whom 54 attended for the third, 315 for the second, and 529 for the first course of instruction—Was submitted and noted. It was resolved, that the salary of Miss Beatrice M. Watkins, assistant cookery mistress at the Technical Institute, be increased from £BO to £9O per annum, as from the 24th August, 1905. The minutes of the School Management Committee were approved in their entirety. NEW SCHOOL ATTENDANCE OFFICER. At a meeting of the School Attendance Committee an application for permission to close the St. George's (Infants') Council school from noon on Thursday, the 21st, until the evening of Friday, the 22nd September next, was submitted, and it was resolved that the I desired permission be granted. It was resolved that the resignation of Mr. George A. Donaldson, assistant School Attendance Inspector he accepted; the engagement to terminate on the 9th September, 1905; also that Mr. Ernest L. Pearce he appointed assistant School Attendance Inspector (sub- ject to one month's trial) at a commencing salary at the rate of £65 per annum, rising by annual increments of CG 10s. ton maximum of .£9l; the engagement to date from the 11th September, 1905, and, subject to his service during the first month proving satisfactory, to be determinable by one month's notice, to expire on the last day of any calender month, given by or to the Committee. Councillor EVANS, who moved the adoption of the minutes, explained that it had for some years been the cnstorn to hold a harvest festival in St. George's Mission and the committee, he thought wisely, had granted permission for the school to be closed for that purpose on the date named. Councillor BROCRII seconded. Mr. MAY, in supporting the minutes. took occasion to remark that it was satisfactory to have such explana- tions as they had just received from the chairman of the School Management Committee, as contrasted with the attacks made by another member of that committee On those who were trying. to do their duty. (Hear, hear.) The GRAIRNIAN : I am afraid you cannot discuss the School Management Committee now. Mr. MAY pointed out the advisability of explaining minutes to avoid 'What was happening to the Town Council itself—that public meetings should be held to condemn its 'mode of procedure owing to the suspicion created by keeping things in the dark. The minutes were approved. THE NECESSITY FOR INCREASED SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>Tae MAvort moved the adoption of the minutes of the Sites and Buildings Committee which contained nothing of public interest. Mr. R. T. FAJRCLOTIGFI seconded.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. UPSON said the minutes of the committee offered a very fair mark indeed for the criticism of some gentlemen who had been so active in criticising the minutes of the School Management Com- mittee. There they had three minutes representing a month's work, and they conveyed no information at all. The Sites and Buildings Committee was one of the most important committees of the authority, for surely they were all feeling the necessity for progress to be made in extension and budding schemes. Nothing had been done as far as he knew and the minutes afforded no in- formation as to what the Committee is doing to supply places for the children who were at present crowded out, of the schools. I The MAyoR : There is nothing on the minutes regarding that matter. Mr, UPSON I know. The MAvon: Well you are out of order. Mr. UPSON said he wished to emphasise that the more voluminous and explanatory minutes were the more persistently they vilP, (, attached. Therefore, he was getting back a little of his own. (Laughter.) Minutes which IVP/13 so concise as those of the Sites and Buildings Committee afforded no information whatever. Councillor LORD : With regard to speaking when the Press representatives are present, I think our.friend Mr. Upson is doing as much of that as anyone. I hope the Press vvill report all he has said. fie has done very well to-night. (Laughter.) Mr. UPSON : May I ask what the point of that is.? The thiairota's : lam not responsible for pointing out all the points. (Laughter.) Mr. UPSON : I was criticising the minutes. Alderman Sminatunsi said if the work done was represented by the accounts passed (RI 7s. Gd.) there was nothing much to be said about it. The MAYOR : I have nothing to answer, and I will be obliged if you will pass the minutes. The inmates were confirmed.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Salt Trade. Shipments of white and ruck salt from Cheshire last month readied 74,585 tons, or nearly 14,000 tons in excess of August, 1904. This is the highest ship- ment in August for ten years. Liverpool .shipped, .54,567 tons, an increase of 0,000, and Runcorn and Manchester Ship Canal improved by nearly 8,000 tons Coast shipments shoived 7,000 tons increase.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Miss Hackney's Health</head>
        <p>Miss Hackney's Health</p>
        <p>Severe Headaches Chronic Indigestion completely cured by Iron-0x Tonic Tablets.</p>
        <p>Miss Harriet Hackney, of Dunston, near Lincoln, has experienced such a remarkable cure through the use of Don-Ox Tonic Tablets that she feels impelled to give it wide publicity in the hope that other girls who suffer, as she suffered, may learn from her case that Don-Ox Tonic Tablets can cure the severest case of indigestion, will remove headaches, will give perfect health. In Miss Hackney's case, undoubtedly, the head- aches were the direct result of Indigestion. She had tried many so-called cures, seeking in vain for relief from the heac' pains which daily distressed her, but it was not until she took Iron-Ox Tablets that she was able to cure the Indigestion, and thus remove the cause of</p>
        <p>Miss HARRIZT HACKKEY.</p>
        <p>the heulsches. The following is her voluntary statement :</p>
        <p>The Sycamores, Dunston, near Lincoln. I used to suffer a great deal from severe pains in the head and from Indigestion. Your Iron-Oz Tablets have done me a great deal of good. They have strengthened me, have cured the Indigestion, with the result that the headaches have quite dis- appeared. I have tried many different remedies before, but they did not do me any good. A very short treatment with Iron-Ox Tablets has simply worked wonders, and I want to thank you for it. (Sgd) HARRIET HACKNEY. Pause a moment and consider the full meaning of this simple, straightforward statement. Miss Hackney's life was darkened by constantly recurr- ing headaches. Every day she suffered, every day she was made nervous and miserable by the cease less, throbbing pains. In addition, her system was weakened by imperfect indigestion. She felt weak and irritable and wretched all the time. She could not take a proper interest in the affairs of her daily life. Naturally, she was unhappy.</p>
        <p>Now C 011199 the second chapier-of this little story from real life. Miss Hackney began to take Iron- Ox Tablets. Very soon the headaches bad ceased, because Iron-Ox Tablets had struck right at the root of the trouble. They cured her indigestion. Simply by helping Nature, simply by strengthening her digestive organs, they have removed the pain, the annoyance, the suffering. As Miss Hackney says, They have strengthened me, they cured the indigestion, with the result that the headaches have disappeared." Oh that every man and every woman who suffers from Indigestion, from the headaches, pain and dis- tress which it causes, would read these words and fully appreciate the wonderful truths which they contain. Wouldn't you be happier, wouldn't you be better, wouldn't you be able to work harder, wouldn't those around you be happier.if you could permanently rid yourself of the Indigestion which irritates and distresses and weakens you ? Then, let Iron-Ox Tonic Tablets do for you what they did for Miss Harriet Hackney. Let her experience be your guide. Go to-day to your own chemist. Purohase a box of Fifty Iron-Ox Tablets for One Shilling,and begin your cure this. very _ -</p>
        <p>As promised last week I here give you full particulars of the Watch Competition. You will remember that the first prize has been awarded to Ernest Doney. Some of the lists were very good, and in point of numbers, very close; in fact, the second one contained only two names lees. This was sent by Thomas Thacker; I have forwarded him a very nice prize. The rest were somewhat lower in a um- bers of new Members, but I have decided to send to all competitors a nice present. I have been greatly pleased with tlie interest shown, and I hope that those of you who did not enter for these prizes, will do your best to secure at least two or three members for our "Corner." I want to make up our number to one thousand during the next month or two, and until further notice shall give to every one who sends in two dozen names, a nice post-card album. I shall print the names of all who join. Further down you will see a list, but the space is rather limited, so _I cannot print all this week. I have sent a prize volume to Mary Plinston, being the 350th member of the League. The puzzles are a little difficult this time, but I have no doubt that some at you will be able to send in correct solutions. I will send a special prize this week to the first correct answer. I hear from several of my nephews and nieces that they are making grand preparations for the coming Flower Show which will be held amongst our members; this is quite right, the time is coming on for the preparation of the ground for sowing seeds. You must remember that October is a very busy month with all gardeners, getting under cover the summer plants and preparing for the spring. I hope all of you take great interest in flowers; no hobby is so nice and fascinating, and think of the great amount of pleasure that it given by flowers. With good wishes from Your affectionate UNCLE NED.</p>
