        <p>'RIALS OF THE TITLED SCHOOL</p>
        <p>The sons of noblemen sometimes make them- selves very unpopular in public and private schools. No matter how blue-blooded he may be, a boy ought to show the best side of his character when amongst youngsters of his own age; but too often the titled pupil holds him- self ajoof from his schoolfellows in a proud, conceited manner, making enemies everywhere tip his studied reserve. The average school- boy can put up with a great deal, but he will not tolerate " standoffishness."</p>
        <p>A young lord of fifteen, who went to a large public school a few years ago, believed that he would everywhere be treated with respect and servility, but he was brought down to his level in a very effective and original manner. One of his most annoying weaknesses was a lavish display of pocket-money, of which he seemed to possess an inexhaustible store, though he never spent it upon anyone but himself. Ho used to pull gold out of his pocket with his handkerchief as though by accident, and would occasionally produce a crisp five-pound note in an off-hand way. Altogether, he thought him- self something big, and an official-looking letter which he received one morning made him swell out still more with pride. This was how the letter ran:—.</p>
        <p>To the Right Hon. Lord —: My Lord, my Committee have been informed of your advent to School, and I am instructed to write you at once in order to secure your patronage. Our club, carried on in the most approved fashion, and entirely restricted to members of the aristocracy, has never yet been able to secure as a patron a young gentleman of such high social standing as yourself, and on this account I beg that you will excuse my rather indecorous haste in approaching you on the subject. Should you, however, decide upon honouring us with the use of your name, I shall be glad to hear from you. (Our patrons generally subscribe about £lO to the club funds on entering).—l beg to remain, my lord, your very humble servant, F. G—, Secretary, -- Gentlemen's Club.</p>
        <p>[To be continued.] • TH E RAINY-DAY BAD.</p>
        <p>What is a rainy-day bag? It is one of the most useful articles that I ever spent a long summer's day in making. It is nothing more nor less than a linen travelling-bag, but very much smaller than those commonly seen. The large travelling-bags will hold all sorts of shawls and wraps—indeed, like a street-car, its capacity never has been fully tested. But my rainy-day bag is small, and is made to hold nothing more than a waterproof and a pair of over-shoes. And the convenience of,it When it looks like rain, one has only to take this jaunty little bag along, instead of carrying rubbers, dear knows how 1 and one's waterproof over the arm, or, worse still, in one of those misshapen little bags sold with waterproofs. To make one, it is only necessary to roll your waterproof and overshoes into a snug oblong parcel of about the same proportions as a child's muff. Note the dimensions—the distance across and. around. The average size will be about fifteen inches around by nine and one-half in width. This will allow an inch for lapping to- gather; and three buttons, with good, firm button-holes should close it. Put one handle on just outside of the buttons, and another just outside of the button-holes, so that when carry- ing the bag the tendency will be to relieve the strain on the button-holes. The end pieces are circular, and measure four and one-half inches in diameter. The bag may be lined with nil- silk, but drilling of some dark colour is as good. The material for the outside may be of almost any strong cloth, but Adah canvas is particularly recommerided, as it does not discolour readily, and is very durable. The even texture, too, will recommend it to many young people who may wish to embellish the little satchel with geo- metrical designs worked in silk or worsted. Many of the larger travelling-bags are trimmed with worsted dress-braid, neatly feather-stitched on, and this, too, makes a pretty ornament. The handles should be lined with burlap or wiggin, to prevent their becoming stringy with use.</p>
