      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS COLLIERY FATALITY. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest on Monday at Thatto Heath on the body of James Preston aged eighteen, of Mill-street, Prescot, who was killed owing to being caught by a runaway journey of full tubs in the Lea Green Colliery on Thursday last.—The Coroner explained that a journey of fifteen full tubs was on the way up a brow when the coupling chain between the second and third tubs broke, and the tubs ran away down the brow. Deceased, who was coming up the brow, was caught by them and killed.—John Thomas, the Ilreman, said the tubs were on top of one another all over the road. There was about 2ft. Gin, on each side Of the roadway. There were several manholes in the vicinity, but sometimes when the tubs broke away there 'Was no time to get out of the way.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and expressed the °Pinion that the fault in the link that broke was due to some dira having accumulated during the welding, and the defect would be there, but it would be impossible to ascertain it. 40,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RT. HON. A. J. BALFCR AND GOLF,</head>
        <p>THE RT. HON. A. J. BALFCR AND GOLF,</p>
        <p>DR. J. G. MCPHERSON. (Author Qt. " Golf, the Best of Games," etc.).</p>
        <p>It has been my privilege to know Mr. Balfour as a golfer, in several ways. After I had writ teir a score of articles on golf to the " Scou. Observer," Mr. Balfour was so enamoured of them that he urged the editor (Dr. W. E. Henley) to get me to publish them in book. form. This I consented to do, if only he would, write the preface to the volume ; and he very graciously—for love of the sport —agreed to do so. I have never ceased to be grateful for this recognition. Very modestly he said in that preface (in 181/1) : "It would be impertinent in one who has no pretentions to be a player to offer any authoritative opinion upon the practical advice contained in the pleasant. pages. But I hope to profit by it." Mr. Balfour is a golfing enthusiast. Even when his life was threatened as Chief Secretary for Ireland, he played the game on North Ber- wick Links. though shadowed by guardnig detectives. The game banished all his fears. With no covering on his head, his -well-known figure was seen stalking over the course as happy as if no questions of State and public policy were in existence.</p>
        <p>- Since that time Mr. Balfour has very much improved in the game. He is one of the princi- pal competitors in the Parliamentary tourneys, and a most interesting foursome was played foi years at North Berwick, with Messrs Balfoui and Laidlay on the one side and Messrs 1)( Zote and Penn on the other. They were about square after many matches. But Mr. Penn ha, gone to his rest; and that memorable foursome i- blasted for ever. MR. BALFOUR SPEAKS OF VIE GAME,</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour has said many good things in favour of the game on performing special golfing functions. In opening a course at Bradford he made this trite remark : " I would like to put it to any of you whether you would prefer to be a Member of Parliament or a scratch player at golf. That is a matter for each man to deter- mine for himself. I can only say that nay own inclination is for golf." _. . .</p>
        <p>Even when Dir. Balfour held his anxious place as leader of the House of COMMOIN, he still managed to run out on his motor and open a golf course near London. He cou- trusted the rival and antagonistic subjects of politics and golf. He considered that it was impossible for any man to play golf to his own satisfaction or to the satisfaction of anyone who had the misfortune to witness his performance, if he occupied his whole week in polities. But with Braid for a partner (against one of the Whips and Herd), he became a changed man. So far in favour of foursome play ! Mr. Balfour is an exceedingly keen supporter of the game. It banishes all care. It makes him an ordinary mortal. Such a grand equalizer is golf with true enthusiasts. "As a Scots man," Mr. Balfour says," " my heart swells with pride when I reflect that it is from Scotland that the infection has spread, not only through- out the United kingdom, but to every part of the world where the English language is spoken ; and you will find it in the most unex- pected places—places where sand is to be found in every spot but the bunkers, and where the greens are innocent of grass—you will find elitliusilstic golfers of Scottish and English descent. You will find them playing under the most unfavourable circumstances, and looking back with a longing memory to the magnificent greens of their native country."</p>
        <p>PLAYS IT 'ENTHUSIASTICALLY,</p>
        <p>Mr. EaHour is of opinion that the growth of the golf courses in the neighbourhood of Lon- don—and their name is legion—has done more than anything else in the last decade to make life healthy and pleasant to the busy workers of the metropolis. He rejoices when lie hears of any new golf course which is -opened within thirty miles of London ; because he knows that it is impossible for the supply to exceed the de- mand, and that every new golf course, with the amenity of scenery and the advantage of judicious laying out, will not only find persons ready to take advantage of it, but will soon create its own market and be crowded on every day on which the workers of the city can tind leisure.</p>
        <p>" Those," he remarked with fervour, "whose energy and enterprise are bringing these golf courses into existence, not only provide for themselves a great fund of pleasure, but pro- vide a great social advantage. These courses constitute a source of healthy recreation, to which there is no dark side at all, which leads to no abuse, is capable of no excess, and can do othing but good., ' Mr. Balfour plays bare headed in all weathers. I remember seeing him, two or three yeers ago, over St. Andrew's links. He was playing in a foursome—young Mr. Asquith was against him, showing that politics was out of count. He was quite interested, but thoroughly philoso- phical. At the " short" hole, coming in, on the putting green, his partner's ball cannoned their opponents', and one of the opposing players' hall cannoned off the other ball into the hole. Mr. Balfour simply smiled, and said, "This is curious billiards." He topped two tee-shots, and said nothing ; but when he foozled the third, he simply remarked (without the usual Sourish of the driver by ordinary misses's), " Isn't it funny ?" That is a philosopher. In his speech at the opening of the new Lon- don course, Mr. Balfour concluded with the following sentences. " I do really feel," he said, that there is this connection between golf and schemes of social amelioration. I wish I could feel that every scheme in which the [-louse of Commons interests itself, every plan of social or political amelioration to which it de- votes its time, could have the same unstinted praise given to it by men of all shades of poli- tical opinion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>RELIEVES IN IT AS AN EDUCATOR,</head>
        <p>RELIEVES IN IT AS AN EDUCATOR,</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour considers that golf is a capital trainer for standing a loss as well as a gain in life. Still be would rather lose a game of golf than not play at all : " 'Tis better to have golfed and lost, Then never to have golfed at all." He is of opinion that Scotch people have the true art of enjoying. It is impossible to add to the sphere of enjoyment more successfully than through the instrumentality of a good Club, which offers to every man, at a practically in- significant cost, every day, in all weathers, at all times of the year, an exercise healthy, delight- ful, agreeable to the winner and to the loser equally ; suitable to the best players in the world, and offering pleasure to the worst player ; good for the beginner and the finished expert ; good for the young, the middle-aged, and the old ; good for the rich and the poor. For the universality of the pleasure which a game of golf supplies to its devotee, for its universal adaptation to every age and either sex, there is nothing in the world which can compare with it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour is a capital representative of the second-class golfer, who knows that be is still some little short of the master-hand, whose play is so easy, certain, and brilliant in all details. And he advocates golf above all games, not only for its fine pliymieul exercise, ever-deepening interest, and all-absorbing enjoyment, but for its ethical and moral training. In losses as well as gains it can teach great lessons : " Be strong to do, ;nal pat i ent to bear ; • For the heart that is true ;,111111 win." _Even duffers can receive grand moral training from the game. Golf is a so.ool for the develop- ment of the manly aild practical virtues, and a field of discipline for the game of life, which must tell powerfully on the race as it is spread- ing over the world. The merest tyro in the sport must find that golf affords great scope for the virtue of self-control. It it 3 par excel- Wane. a zame of honour.</p>
        <p>6.TD -1;57 ITS LrssoN9.</p>
        <p>A study of Mr. Balfour, as it golfer, will show one the real philosophy of the game. The golfer will soon see that, if he loses his temper he loses the, hole. Ile must, like Mr. Balfour. be able to smile when Ile makes a mistake. A beginner, one day, after driving _his ball into a bunker, brought the head of the club smack down 'on the ground as if to break it ; but he was called up by his caddie's moralizing: " Sir, the faues 110 at that end, but at the tither end o' the shaft."</p>
        <p>As in the hands of Mr.`Balfour, the game of golf is a severe training in accuracy and. truth- fulness. It is good, therefore, for all public men. It is highly recommended to ecclesiastics</p>
        <p>tune war-corresponaents itto. more (Riotously, to statesmen and diplomatists. A politician who has an ambition to enter parliamentary or municipal life should always be compelled to take a preliminary course in golf. 'Phis view strongly advocated by ,Yer natthew Vikae- Ridley, once flome Secretary.</p>
        <p>We should be glad to and these men of high parliamentary distinction thus plainly advocat- ing golf as a trainer in manliness. The game has taken deep root wherever the energetic Anglo-Saxon race has flourished. The vigorous characteristic of that race, compared with the Chinaman who considers perfect happiness as embodied in perfect indolence, is embodied in the paenne of golf. We recall the far-seeing epigram of the cl.ark Hemlitus :—" The flying arrow rests."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>BOARDING OUT OF WORKHOUSE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE BOARDING OUT OF WORKHOUSE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Guardians Still Undecided.</p>
        <p>The adjourned debate on the question of the advis- ability of boarding out children under their charge was resumed by the members of the Runcorn Board of Guardians at their meeting on Tuesday. Mr. A. R. Norman presided over the meeting and there were also present :—Messrs. C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, P. Mounfield, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. Stokes, J. Whitlow, A, Broome, T. Baxter, H. M. Davies, S. Woodward, J. H. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, C. E. Linaker, G. Pilling, W. Harrison, S, Horton C. Horton J. Fryer, J. Spruce, W. N. Morris, P. Collins, Douglas J. W. Temperley, J. Rutter, J. Hallows, J. Whitley, R. Billington C. E. Linaker, jun., W. Turner, A. G. Guest, T. Antwis;G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), De Prez, Pearson and Cook (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (workhouse master). Mr. Linaker re-introduced the debate, and asked the Chairman if he had any additional information, as he would like to know how far they could adapt the buildings at present occupied by the children for other purposes. If that could be done and the children boarded out without incuring any additional expense to the Union he for one had no doubt that it was the best course to adopt. He believed in sending the children out if they could do so Rithout adding to the rates. He also desired that they should have a guarantee that the cost of boarding out of ch ldren should not be raised from ss. per week. In a small house like theirs it was not possible to have trainers who would find out the adaptability of the children but in institutions where there were a large number of children it was possible. The cost of maintaining the children In the Workhouse came to 4s. ficl. per week without including the expense incurred from the engaging of an industrial trainer. Food cost is. Gd. and clothing is. 3d. and he believed that if they added other charges incurred through the children it would be found that the actual cost was ss. 3d. per week and that by sending the children out they would not only save 3d. per week but would put them in a better position for after life. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Norman said he had communicated with different homes the authorities of which had emphatically stated that the charge should never be more than ss. per week,in fact they rather indignantly repudiated any suggestion to the contrary. Mr. Linaker's figures rather surprised him and he found that the clerk when stating the cost of the children was 4s. fid. " I cannot vouch for the exactness of the above figures and am of opinion that if thoroughly investigated the cost would prove to be rather more." He had not been able to go into the question of the adaptability of the childrens' quarters to other purposes and he asked that the discussion might be deferred so that he might go into the matter.He desired the Board to have the fullest possible information. Mr. Woodward moved the adjournment of the question, and that the clerk be initructed to go into the exact cost and every detail of expenditure in connection with the children in the house and report to the Board. The Board had never had those figures and he for one wanted them, as they would greatly influence his decision. If it was found that the children would cost the Board the same sum or less than they cost at present he was in favour of their being boarded out. Mr. Spruce asked how it was that the children cost 4s. Od. per week, when the adults only cost the Board £3 6s. 2d. yer year. Mr. Ashton explained that £3 6s, 2d. was the average cost for relief, and included out-relief. Some persons only cost the Board is. 6d. Mr. Spruce said he would like to know if it was , correct that Guardians were called upon to pay a premium for children when they left Dr. Barnados home. When the question of cost wasconsidered he would I like the Board to make note of the travelling expenses which would be incurred from time to time, and which 'would be considerable. Mr. Hallows asked if the Board could be assured that the children would be sent out to work when they I reached the age of 14 years. Mr. Norman explained that in the homes the children received a sound elementary education until they reached the ago of 14 years. After that they were taught a trade until they were 16 years of age when they were qualified to take up a position in life. That is one of the great advantages of the homes. Mr. Baxter objected to the adjournment of the dis- cussion as it was quite time the question was decided. Since the last meeting of the Board he had visited one of the homes and was very much disappointed as the , result of his visit. Mr. Woodward : Mr. Baxter has not visited one of the homes. He went with me to a cottage home to which I took a distinct objection. 1 Mr. Horton did not think the Board was justified in keeping the children until they were 16 years of age out of the ratepayers money. He did not think working men should be called upon to educate other children better than they educate their own. Mr. Linaker did not agree with members of the Board who only put in an appearance on special occasions to vote against certain resolutions. Their duty was to come as often as they eould and not when there was anything special on the agenda. Mr. Roylance : It is a disgrace for certain gentle- men on my left to come on special occasions for a special purpose. Upon a vote being taken it was decided to adjourn the debate.</p>
        <p>A REMOTION IN TIIE RATES. The Clerk presented his estimates for the coming half year. The capital for the current half year was £16,727 and the probable expenditure £14,213 leaving a balance of £2,514.The probable expenditure for the coming half- year was common purposes £3,031, county rate £8,447 and police and contingency £4,204, a total of £15,682. Deducting the probable balance a sum of £13,168 had to be provided for from the rates. The Clerk recommended that a rate of Is. 2d. in the £ a reduction of Id. be levied. The probable expenditure in the various parishes was Acton Grnnge, £283; Alvanley, 1.116 : Antrobus, £lB2 ; Appleton, £448 ; Aston, £321 ; Aston Grange, £33 ; Partington, £42; Budworth, £ll7 ; Clifton, £247 ; Crowley, £95 ; Daresbury, £B6 ; Dutton, £549, Frodsham, £OO7 Frodsham Lordship, £576 ; Grappenhall, £553; Halton. £328; Hatton, £11!; £684 ; Keckwick, £228 ' • Kingsley, £383 ; Kingswood, £145 ; Latchford Without, £209; Norley, £126 ; Moore £319; Newton-by-Daresbury, £B6; Newton-by-Frodsharn £ll2 ' • Norley, £235 ; Norton, £4ll ; Preston-o-th'-ilill' £251 ; Runcorn, £3,867; Sevenoakes, £01; Stockham, £5l : Stockton Heath, £928 ; Stretton, £139 ; Sutton, £460, Thelwall, £350; Walton Inferior, £378 ; Walton Superior, £153 ; Weston, £915; Whitley Inferior, £ll2 and Whitley Superior. .£123. The Clerk recommended that the calls he made due on November 20th and February Bth. Mr. Guest objected to the short notice which was given to the overseers of the calls being due. After a long discussion the clerks recommendation was approved. \ RESIGNATION OF MR. T. LEH _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Lee, representative of the North Ward, Runcorn, wrote resigning his seat on the Board on account of continued illhealth. The chairman said that until recently Mr. Lee had taken the greatest interest in the work of the Board. It was decided to accept the resignation with regret. TENDERS ACCEPTED. The following tenders were accepted : Groceries, Messrs. Kay and Co., Frodsham ; fish, Messrs. R. and P. Melhuish, Grimsby ; butcher's meat, Henry Singleton, Warrington; drapery, Mr. R. P. Reed, Halton: clothing, Mr. T. J. Lee, Warrington; drugs and drysaltery, J. H. Weston, Runcorn; boots, etc., J. W. Bayley, Runcorn ; cottins, Mr. F. Roe Dutton; funerals, Messrs. F. Booth and Sons, Runcorn, and E. Scragg, Dutton; coal,Mesars. Richard Evans and Co. ; wheat straw, Mr. John Moore.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FarLlMEs.—Accordingto " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, September 15th, was 141, viz. : New bankruptcy proceedings published in the London Gazette, 78; deeds of arrangement registered, 63. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies' 89; deeds of arrangement 70—total 159; being a decrease of 18. The total number of commercial failures recorded during the 37 weeks of the present year is 6,314 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 37 weeks of last year was 6,315 showing a decrease of 1.</p>
      </div>
