      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>GREAT DEMONSTRATIONS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>GREAT DEMONSTRATIONS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>power of plutocracy to rule. If he could prove that statement, then he could prove that there was the foulest conspiracy on foot against the interests of Great Britain ia the Transvaal. The statement that the object of these men was to exclude the white worker he wanted to prove up to the hilt and in a way which his hearers must accept because he was going to prove it out of the mouths of the mine owners. After the war one of the mine managers of the rand determined to work his mine with a far greater amount of white labour than had ever been employed before—to give men work to do which had formerly only been done by Kaffir labour. That man was Mr. Cresswell, manager of the Village Main Reef Mine. (Applause.) He had two objects in view. In the first place he wanted to get his men working. There was a shortage of labour and he held that under a proper system of the distri- bution of the work and the greater employment of machinery and white men he could operate his mine with white labour at as great a profit as before. He had another object in view. He was an imperialist and a Jingo-Conservative, but he was an Englishman which few of the rand mine managers were. (Applause.) For as the owners were nearly all foreigners, so the mining managers and engineers were nearly all Americans, most having come from the slave States of the South. He had this imperial object in view—first of all he held that the MEN WHO HAD HELPED TO CONQUER THAT COUNTRY should have the first right to get work in it. (Applause.) He held as a true Imperialist that the only way to hold that country for Great Britain was by allowing large numbers of Englishmen to get work in it. He had fought during the war in command of one of the Volunteer regiments and when his men were dis- banded on the rand—and no doubt many of those pre- sent would remember that the reservists of the British Army were given the option of taking their discharge on the rand—he began to employ white men in his mines. Later on Lord Milner appointed a Commission to inquire into the necessity of introducing Chinese labour into the Transvaal and before that Commission, which was a packed Commission of mine owners, Mr. Cresswell gave evidence, agai ns t the advice of his friends, and his evidence went to show that there was no need to go to Asia for labour, that the mines could be operated with white labour, and if it were necessary to introduce labour from anywhere, Great Britain should be the source of supply. (Applause). His work in his mine had not got out of the experi- mental stage, but from the figures he gave he showed that the mines could be worked at a profit with a greater employment of white labour. This evidence naturally did sot please the mine owners who comprised the Commission and Mr. Cresswell was asked" How is it that the other consulting engineers to the rand mine. owners who are equally as clever as you are do not agree that white labour can be employed ? " and each time Mr. Creswell replied" It is because, for political reasons, the mine owners object to the employment of white men." (Applause). .At the conclusion of his evidence, as he was packing up his papers, one of the mine owners foolishly put to him this question : "What right have you to make that statement ?" and Mr. Cresswell said very quietly," It is becaase of a letter I have received from the Chairman of ray Board of Directors on the subject." More foolishly still, they asked him to read the letter and he read it and it became evidence. It proved con- elusively that the object of the mine owners' scheme was to exclude British labour. The letter was from Mr. Tarbutt,Chairman of the Board of the Village Main Reef Mine,and was sent in reply;to Mr. Cresswell's request to be allowed to employ white men on the mine." Fancy," said Mr. Outhwaite, "an Enqlishra an having to write and a,* for permission to employ Englishmen in a laod that England had just conquered." ( tpplause.) This was what Mr. Tarbutt replied :—" With reference to your trial of white labour for surface work on the 11 mines, I have cousnited the Consolidated Goldfields people, and one of our directors has consulted Messrs. Wernher, Beit, and Co., and the feeling seems to be one of fear that if a large number of white men are i employed on the rand n the position of labourers, the same troubles will arise as are now prevalent in the Australian colonies, namely, that the combination of the labouring classes will.become so strong as to be able more or less to dictate, not only on questions of wages but also on political questions by</p>
      </div>
