        <p>THE POWER OF THEIR VOTES. when a representative Government is established." (Applause). These great firms operated more than half the mines of the Transvaal. More than half the output of the gold of the Transvaal came into the coffers of the ifirms whose representative stated that white men were not to be employed for fear of their votes. These were the men who engineered the war for the franchise, and when the country was conquered they said " No Englishmen lin the Transvaal for fear of their votes." There was something in the letter which, as an Australian' he would like to explain to them, in regard to what Mr. Tarbutt meant when he said the same troubles would arise as were now prevalent in the Australian colonies, About that time there had been an election for the Commonwealth Parliament of Australia, and from Western Australia—where the gold fields were, and where the conditions were the same as they should be in the ' Transvaal, that was the white miners were in the majority—every man sent to the Lower and Upper Houses, with one solitary excep- tion, was sent to join the Federal Labour Party. In Western Australia to-day in the State Parliament there was a Labour Government ruling the country, and ruling it well. (Applause). That was the condition existing in Australia that Messrs. Wernher, Belt and Co., were not going to allow to arise in the Transvaal. They were not going to have Labour parties there. But he could tell Messrs. Wernher, Belt and Co., as an Australian that as to whether in Australia Labour parties had been good or bad there was a difference of opinion, but they would not find ten men throughout the length and breadth of that land who would uot sooner be ruled by any Labour party than by Messrs. Wernher,Beit and Co. (Applause.) lie would quote what the director of the Consolidated Gold Fields (Mr. C. D. Rudd) had to say. After the war he went to the Transvaal to study the conditions and on returning furnished a memorandum to his company on the Labour question. He said : "Could we replace the 200,000 native workers by 100,000 unskilled whites, they would simply hold the country in</p>
        <p>THE HOLLOW OF THEIR HANDS and without any disparagement to the British labourer I must say that I prefer to see the more intellectual section of the community at the helm"—that was Messrs. Wernher, Belt, Eckstein and Co., he (Mr. Outh- Waite) presumed. (Laughter.) Mr. Rudd did not say that the mines would not pay if worked by 100,000 whiternen. What he objected to was that the 100,000 British workers would hold the government of the country in the hollow of their hands. That was where he (Mr. Outhwaite) joined issue with Mr. Rudd and all the rest of the gang. (Applause.) He said that until the British worker was at the helm in the Transvaal, the Transvaal would be a curse to Great Britain. He said that until that country was governed in accordance with British traditions by English- men instead of in accordance with the ideas of cosmopolitan finance in order to increase their greed and profits and dividends, that land was a curse to England. One more quotation he would read— this time a partner of Messrs. Wernher, Belt and Co.— Mr. Lionel Phillips. He said: "The wastrels of England we will not have at any price, because at Gd. a day they would be dear. Your good, honest workmen we cannot afford." "Yes," continued Mr. Outhwaite " "I like the term</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 23, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 23, 1905.</p>
        <p>or civil war or revolution and the people of Great Britain would again be privileged to pay the cost. THE WHOLE THING WAS A FRAUD. It was immoral and could not exist.(Cheers).Look at the imperial aspect. He would remind them of the state- ments made by the imperialist of Birmingham who speaking only a few weeks ago upon the coustitution granted to the Transvaal for the purpose of allowing the Boer to remain on the rand, said he feared that constitution was too liberal and was afraid that the Boers would be able to get back by political means the power which they lost. He (Mr. Chamberlain) warned his audience to beware of the return to power o f the Pro- Boer and Little Englander party in England. He took Mr. Chamberlain at his word. Supposing that danger did exist in the granting of the franchise, it existed because there were not enough Englishmen in the Transvaal. (Applause.) If traitor there be, who was the traitor ? He said it was the man who put 50,00 D Chinese miners into the rand mines instead of white men. (Applause.) He was doubly traitor who was traitor to his country and he (the speaker) was reminded of the old words :</p>
        <p>The traitor to humanity Is the traitor most accursed. (Applause) He said that every man who had anything to do with this question, with this infamy, was a traitor to humanity and that he was going to prove. It was said a little while ago—he doubted whether Mr. Chamberlain and his party dare say it now—that to term the system as slavery was to speak what was false, that it was hysteria, Exeter Hall talk, and so on. Let them see if the essentials of slavery did exist. First of all let them see whence these Chinese labourers came, how they came, and what was their condition when they got under the flag beneath which they were told no slave could breath. Having some knowledge of the Chinese and of Asiastic systems, he would ask his hearers to remember that these men did not come as free men. In great areas of China ruled by their Viceroys under Asiatic despotism, in the hands of the Viceroys was the life of every man, woman and child. They had the power of life and death over their subjects. It was these men who were largely providing the serfs for the rand mines. Mr. Outhwatte quoted a telegram from a "Morning Leader" Hong Kong correspondent stating that the rebels captured by the Viceroy in an outbreak at Kaaa-tse were being given the option of losing their heads or volunteering for service in the South African mines. (Laughter.) In "The Eastern Times," a newspaper published in China, it was also stated that officials at Hong Kong were making arrangements for the ship- , ment of Kan-tse rebels who had surrendered to South I Africa to labour in the mines. rho Viceroy, it was added, hada great number of rebels on his hands and expected to have a great many more before long, hence the idea of getting them shipped to South Africa had a great deal of brightness ] and originality to recommend it. (Laughter.) Reuter's correspondent cabling from Hong Kong said "There seems no doubt that the Viceroy of the Two Kans has inquire], whether captured rebels would be accented by the emigration authorities. The reply was in the affirmative, provided the men came voluntarily." (Laughter.) If the Viceroy said to the men over whom he had power of life atm death "Go and recruit for the rand mines "—because on every man he got about ss. a head—and they went, they did so voluntarily and were accepted. (Laughter). To show the barbarities practised at the instigation of the Viceroys in China Mr. Outhwaite quoted a description of an execution by the " slow carving" process carried out by order of one of the Viceroys, who, he said, was recruiting serfs for the rand, mines. In China the men lived under the foulest slavery, but when they escaped from such condi- tions to British territory, were things much better for them? The men were beinglrecruited under the Transvaal Labour Ordinance which inflicted certain penalties of fines and imprisonment on the men who infringed the terms of their servitude. Within the year during which the system had been in operation they had already in ' the British Courts of Justice</p>
        <p>FINED AND DSPRTSONEO 2,500 of these wretched serfs for trying to get their freedom, to break from the terms of their servitude. During the last month for which there were details, 500 were fined and imprisoaed. They were therefore punish- ing them at the rate of over 5,000 a year. To-day in the prisons of the Transvaal there were 1,000 wretched serfs confined for doing no wrong except trying to get their feeedom. The mine owners had. however, found that they could not compel the Chinese to accept their servitude under the penalty that could be imposed in a British Court of Justice, so they began to enforce penalties themselves. They introduced into the British territory beneath the British flag the BA RBA ROUS SYSTEMS of torture such as the Viceroy of the Two Kens in his I own barbaric land vould adopt. They brought the torturers from China and called them Chinese police, and so when they seat the labourers to work in thel mines and they did not doss much work £1,4 the employers I thought would be equiteble for to wages they were getting—ls, a day—they were taken from the mine head to the compound and flogged by the Chinese police. Not only was there flogging,but there were the terrible systems of Asiatic torture introduced in the mines. 1 They had absolute evidence of that now sworn to even in British Courts of Justice. No longer could it be deniedl land even the Free Church Council of Johannesburg had I gone to Lord Selborne and protested against the flogg- ings ' ings in the mines. (Applause.) He said " even ' because so often had he heard that Council quoted in England when, to the shame of those men who did not I help them, he and others were fighting one of the noblest causes ever fought against one of the foulest and most corrupt tyrannies ever in existence. Under the new regulatione it was ordered that in the Transvaed, under .the British flag, whitemen might carry arms and that they might scour° the country and wherever they found a refuge they could arrest him and bring him to the authorities anu on every Chinamen they captured the authorities woald pay .Cl.. That money was collected from the mine owners who,when they got the serf back, deducted it from his wages. (" Shame.") They saw that these men—for after all they were men—were bursting from their compounds and sweeping through the Transvaal. There were nearly a thousand out ; they were hungry and starving, and when men tried to protect their pro- perty they killed as other men did. It was not the fault of these men. It was the fault of the Govern- ment which allowed the system. Such a state of affairs had come about that he received a letter that day from a man of the highest authority who stated that a number of Dutch farmers on the veldt sleep together in one hoase at night for fear of attacks on their lonely farmsteads by the Chinese marauders. For all this, done .in the name of freedom, justiee and civilization, he (Mr. Guth- Waite) asked his hearers if A GREAT OBLIGATION did not rest upon them to do away with this thing. He spoke, and was glad to speak, as a Colonial for this reason. Not long ago Imperial statesmenallowed eighty thousand of colonials to fight in the Transvaal on the plea that it was a war for justice, that it was a war for freedom, that it was a war for civilization, to open the Transvaal as a field for white laeour, amid the colonies had is right to say as they had done by votes of millions to the Government of Eugland : "Justify this war to us in the only way in which it can be justified by bring- ing about greater freedom and greater civilization in the Transvaal." (Applause). Well might it be asked : What had they to show for the 21,000 Englishmen and Colonials who had died in defence of their country on the field of battle, for the 4,000 brave Dutchmen who died in defence of their country ? The tragedy of it all was the knowledge that the Dutchmen knew what the mine owners were forcing this war upon them for. There was no quarrel between Englishmen and Dutch- men as Englishmen and Dutchmen. The quarrel was between the Dutchmen and the cosmopolitan financier, alien to our race, who wanted to seize the mines and have cheap labour. And what had they to say about the 4,000 Dutch women and the 15,000 children who died in the concentration camps? If they had only the Chineee compound to show for it the people stood condemned and the colonies had a right to say they had been betrayed by the British Government. (Applause). A moral obligation rested upon the people of England to sweep away this foul system, and to justify as far as possible the war in the only way it could be justified. It seemed to him that in Lancashire at least an appeal could not be made in vain. It would be strange that Lancashire should give toleration of any sort to the introduction of slavery because it was Lancashire that furnished</p>
        <p>A NOBLE EXAMPLE in the history of Laboar not long ago—the example that the British worker would not tolerate slavery. The last attempt that W2S made on the part of any section or class in England to get the sanction of England to slavery was at the time of the civil war in America in 1862. The ships of the North blockaded the ports of the South from which Lancashire obtained its cotton supply. The consequence was the great cotton famine and awful distress prevailed throughout the length and breadth of the county. At that time there came emissaries from the slave owners of the South and they tried to get the Tory Government Of Great Britain to join with them to open the ports. They pleaded that if that were done Lancashire could get its cotton and they appealed to the Lancashire operatives and their families who were starving to lend their aid to the slave-owing South so that they might profit. Then it was that John Bright thundered forth and appealed to the Lancashire operatives to put humanity before their immediate gain. There came to his (Nlr. Outhwaite's) mind the words of one of the gre tt speeches made byJohn Bright, he believed in Lancashiretin which he said : "These men propose this monstrous thing : That over a territory forty times the size of England, the curse and blight of slavery shall be for ever perpetuated. I cannot With believe that such a fate will befall that fair land,stricken itrsavjoaguernseoyf to the Is u cannot will believe tihtatnociwvihlLa itia the sink into endless night to gratify the ambition of the men Who seek to wade through slaughter to the throne And shut the gates of mercy on mankind." (Applause.) " He .did not,,, concluded Mr. Outhwaite, "appeal in vaia to Lancashire. I say to you people: the mine owners of the Transvaal have waded through slaughter to a throne. I say they have shut the gates of mercy on mankind and I say it is for you, and especially for the workers of England, to force open wide those gates, though profits and dividends do fall to zaulti-millionaires." (Loud and prolonged cheers)".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>TELEGRAM_FROM MR. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH LIOWE3 read the following telegram which had been received from Mr. A. H. Crosfield, and which was addressed to the Chairman of the meeting : " All best wishes, my friend, for successful and enthusiastic meeting. Recent news from Rand only confirms the opinion of those who,like John Burns condemned the ill-omened and sinister introduction of Chinese quasi-slavery as marking another stage in Jingo Rake's progress of late." (Applause). MR. F. NEILSON AND THE BIRIIINGHAM CAMPAIGN. THE LAND QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. NEtr.sos, who had a cordial greeting, said it seemed to him that after the great mad orgies through which they had passed at the time of the war men were to-day beginning to reflect and think seriously of these matters. Never had he been so convinced of that as he had been during the past five weeks. No doubt many of his hearers were surprised when they heard that the West Birmingham Liberal Association had sent an invitation to Mr. Outhwaite to address them with a view to becoming their candidate. At the time men who knew Mr. Outhwaite well said there could</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NOT BE A BETTER MAN</head>
        <p>NOT BE A BETTER MAN</p>
        <p>to go to that constituency and fight the great battle of those principles that Liberalism had always represented. (Applause.) He would never forget Mr. Outhwaite's first public meeting when he spoke about Chinese labour at West Birmingham, where meetings had hardly been known for a long period of years. Men who came to scoff voted with the others unanimously condemning such labour. (Applause.)Since that time Mr. Outhwaite had been steadily convincing arid converting the people. (Hear, hear.) If people in West Birmingham would turn their ears to those who had a message to give there was certainly hope for the the rest of England—even for Liverpool with all its Toryism. (Applause.) Continuing, he said that they as Christian men and wo nen must think before they voted. They must not bow down before the personality of any statemen no matter what party they belonged to, but use the brains that God had given them. (Applause.) Liberalism to-day, meant the champione'aip for the people and what the people of England needed. How could they raise a great nation if they did not give the people of England a chance to rise? It could not be a great nation by giving sections of the community all the opportunties. (Applause.) The speaker referred at length to the land monopoly and pointed out that through it men were driven from the country to the townslwhich became congested. Ia Shropshire more than a quarter of the land was owned by 13 people and there were in it 5,000 paupers. On the face of it it was a most terrible crime that such a thing should exist. There was a population of 253,090 and nearly one million acres, and game preserves were being made year after year, driving out not only the farmer but the agricultural labourer himself. Though crime was decreasing the police force was becoming bigger and bigger, so that the police could act as night gamekeepers for " their lordships." Bat they could set all these things right. The site on which Parr Hall stood was land and the value of it had been made by the people of Warrington ; and so it was all the way through the districts. The greatest subject of interest to the people of the future must be, if they wished to see more equal distribution of wealth, the relief of taxation that fell upon the working class:9. It must be placed on the shoulders of the people who were able to bear it. (Loud appl...use.) As long as England tolerated the evils of which he corn- pinned, so lion; would they have poverty, arid great poverty where there was great wealth. Look, for instance, at a town like )Varrington, where he had no doubt there was great wealth as well as great poverty. It was the true and patriotic duty of all to fight against monopoly and relieve the people, give them an oppor- tunity, let the town spread, let the manufacturers have a chance. Take the burden off the industry itself and put it on the land on which the industry was built and we would find that in England of the y.c9.rs to come there would be no possibility of her crumbling to dust as other great empires had done, but they would see a brighter light in men's eyes, women with a happier smile and children would grow rip into a citizenship which all the world would envy and which would be an everlastingthonour to the working classes to whom they looked to carry out these things. (Cheers.) VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. TFtom AS B moved a vote of thanks to the speakers for their addresses. Alderman TENNION, in seconding, said that that eKcel- lent meeting was due to the efforts of the Progressive Labour League. He hoped that after listening to the able, yet somewhat tragic address of Mr. Oethwaite,and the very inspiriting address of Mr. Nielson, the result of the meeting would be shown in the future representa- tion of the borough. (Applause.) He did not know what most of those present thought, but the object of that meeting was to educate the voter in Warrington so that when he cast his vote at the next election, he should be in the position of having some facts, figures and dates to go upon. At any rate there would be no excuse, whether he voted for the Conservatives or for the Liberals, to say that the Liberal Party and the other associations had not given a full opportunity of thoroughly understanding all these questions. (Ap- plause.) Mr. JOSEPH HOWES seconded. Mr. Neilson had been standing by Hr. Outhwaite's side all along at Birming- ham, and they had addressed open-air meetings—not because they could not obtain the use of halls, but because there was more room outside than inside. (Laughter.) Some time ago, when the Liberals issued posters calling attention to the recent Liberal victories, someone posted slips over them which asked the Liberals " not to count their chickens before they were hatched." He recognized the wisdom of the advice but as Liberals, they had been hatching all along the line, and all the chickens had turned out Radicals. (Laughter and applause.) He believed that when the polls were opened at ‘Varrington they would realise a glorious state of things here. (Applause.) The people of Warrington had heard the rank and file of the Liberal party but the Liberal Association were anxious to get some of therleading statesmen down also so that they could hear the leaders. (Applause.) The resolution was carried with great heartiness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>MR. °UTE-MATTE AND MR. CHAMBERLAIN</head>
        <p>MR. °UTE-MATTE AND MR. CHAMBERLAIN</p>
        <p>, Mr. OUTIIWAITE, on rising to reply, was again loudly cheered. Ile said it was an enormous pleasure to him to have addressed such a large meeting, because it proved to him once again, if it were necessary, how little the Government of England represented the people ; and in that fact lay the great hope of the future He had a desire at one time to "stump" Australia from end to end as a Republican preaching hatred of the British Government but he had since learned how little the Government of England represented the people, and he was glad of every opportunity that came in his way to do all he could to shorten its reign, and show the people the truth of the whole thing with regard to South Africa. It was not he (Mr. Oathwa(te) who was opposing Mr. Chamberlain; it was the people who were fighting him. The days of Mr. Chamberlain were num- bered in the land as a man in whom the people could trust. He was in the hands of a higber power. It was said " The mills of God grind slowly, but they grind exceeding small," and they were going to grind exceed- ing small " Mr Joseph Chamberlain and all the men who had had anything to do with this great infamy. It would come. The truth was unfolding, the tragedy was rolling on in the Transvaal, and he said that the guilty would be brought to justice sooner or later. And when retribution fell it would chiefly fall upon the head of Mr. Joseph Chamberlain. He had much pleasure in proposing that the best thanks of the meeting be accorded to Councillor Evans for his services in the I chair. (Applause). Mr. NEILSON in seconding urged upon his hearers their duty in studying those great questions to obtain a full knowledge of the facts. The vote was heartily carried and a brief reply by the chairman brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>MEETING IN THE GO-OPERATIVE HALL.</head>
        <p>MEETING IN THE GO-OPERATIVE HALL.</p>
        <p>Other Aspects of the Chinese Labour • Question. WHITE MEN ABLE TO DO THE WORK. So great was the success of Tuesday evening's meeting that it was arranged that Mr. Outhwaite should address a second gathering in the Co-operative Hall' Sankey-street, on Wednesday evening. There was !another crowded and enthusiastic assembly and Mr. Outhwaite's speech created a great impression. The Rev. C. Harvey Cook presided and was supported on the platform by Mr. R. L. Outhwaite, Mr. J. Howes, Councillors Evans, Magian, and Burton, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, J.P., J. Morton, E. Osborne, F. Hitchen. T. Barnes, J. H. Morton and Powell, Mrs. Harvey Cook, Mrs. Osborne, and Mrs. Howes. THE CHAIRMAN AND NATIONAL RIGHTEOUSNESS. The CHAIRMAN said his attendance at the meeting in Parr Hall and the moral fire and ring of the speeches I then made persuaded him to attend the second meeting and had caused him to accept the chairmanship. It was time, as their speakers on Tuesday told 'them again and again, that they looked upon political matters not from any dilletantc position but from a righteous standpoint and that they brought to bear upon political questions that " righteousness which exalteth a nation"— (applause)—and those vigorous moral principles, with- out which no nation could;solidly build up itself having any firm foundation or any fine superstructure, fie had been in Warrington for two years and he had been try- ing to measure the town, and what he felt it wanted and, as he looked his hearers in the face and spoke to them as men and workingmen mostly, he wanted them to remember one thing especially that was said on Tuesday evening: "Don't be led away again." (Rear, hear.) They must be perfectly sure that they knew where they were going and not put one foot forward before they knew where they were going to put it down. Above all, they must BRING THEIR CONSCIENCES nrro THEIR POLITICS. He had abstained from taking any part in political</p>
        <p>meetings for some seven or eight years, because it seemed to him that both the Liberal party and Conserv- ative party were each only anxious to get into (Ace, that each only sought position to grind their own axes. But as he said to the two speakers onTuesday night—and he said to his hearers now—it was a delight to him to find the tone of ringing righteousness that was so pro- nounced in the days of Bright and Gladstone rising again in the younger states nen. (Applause). For that reason, as a man first and as a Christian minister after- wards, he had taken his position again amongst those who were working for the common wealth and common health of the nation. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-23_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>MR. OUTEIWAITE'S SPEECEI.</head>
        <p>MR. OUTEIWAITE'S SPEECEI.</p>
        <p>Mr. OUTFIWAITE, who had a rousing reception, announced his intention of dealing with the subject of Chinese labour from further points of view. Before he did so it would be necessary for his hearers to get some clear conception of what the Transvaal was and of the peculiar conditions that existed there. Being a Colonial himself, he knew a great many of the colonies which comprised the empire. He might say he considered he knew as much about the empire as was known by the great statesman down in Birmingharn—(Laughter)— but really, as a matter of fact, he had learnt a great deal about the colonies that he never knew before by way of the Birmingham mouth-piece. (Laughter). There was no place so rich in natural wealth as the Transvaal. In the gold mines in one small area 40 miles in length and a mile in width, there was gold I estimated to be worth £2,000,000,000, and it was also estimated by the mine owners that given a sufficient number of Asiatic self labour they would in five years be able to produce gold at the rate of about £50,000,000 a year—or more gold than was produced by the rest of the world put together. The fransvaal was virtually the</p>
        <p>TREASURE HOUSE OF THE BRITISH EMPIRF. The enormous wealth was lying below the surface of the ground waiting the hand of labour to bring it fourth and the question was as to whose labour was going to produce that wealth.lf it was to be the labour of white men and freemen, such as had produced gold in every other British colony and built up those British colonies under their protection, then in course of time the Transvaal would become a great British colony and a tower of strength to Great Britain. But if it was going to be produced by Asiatic serf labour the State would be nothing but a great Asiatic convict settlement, and would be a curse and a shame to Great Britain. (Applause). The conditions in the Transvaal were different to those which existed in any other colony. Its groat wealth, which was beyond computation, was in the hands of very few men who had no interest what- ever in the prestige and Lonour of England. Their only question was to got as much as they could out of the gold of the country. He did not blame them, and they were not to blame if they drove every white man from the country. He, how- ever, blamed the Government of England which allowed them to do such things —(applause)--and the blame in the first instance lay upon the people of England who elected such a Government to represent them. (Ap- plause.) The prosperity of the Transvaal was based upon the production of gold and the development of the mineral wealth of the country. The mine owners had the enormous power of being able to say to the people " you must grant our demands or we will close down the mines." If they carried out that threat they would bankrupt every individual and ruin the com- mercial community. That power was possessed by no other mine owners in the British Colonies. If the mine owners in Australia did not choose to work their mines and fulfil the labour convention the mines were subject to forfeiture and any man possessing a miner's right, which cost is. could claim the forfeiture. The mine owners in the Transvaal were however, wise, far-seeing men, who realized that if the same class of men worked in the mines of the Transvaal as produced the gold in other British Colonies, they would introduce laws to do away with their power to coerce the community. Con- sequently they were determined that such a class of labour should never be introduced. The whole tragedy of South Africa was a great fight by</p>
        <p>MONOPOLY AGAINST THE PEOPLE. Mr. Outhwaite proceeded to read the names of the ownersmine proof of their foreign nationality. We in Eriga as a England were told that the people of the Trans- vaal consented to the introduction of Chinese labour , but he desired to show them how absolutely untrue was the information we got from the Tory press of the Transvaal. He was in the agitation from first to last. Early in the history of the movement a great meeting was called at Johannesburg at which, by a vote of 5,000 to two the following resolution was passed: "We pledge ourselves by all means in our power to prevent the introduction of Asiatics into the Transvaal." (Applause.) At the greatest meeting ever held in Pretoria the same resolution was adopted by 2,000 votes to one. The mine owners then began to bring into operation the great forces with which to compel the people to submit to their demands. They threatened to close down the mines and bankrupt the State if they wen) not allowed to introduce Chinese labour. (Applause.) Sir Arthur Lawley, Lieutenant- Governor of the Transvaal, was approached by a depu- tation representing every trade and commercial union, and asked to give the people of the Transvaal the right to vote upon the question. Mr. Lawlay said such a course would be unconstitutional but promised he would be guided by public meetings. A public meeting was at once called at Johannesburg for the purpose of taking a vote upon the question but despite appeals to the police for assistance the meeting was</p>
        <p>BROKEN UP BY GANGS OF RUFFIANS hired at 15s. for the night by the mine owners. He had secured affidavits from some of the men, sworn before justices,to prove that was so.(Applause.)The next day Lord Milner cabled to England saying thatthe meeting called to oppose Chinese labour had been a complete fiasco. (Applause.) That was how the people of England had been misled even by the representative of the British Government. When the labour ordinance was passed the Johannesburg Trades and Labour Council which represented every trade union on the rand passed the following resolution "That this meeting protest against the introduction of Asiatics being firmly con- vinced that it will ultimately result in the reduction of white labour, and thereby be detrimental to the State" and yet Lord Milner told the British Government that the workers of the Transvaal were in favour of the ; project. The whole thing had been</p>
        <p>ENGINEERED IN FRAUD and forced upon the people by the Tory Govern- ment of England and it was upon the Tory Government that the real blame rested. (Applause.) A plea which had often been raised in England and also on the rand was that Chinese labour was necessary. He held himself that the thing was a crime, that it was wrong and immoral and therefore it could not be neces- sary. (Hear, hear.) He would, however, take the supporters of the scheme at their own word and deal with the supposed necessity of the labour. If the mine owners had wanted coloured labour they could have got sufficient Kaffirs if they had liked to treat the Kaffir properly and give him a fair rate of wages. (Applause.) No greater attrocity had ever been committed in the history of a white race in contact with blacks than the attrocity that Was going on in connection with the treatment of the Kaffir labourer in the mines. (Applause.) He said that since the war war there had been upwards of 10,000 Kaffirs killed n the rand mines simply through the neglect to provide a proper system of ventilation, proper treatment, and proper food. If the Kaffiir died from natural causes to that extent the mine owners had no right to be allowed to introduce a single Kaffir labourer into the mines. The Kaffir did not come to the mines as free men any more than the Chinese did. Eighty per cent. of the Kaffir labourers were sent by the Portuguese Government for 10s.a head received under a treaty made with the British Government in the interests of the mine owners. There was another way in which the mine owners could have solved the problem—the way he held they should be compelled to solve it—and that was by the employment of white men in the mines. (Loud applause.) The first thought that would occur to his hearers would be, "Why should you wish white men to be employed in the mines whore the mortality is so great?" They would no doubt say that the mines must naturally be unhealthy. They must disabuse their minds of that idea for this reason. The Kaffir was sent from a tropical country, unused to work of that kind, to the rand which was 6,000 feet above sea level and in winter was one of the coldest of climates. The very cause which largely contributed to the enormous mortality of the Kaffir was the very reason which made the rand the best gold-mining field in the world for white labour. (Applause). In a tropical climate the white man could not compete with coloured labour, but the rand had an ideal climate for the white man to work in. So too for the same reason, the mines should be the best of all gold mines in existence for white men to work in. The temperature at the bottom of the shaft of one of the deepest mines was only about 65 degrees whereas in Western Australia men were work- ing up to 100 degrees. He doubted if there was a coal mine in England with such a low temperature as existed in a rand mine. So far as those conditions were con- cerned the mines were SPLENDID FOR WHITE MEN. however, tho owners did not construct ventilation shafts men would die because of the accurumulation of dynamite gasses. The mortality of the mines was all a question of the spending of money by the mine owners. (Applause). It was simply a question of compelling. the mine owners to adopt the same precautions for the health of their workers as the mine owners of England were compelled to adopt. (Applause). At the time of the Chertsey election Mr. Balfour sent a letter to the electors stating that the Chinese were being introduced into the mines to do work which the British worker could not and would not do. That was untrue as fares it went. What he should have said was " which the British worker could not and would not do at Is. a day." (Applause.) Every ounce of the gold in Australia was produced by white labour and they would not allow a single Asiatic to be employed under ground. The rate of wages in Western Australia was fixed by law and the skilled miners received 14s. 6d. per day. The mine owners of the Transvaal should be compelled to work their mines by skilled labour paid at a high rate of wages. They could work their mines cheaper by highly skilled white labour than they would be able to work them with Chinese at Is. a day. A skilled miner with a rock drill could do the work in Australia that 30 Chinese serfs were doing on the rand. Cheap labour did not mean cheap production. (Applause.) To show that white men could work in the mines Mr. Outhwaite</p>
        <p>quoted from the official report of the Crown Reef -Mine - a statement that work of a particularly dangerous character had been done with credit by unskilled white labourers 359 of whom were employed. The work- ing costs, however, ran up to 30s. a ton and consequently, the report stated, it became necessary to reduce the number of white labourers, and over half of them were discharged. ("Shame.") The report recom- mended that total dividends of 140 per cent, be paid, the amount paid in dividends being nearly, £2,000,000. Mr. Outhwaite quoted the rate of dividends paid by the' mines in 1904 which was stated to have been a period of depression, and the amounts ranged from 60 per cent. to " the paltry little dividend "of 250 per cent. There had been invested in the rand mines to capital account £30,000,000 and there had'already been paid in profits and dividends something like £40,000,000. The people of England had invested in the Transvaal £250,000,000 which was the cost of the war. England was not going to get back one single penny piece in coin as the result of that expenditure. The mine owners got Chinese labour from Mr. Chamberlain. What he got from the mine owners was:a promissory note to help some time in the flotation of a loan of £30,000,000. Mr. Chamberlain had not dared to present that promissory note for par ment becanse he knew it would be dishonoured if he did TIE WOULD NEVER GET A SINGLE PENNY PIECE to jingle on the tombstone of his party from the mine owners. (Applause). The interest on the loan of 230,000,000 would have to be raised in the Transvaal, . .</p>
        <p>but Mr. Chamberlain made a pledge to the mine owners not to increase the taxation levied upon the mines; The mine owners—the only people who could pay an° who benefited by the war - therefore escaped their obliga- tion to pay towards the cost of the war. (Applause.) There was one aspect upon which he liked to speak, because he spoke as one having authority to do so for a very great number of people. There might be differ- ences of opinion on the question in England, but there was very little difference of opinion, as the Tory Government would find when the people had a chance' ` of speaking. (Applause). In the colonies there wan, absolutely no difference of opinion. Ten millions of colonists were unanimous on the question. They had a right to speak as believing the statement of the Imperiali colonial secretary of Birmingham, that the war was for justico,freedom and civilization they sent 80,000 men to [assist in the sweeping away of the Boer Government. Having done that they had the right to say what should happen in the Transvaal and had a right to protest , I against the introduction of Asiatic labour, and their I protest should have been considered by the [ British Government. (Applause). Australia by a • I unanimous vote of both houses of Parliament protested I New Zealand did the same, and Canada was equalll I • against It. TEN MILLION COLONISTS PROTESTED but the Imperial Government, which was supposed to represent the colonies did not listen to them, but the I demand by Wernher,Beit, Eckstein and Co., was heeded. MI. Seddon, the Jingo Imperialist Prime Minister of New Zealand said that the help of the Australasian:; colonies during the war would not have been, given if they had dreamed of the fact that lt would have enabled the exclusion of white men and the introduction of Chinese. Mr. Deakin, the Prime 'Minister of Australia, said if the authorities' ' had informed them that the war was for Chinese mine,'" it would have put a different aspect on the war. they had warned them at the commencement of the struggle they would have said "Keep your mines, Your - cheapness is too dearly purchased. It is not to be , bought with blood." (Applause). The leading paper in Australia. "The Melbourne Age" hoped tie Govern- ment would actually be overthrown in the Transvaal bi . force of arms. The Transvaal was drifting towards revolu: tion and he said that if that day did dawn, and he hoped it never would, he knew where his befooled felloW- " countrymen would be—they would not be found fighting under the British flag in support of Chinese labour for alien magnates. That was the result of the war Or justice, freedom and civilization. He asked them we.re they satisfied? (Cries of "No.") The only way n• justify the war was by introducing conditions of, grea"" =- freedom and justice. No nation could eiloW its greatness to be undermined in that wsl, and the foundation of England's greatness wa" its love of freedom. They could not undermine every- thing which had made England great in order to give slave labour to Messrs. Wernher, Beit,Eckstein and Co., 5 without damaging the nation and bringing inevitablr retribution upon themselves. (Loud and prolongen' cheers.) SPEECH BY MR. JOSEPH HOWES. Mr. Howks in proposing a vote of thanks to Afr• Outhwaite said it was necessary they should have all the ; information which it was possible on the subject because they had been so grossly and criminally misled. It was said that if the mine owners were not allowed to cerrY on the business according to their own notions tlig country would be ruined. They did not mean by "ruin what decent people meant by the word. Their success would curse the country and their ruin would bless the country. If they could make everyone of the mine owners bankrupt that night it would be THE GRANDEST THING FOR HUMANITY and civilization that ever happened. People perhapl thought that when the Liberals were returned to Pardo- ment the difficulty would be over. He was afraid it would not, and he did not want the people to be dceived. The Chinese were indentur3d and were the slaves of their masters. The Government could not saY at once" Send away the Chinaman. We will not eller it." The owners would come upon them for compenss;- tion for the loss of their slaves. ( A voice : "Pay it. ') Perhaps they might pay, but he hoped they would not; Sir It would be a terrible thing if they did. The waY r? of the difliculty which he believed was suggested by the Henry Campbell-Bannerman was that if they gave t Transvaal thoroughly representative Governmen.t thei might leave them to settle the difficulty. The rtTle magnates would fight as strongly against representatgee Government as they would against the abolition of slave system. His hearers must not think that when thei had returned a Liberal Government the struggle would be, over. Then the trial of strength would commence an". they would probably have to go on holding meetin and strengthening the Government. The Honese Lords still existed and the Archbishop of Canterb had declared Chinese labour to be a regrettable necessitYt' (" Shame.") When they had representative Governraea in the Transvaal there must be NO FANCY FRANCHISE or property qualifications. There must at least be In _ hood suffrage. (Applause.) If the dignity of labour was to be maintained in the Transvaal the people England would have to do it, not Tory workingulef, gs, some of whom were meeting at some street corner defend Chinese labour. (Laughter.) He believed 04' the men who loved freedom in this land would see th'' • freedom obtained in other lands. (Applause.) Mr. C. JACKSON HOLMES seconded and the vote Wi6s ' very heartily accorded. Acknowledging the vote of thanks Mr. Outhwait! ' said his work in this country had brought great hope" , to him and he believed England was going to make en,. of those great moves forward that she had made or; certain great epochs in tho past. (Applause). Th„hen. was going to be virtually a great wave of moral "erEr siasm sweeping over the country. The people " simply going to say " This is NOT GOOD ENOUGH FOR ENGLAND." He was confident that the great cry be was going to id "Remember South Africa " and the Government W°lll,_ be swept clean out of existence. (Appause.) 111e1,4r. lug a vote of thanks to the Rev. Harvey Cook, , ,is Outhwaito expressed particular pleasure that one or calling should occupy the chair. It was time that evet:'. section of the clergy of England should come o; out this 7matter—(Applause)—and those who did not c 11.1; out should be branded as hireling shepherds. gen „wans like the Archbishop of Canterbury defended this should be branded too, however high they rnighbe--. It was no new thing for the Church to defend slay r;, They had to fight the Church in the Southern StatesY:f , America when they were trying to overthrow slaryr there. He was glad to see a minister in the , because the question was a great moral issue. (rw pismire.) ne of Mr. E. OSBORNE seconded. He said he Was C) ce those who had not hesitated in the past to denoun certain ministers for their inaction in this matter:l he ; few days ago a preacher in one of their pulpits s:5l err-. could not account for the masses not going to oh He (Mr. Osborne) could account for it. It srtirch that the masses were missing the Churcb,but thof ill was missing the masses. He was satisfied that I; tO Warrington they could get their Christian , come forward and fight this moral battle there w?rch be no doubt as to the result of the election w1".,3 would put an end to this contemptible Government aP its disastrous work. (Applause.) The vote was heartily carried. The CHAIRMAN in responding said he was prou belong to the only section of the Church in Entt 00 whose ministers, he thought on the whole, stood 0 d 10 the South African war. (Hear, hear.) He bolongewere that order called Unitarian. Sometimes they ahoy . looked upon as leprous sort of individuals going tO making a sort of moral plague, but he was haPPY the count his ancestry amongst ',those who had suPtfirians great song of freedom. It was because Mit especially had had had to bear the brunt of being Pe,eni cuted and knew what it was to win their fr,erd. through seas of blood that they had compassion.to _wa focall men who were seeking freedom and their opportunity and best expression. (Applause.) dlotao</p>
