        <p>foolish and wild as it is base and improper." The Duke of Belsize frowned. No one."., continued the others t! could be more keenly interested in the fortunes of the Stuarts than I, as may be gathered from my book on 'The Fall of the House of Stuart.'" ' "That is why you received this invitation," mid the Duke. It singled you out from amongst men of influence, as one who had the subject at heart. In your final chapter you go so far as to express your opinion in favour of their rights." "That is speaking theoretically," inter- rupted the Cabinet Minister, "that is judging 'events of two hundred years ago and more, historically.' But I do not and never did ad- vocate absurdity or revolution. Why the King himself has laughingly taken me to task for what he termed my technical trea- son 1" "Right is right," said Lord, Swanage, sententiously, "however the great lapse of time. A wrong of old standing is still a wrong to be repaired:" "I am surprised and astonished," went on Lord Oddingly, taking no notice of the last remark, "to see such a gathering and to hear such sentiments in the twentieth century. Poly, what is the cause of this militant revival of Jacobitism ? " "The fact that owing to death the claim —the right, I should say—to the throne has devolved upon a young man of charac- ter and energy,' answered lan Frazer, who had been waiting for a chance of putting himself forward. "In this lies the favour- able opportunity, which has been absent for (ears.' "You refer, of course, to Prince Rudolph 3f Bavarienburg ? " said the Secretary for War. Frazer bowed assent. "And is he aware of your intentions?. Is he favourably disposed towards such a plot ? " For an instant a somewhat foolish look crossed the faces of the conspirators. The duke recovered himself almost at ence. "We have every reason to believe so," ke answered boldly. "We are at present in communication with him." "Then you consider your scheme is rapidly ripening ? " pursued Lord Oddingly, eyeing the company coolly with considerable curiosity. "Indeed, we do," answered the duke en- thusiastically. "Each week. brings us more favourable reports, especially from Scotland, and the Catholics in Ireland, and the numer- ous monasteries and brotherhoods, which are now so firmly established in England, who are all eager for the return of a Catholic sovereign. In addition, we have numerous supporters in many quarters, and you can see for yourself the social and political force of the Head Centre, from which the whole scheme is being organised." Lord Oddingly looked round the table with a grim contemptuous smile. ' " I see before me many people of wealth and position, if not brains,' he said bitterly, "who ought to know better than to crave for false excitement or self-advancement by the tortuous path of traitor's tricks. I even see two of my own colleagues in the Cabinet,—to say nothing of a past colleague and others I had looked upon as poSsible future administrators. I see others who are intimate in high places with those they seek to overthrow. I see a group of younger .nien of great promise led astray by foolish ambitions or the hope of excitement. What I see, I see with great regret." During this candid speech various ex- pressions played over the faces of his audience. Some, including the Duchess of Belsize, flushed with anger, and on the face of her husband was plainly written the deepest annoyance. Come, come, Lord Oddingly," he said, in a sharp, stac to voice, wnich betray6l ms feelings, you were not invited here to pass insulting criticisms upon our intelligence or our morals." "Then why was I asked ? " asked the Secretary for War with equal sharpness in his tone. "Because we, knowing from your history ydur strong Jacobite tendencies, wished to enrol you as one of us and to profit by your judgment and your influence." "Then you will be disappointed, I assure you," rejoined Lord Oddingly, breaking out into scornful anger. "I am not a traitor, and I have neither part nor lot with traitors. I can find no words to express my surprise, disgust and reprobation bf the whole thing, and I am ashamed to see those who were honourable and honoured men and women lending themselves to such a nefarious scheme. No, emphatically no I That is my final answer. I will have nothing to do with treason and treachery." Angry exclamations broke out on all sides, and the hot blood rushed into many faces. "You are a bold man, my lord," ex- claimed the Duke, angrily, "to speak sc frankly your unfavourable opinion, when you are in our hands. Were we what you declare, your life would scarcely be worth ' an hour's purchase: but we can afford to laugh at your helplessness. Were you to repeat your tale, were you to mention the scene of to-night, the whole country from the King downwards would laugh you out of public life. Our names are sufficient safe- guardif you were to be dishonourable enough to talk.; and your life would then pay the penalty. I throw back your own words ir your teeth; I give you back your own names. You know the right and will not do it. You deliberately, from convenience and caution, choose the wrong. Of such traitors and villains the cause has no use; and in due course they will reap their due re- ward. I can hardly believe that such can really be your final answer as an honourable man." Lord Oddingly's ur curled contemp- tuously. "It is," he replied a bruptly, looking the chairman full in the face. " And you, Lord Portcullis?" asked the Duke, turning to him. " I am entirely at one with Lord Odding- ly," replied the younger man, warmly. ' I cannot find words to express my surprise and disgust at the ,plan proposed .to us to- night. I must take, my stand by his side as I am in perfect harmony with him,, and I must beg you to excuse me too." While speaking Lord Portcullis simulated indignation in tone and gesture: and, rising from the table, he walked over to Lord Oddingly and took up his position beside him. " There is, I think, nothing more to 'be said," said Lord Oddingly, moving towards the door. "I am grieved," said lan Frazer, who ac- companied him to open it, "that my lack of tact should have drawn you into such an unpleasant position." The Secretary fdr War took no notice of As the trio reached the door, the Duke of Belsize spoke again. "We rely upon your discretion, my lord." Lord Oddingly turned at the door. " It will not be long before your Grace learns the result of the duel between my sense of responsibility and my discretion." Then he turned sharply upon his heel and left the room, followed by Lord Portcullis. The door was sharply locked behind them. Portcullis was the first to break the silence, as they put on their coats. " May I give you a lift in my car, Lord Oddingly? ' he said in a rather hoarse voice. " Thank you, thank you very much," responded the other. " I shall be very grateful. I came up with Mr. Frazer and have no conveyance of my own." The night outside was clear' and frosty, and the stars looked down on the Heath with pale, cold eyes. A shiver ran down the younger man's back as the keen air caught his face. "It is a cold night," he said, as he stepped into his car. Lord Oddingly took his seat beside him. and Portcullis started the silent engine. The car glided forward without a sound, and gassed through the great gates, which were</p>
