        <p>• Imperialism is shop-soiled. Even Mr. Pudyard * Kipling is holding a clearance sale of damaged ideas.— The "Star." * * * That unhappy joke about the chickens has recoiled. The audience in Parr Hall roared on Tuesday when • Mr. Howes said " the chickens are hatching all along the line and, moreover, they are all Radwals." * * * There must be a strain of rashness in the compos- ition of Alderman Smethurst'or he would not have offered £lOO to the Infirmary if anyone cold prove that he bad been guilty of improper trading. He might know that those persistent people, the Non- Traders will make a big effort to earn that "cool hundred," and they can't make the effort without causing Mr. Smethurst considerable worry. Rather unwise, Mr. Alderman! * * * What impressed one most powerfully about Mr. Orithwaite's addresses on Chinese labour was the care With which he proved his statements to the hilt. He Was able to give chapter and verse for everything he placed before his hearers and there is no doubt that be convinced the most sceptical. it has so often been said that white men are unable to do the work that we Would call our readers' special attention to his ex- posure of this fallacy. According to the official report of one of the mines, which Mr. Outhwaite read, White labourers did some of the most difficult and dangerous work underground and did it so well as to call forth the praise of their employers, while the same year dividends totalling 140 per cent. were declared. The Chinese are employed because they are, or have to be, satisfied with Is. or is. lid. per day and they have not developed any of those politi- cal notions which the mine owners are so afraid the British workman would bring to bear in the Transvaal.</p>
