        <p>STET] Uhl TONT U. ilryi RACKING HEADACHES, PAINS AFTER EATING, GEED AND KEPT CURED BY POUF/ e'CINCOrI: L9O eL.cit. rf „ rj "I had been almost a life-long sufferer from indigestion when Mother Seigel's Syrup and Pills restored me to health. "lii my ease the ailment took the form of violent pains across the body and a great load or pressure - on the chest. "I had often a sick bilious 'feel- ing, while the headaches I endurcil were past my power to describe. " I had no appetite and the pain after food made me afraid to eat. I also suffered much from consti- pation. " Nothing brought any real relief until I got Mother Seigel's Syrup. "I felt better after a few doses, and resolved to try the I'ills also. " Their action was unlike any other medicine I had ever taken— they were gentle yet effective and drove all impurities out of my system. " The Syrup and Pills soon res- tored me to sound health, id sines then, by taliing nitliCC3Siolllll (10-,e of your excellent riled:eines. I have continued in good health." Alice Oliver, l.r2ttenhain Road, Lloyd's Peak, Wa:t.lanistuw, April Nth, 1905. - Price iff &amp; 2/6 bottle.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES. _- A DEAR POMEGRANATE. 1 At Widnes on Monday, Thomas Brodigan, residing in Travis-street, was sent to goal for one month for steal- ing pomegranate, valued at a penny, from a stall in the Widnes market on Saturday night. There were Iseveral other convictions against him. GIFT TO WIDNES PUBLIC. The Rev. Douglas E. Morton vicar of Widnes, has just received a communication from Mr. and Mrs. F. H. IGossage, expressing their willingness to give a cheque Ifor £5OO to further the cost of providing a clock for Ithe tower in St. Paul's Church, Widnes. This is supply- ing a long-felt want, because up to the present time the town is without a public clock. Plans and designs have been submitted, and after these have been passed the work will proceed. Mr. T. S. Timmis, another of the church's benefactors, who has also taken a great interest lin the scheme, handed over to the vicar on Monday Ilmorning a cheque for £3OO. BA_BY FALLS INTO THE CANAL. On Monday at Runcorn, a young woman named Maria Evans, of Gilbert-street, was charged with being drunk while in charge of a baby. She pleaded guilty. Evidence was given by Joseph Bazley, a lock tenter on Bridgewater Canal, that on Saturday night the prisoner was on Waterloo Bridge carrying a baby in her arms. She was drunk, and dropped the baby over the bridge into the Bridgewater Canal. The woman screamed and cried out, " Oh, my baby's in the water." Witness and a young man named named Richard Siddall resoused the baby with a boathook. The defendant said she rested the child on the wall of the bridge in order to put its cape on, and it overbalanced. She seized its frock, but it was only pinned, and the baby went. In sentencing the defendant to a month's hard labour. Mr. Ookleston toll her he was getting off very easily. A PLAGUE OF RATS. A plague of rats has broken out at Aston Workhouse Infirmary. It was mentioned to the Guardians that the rats were a terror to the nurses, and ran over the beds of patients at night. The Infirmary Committee has received offers to exterminate the rats, one individual undertaking to accomplish the task for the sum of 15 guineas.</p>
